1 OCCU Merchant, mathematician, astronomer; member of the Royal Society
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Brattle
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Thomas Brattle''' (1657–1713) was a well-educated and prosperous Boston merchant who served as treasurer of Harvard College, and was a member of the intellectually elite Royal Society.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Brattle was born 5 September 1657. He attended and graduated from Harvard College in 1676, later becoming treasurer of the college. He became a member of the Ancient and Honorable Artillery Company of Massachusetts in 1672.
2 CONC  He was the principal founder of the Brattle Street Church in Boston, with Dr. Coleman as the first minister.
2 CONT
2 CONT In early October 1692 he wrote a letter to an English clergyman which was critical of the Salem witch trials. The letter was circulated widely in Boston at the time, and it continues to be studied for its reasoned attack on the wi
2 CONC tchcraft trials in Salem. Brattle presents a compelling argument against the legal premises and procedures involved in the afflictions, accusations, and executions, with a particular focus on the invalidity of spectral evidence in
2 CONC  proceedings. He concludes by saying "I am afraid that ages will not wear off that reproach and those stains which these things will leave behind them upon out land."
2 CONT
2 CONT Brattle died on 18 May 1713 at age 56.
2 CONT
2 CONT ******************************************************************
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://www.ebooksread.com/authors-eng/josiah-quincy/ the-history-of-harvard-university- volume-1-ala/1-the-history-of-harvard-university-volume-1-ala.shtml JOSIAH QUINCY -- HISTORY OF HARVARD]
2 CONT
2 CONT Among those entitled to grateful remembrance for services and bounties to Harvard College, the name of Thomas Brattle is conspicuous. He was a  merchant of Boston, distinguished for opulence, activity, and talent, and for the zeal
2 CONC  and readiness with which he devoted his time, wealth, and intellectual power to objects of private benevolence and public  usefulness.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born in 1657, was graduated at Harvard College in 1676, and died in 1713, at the age of  fifty-six. He was chosen Treasurer of the College in 1693, and held that office twenty years, until his death. In the July preceding t
2 CONC hat event, he caused the settlement he had made with his predecessor, in 1693, and his own account of receipts and expen- ditures during the intervening period, to be entered on the records of the Corporation ; carefully specify- 
2 CONC ing the particulars of the real and personal estate of the College, with the incomes of both ; thus giving the most authentic and satisfactory development of its property and pecuniary relations at that early period. Two years ela
2 CONC psed after his death before a successor was appointed. During the interval, his brother and executor, the Rev. William Brattle, at the solicitation of the Corporation, took upon himself the  superintendence of the finances of the 
2 CONC College.  By the accounts of these treasurers, it appears that  Thomas Brattle received, in 1693, from John Richards, his
2 CONT
2 CONT HISTORY OF HARVARD UNIVERSITY  411         CHAPTER XIX
2 CONT
2 CONT predecessor, upwards of one thousand five hundred  and fifty pounds, lawful money, personal estate of the  College, and a real estate yielding one hundred pounds annual income ; and that William Brattle, his  executor, delivered o
2 CONC ver to John White, his successor, in 1715, nearly three thousand eight hundred pounds, personal estate, and real estate, yielding two hundred  and eighty pounds. Such was the accumulation of the property of the College, while its 
2 CONC finances were under the management of these brothers.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From "An account of some of the descendants of Capt. Thomas Brattle" by Harris, Edward Doubleday, 1839-1919, comp Published 1867 https://archive.org/stream/accountofsomeofd1867harr/accountofsomeofd1867harr_djvu.txt
2 CONT
2 CONT " 21 (May 21st). The College Treasurer was inter' d at Boston  sub monumento Pati'is, matrisque, arique ex parte {tit aiunf)  certiori: senatu, Poftuloque Academico ftrceeunte feestru" 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was never married. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His will was presented for probate at Boston, May 23, 1713, and  is as follows : 
2 CONT
2 CONT "In the name of God, Amen — I, Thomas Brattle of Boston,  in y e County of Suffolk within Her Maj ts province of the Massa-  chusetts Bay in New England, Esq r ., being at this time in bodily  health and of a Sound disposing mind,
2 CONC  but not knowing how  Soon or Sudden my great Change may be, Do make this my last  Will & Testament in manner following, that is to say, I first &  principally Commit my Soul into y e hands of Almighty God my  Heavenly Father of w
2 CONC hose meer grace and mercy through y e  Merits of Jesus Christ my Saviour, I hope for y e free pardon of  all my Sins, & to Obtain Everlasting Blessedness. My body I  Commit to y e Earth to be decently Interred, & all my dear Rela-
2 CONC   tions and friends to y e care and blessing of Heaven. And as for such  Worldly goods as it has pleased God to bestow upon me who am  less than ye least of his mercies, I Will & Dispose thereof in such  manner as I judge may be m
2 CONC ost beneficial as follows — 
2 CONT
2 CONT " Imp", my funeral Charges and just debts being paid & dis-  charged w th all Convenient speed, I give and devise unto my  brother William Brattle & his heirs forever All those my Messuages  or Tenements in or near Brattle Street 
2 CONC in this towne : whereof one  is in y e present Tenure of Mr. Thomas Clarke, another in y e  present tenure of Mr. Richard Draper, & a third of the widows  Adkins & Lad, together with y e ps. of ground lying between y e  Messuages 
2 CONC of y e s d Draper & y e s d Adkins & Lad, on y e South  side of Brattle Street aforesaid. Provided allways, & it is my ex-  press Will & desire that there be paid annually on y e day of my  decease unto my Sister Elisabeth Oliver 
2 CONC Two third parts (only in  case my Sister Mary Mico shall become a Widow, then to each of  them One Third part) of y e clear Incomes of my s d Messuages,  and ps. of ground, during their Natural lives. 
2 CONT
2 CONT " Item, I give & bequeath unto my brother Edward Brattle Two  hundred pound, to my Sister Elisabeth Oliver Three hundred pound,  to my sister Katharine Winthrop Two hundred pound, & in case  she shall become a Widow again, One hun
2 CONC dred and fifty pound  more, & to my Sister Mary Mico Four hundred pound, & in case  she shall become a Widow, One hundred pound more. Item, I give  & bequeath unto each of my Nephews & Neeces, namely, Elisa-  beth Keeling, Nathan 
2 CONC 1 , James, Brattle, Peter, Sarah & Mary Oliver,  W r illiam & Mary Brattle (now Smith) & John, Katharine & Bethiah  Eyre, a ring of twenty shillings & Fifty pound in money, & to my  brother Joseph Parson Thirty pound. 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Item, I give unto his Excell cy Joseph Dudley, Esq r ., & to (my  Cousen Dudley) his Lady, unto my Cousen Jonathan Tinge, Esq r .  & his wife, & to my Worthy Kinsman Paul Dudley, Esq r . a ring  of twenty Shillings ; unto my Cous
2 CONC en Willard, Wid w of y e late  Rev d M r Sam 1 Willard dec d five pound, & to my good Kinsman  M r Josiah Willard five pound & a ring. 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Item, I give unto my Cousens Cutler & Long of Charlestown  fifty shillings apeece, & to y e Rev d M r Simon Bradstreet, Minister  there, five pound, & to each of my Cousens, namely Anna Smith, 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 
2 CONT
2 CONT 10 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Quinsey, Anna Holman, Shepard & Margarett Fiske, &  Mercy & Sarah Oliver a ring of twenty shillings. Item, I give  unto M r William & Elisabeth Whetcomb & M rs Hannah Davis  fourty shillings apeece, to M rs Margaret Davis als
2 CONC o fourty shillings  & to y e rest of her Sisters twenty Shillings apeece. 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Item, I give unto M r Thomas Clarke, M r John Kilby, M r Rich-  ard Draper, M rs Elisabeth Macarty & Henry Newman, Esq r a  ring of twenty shillings apeece. Item, I give and bequeath unto  Harvard College in Cambridge in New Engl
2 CONC and Two hundred  pound to be laid out in a purchase, or otherwise improv'd on very  good security toward y e maintenance of Some Master of Arts there  and Especially of such a one as is best skill' d in y e Mathematicks  & shall b
2 CONC y all proper methods endeavour the Improvement thereof,  as by reading and teaching y e same, & making Observations &  Communicating them to y e Learned abroad, as in some measure I  have done, respect & preference being evermore 
2 CONC given to such as  are or shall be akin by blood unto me above and before all others.  Item, I give unto y e Rever d President & each of the Fellows of  y e s d College a ring of Twenty shillings, & to the present steward  thereof 
2 CONC one of fourteen or fifteen. Item, I give & bequeath unto  the Rev d M r Benj n Colman the worthy Pastor of y e New Church  in Brattle Street a ring of twenty shillings & five pound in mony.  Item, I give, dedicate & Devote my Orga
2 CONC n to the praise & glory  of God in y e s d Church if they shall accept thereof, & within a  year after my decease procure a Sober person that can play skil-  fully thereon with a loud noise ; otherwise to y e Church of Eng-  land 
2 CONC in this towne on y e same terms & conditions, & on their Non  acceptance* or discontinuance to use it as aboves d unto y e College, 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * Elliot, Cara. "This House which I have built: The Foundation of the Brattle Street Church in Boston and Transformations in Colonial Congregationalism". Gettysburg College.edu. The Gettysburg Historical Journal.  [http://cupola.g
