2 NOTE From "An Account of Some of the Descendants of Capt. Thomas Brattle" by Edward Doubleday Harris (1867):
3 CONT
3 CONT "The history of the armorial device, a cut of which precedes this Account, we have been unable to obtain. Whether it was borne by Captain Thomas Brattle or not, is a matter open for conjecture. It was certainly used by at least tw
3 CONC o of his sons previous to the year 1713. It is described by Gore in his roll of armorial bearings, prepared in the early part of the last century, and possibly owes its existence to his fertile imagination, though we are inclined 
3 CONC to think that it is of more ancient derivation. An elaborate specimen of needle work, dated 1742, whereon is emblazoned the combined arms of the Brattle and Saltonstall families, gives for the Brattle crest the armored arm and bat
3 CONC tle axe, whilst the ancient seal of the Treasurer Brattle, now in the possession of J. P. Gannett, Esq., gives us only the profiled helmet. "
