1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Edward Brattle===
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: 18 Dec 1670, Boston MA
2 CONT Christened: 25 Dec 1670, Boston MA Died: 1719, Marblehead MA
2 CONT * son of Thomas Brattle (abt 1624-1683) Elizabeth Tyng (1638-1682)
2 CONT * Married Mary Legg of Marblehead, daughter of John Legg.  She survived him and married 2) Capt. Nathaniel Norden of Marblehead 3) Col. Edmond Goffe of Cambridge
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT * 37. Mary, who married, March 20, 171 2, James Smith, merchant of Boston, and probably died without issue before her father, as he does not name her or any child of hers in his will.
2 CONT * 38. Thomas, b. Sept. 17, 1695, bapt. at Old South Sept. 20, 1696, died young
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT {{//s3.amazonaws.com/photos.geni.com/p13/71/69/82/46/5344484292b1fd43/edward_brattle_large.jpg|frame|center}}
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===biography===
2 CONT
2 CONT From An account of some of the descendants of Capt. Thomas Brattle by Harris, Edward Doubleday, 1839-1919, comp Published 1867.  Page [https://archive.org/stream/accountofsomeofd1867harr#page/28/mode/2up 28-29]
2 CONT
2 CONT (1) 9. Edward Brattle, youngest son of Thomas and 
2 CONT Elizabeth (Tyng), married March 23, 1692-3, Mary Legg, of  Marblehead. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dec. 10, 1695, his brother Thomas conveyed to them (he then  "merchant of Boston"), that estate now directly in the rear of the  lot occupied by the Church on Brattle Street. It was then described  as being bounded in front S. W. 
2 CONC on highway from the Dock to  Brattle Street 60 ft., S. E. on land of Dr. Macarty 145 ft., N. E.  on land of John Wing 35 ft., and N. W. on Brattle Close 130 ft.  This land he conveyed Jan. 29, 1712, to his son-in-law Smith.  He wa
2 CONC s a captain of the militia, and member of the Ancient and  Honorable Artillery Company in 1694. The last years of his life  were passed at Marblehead, where he possessed considerable real  estate, and where he acquired a high repu
2 CONC tation as a successful  and enterprising merchant. His first recorded purchase of real  estate there, was July 28, 1716. Much of his landed property  there was sold by his widow after his death. He died at Marble-  head sometime b
2 CONC etween Feb. 5 and Oct. 22, 1719. His wife survived him, but the date of her death is unknown to me. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Their issue were : 
2 CONT
2 CONT 37. Mary, who married, March 20, 171 2, James Smith, merchant of Boston, and probably died without issue before her father, as he does not name her or any child of hers in his will. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 38. Thomas, b. Sept. 17, 1695, bapt. at Old South Sept. 20, 1696, died young. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===will===
2 CONT
2 CONT The will of Edward Brattle on record in Essex Probate is as follows : — 
2 CONT
2 CONT "In the Name of God, Amen. I, Edward Brattle of Marble- 
2 CONT head in New England, merchant, being Sick & not Knowing how  Soon It may please God to take me out of this world, yet all this  time thro' mercy of sound judgment & mind doe make this my  last Will and testament. I commend my Soul
2 CONC  to y e mercy of God  thro' Jesus Christ my Blessed Redeemer, my Body I committ to  ye Earth in hopes of a Glorious Resurrection, and as to the Estate  which it hath pleased God from his goodness to favor me with I  doe dispose of
2 CONC  in after this manner, first y* my just debts and  funeral charges be paid with all convenient speed. 
2 CONT
2 CONT " I give to my sisters Eliz a Oliver, Katharine Winthrop, & Mary  Mico ten pound to each of them to be pay d them by my Exe 8 att  my decease for y e putting themselves in mourning. I give to my  nephew William Brattle ten pounds 
2 CONC for a suite of mourning. I  give to my son-in-law Mr. James Smith twenty pounds for a Suit  of mourning. I will y* att my Death a true Inventory to be taken  of all my Estate Real & personall by three good men under oath,  and y* 
2 CONC all my interest in vessels, horses and land & house lots,  goods in my Shopp & ware house reserving what house hold  goods my wife has a mind to keep and land hereafter mentioned  to my nephew W m Brattle. I say that all Real be b
2 CONC y my Exec 3  sold for ye most they can gett and y* ye money with what moneys  I have by me att my Death be by my Exec 8 put out att interest on  good security as they shall think proper and ye yearly income  thereof as also such p
2 CONC art of my household goods as my wife 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3° 
2 CONT
2 CONT desires to Keep be allowed unto my Loving wife Mary Brattle  During her Natural Life, she giving Bond & Surety y* ye house-  hold goods she Keepe at her death shall be made over to such of  my Exec s as shall then be alive, or in 
2 CONC case of their death to my  heirs hereafter mentioned, and at the Death of my wife my will  is that my Executors dispose of my estate as followeth : — 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Item, I give unto my Son in Law Mr. James Smith fifty  pounds money then Currant with the Merchants. 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Item, I give to ye Rev d Mr. Edward Holyoke ten pounds.  Item, I give to ye Rev d Mr. Saml. Cheever five pounds. Item, I  give to ye Rev d Mr. John Barnard five pounds. Item, I give to  my neighbor Mr. Saml. Russell ten pounds. 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Item, I give to my Kinsman & particular friend Mr. Jacob  Wendell of Boston, merch* one of my executors one pound. In  case of his death to his children by my cousin Sarah Wendell* his  wife. 
2 CONT
2 CONT " Item, I give to my nephew William Brattle & his heirs forever  all my interest in lands att Kennebeck, Quaboag & in ye Nara-  gansetts, & one hundred pounds in Money. Item, I will y 4 ye  whole remaining part of my estate wherev
2 CONC er it may be found be  divided into three equal parts or shares, andy* my sister Eliz a Oliver  have one share or part thereof, and in case she be dead, said share  to be equally divided among her Surviving children — and that my 
2 CONC  sister Katharine Winthrop shall have another share or part, and  in case she be dead her said share or part to be equally divided  between her three children. That my sister Mary Mico shall have  ye other share or part & in case 
2 CONC she be dead at y 4 time I give and  will y* said share or part to my dear Kinswoman Eliz a Keeling,  the interest y e of during her naturall life, & att my death I will  y 1 ye principall be divided among ye children lawfully bego
2 CONC tten of  her Body. Having thus disposed of my estate agreeable to my  own mind the executive power is what remains to be taken care 
2 CONT
2 CONT * She was daughter of Dr. James Oliver, of Cambridge. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 1 
2 CONT
2 CONT of, and with respect thereto I have thought fitt with myself &  accordingly doe now constitute & appoint my Loving wife Mary  Brattle & my trusty friends Mr. Jacob Wendell merchant, & Mr.  Sam Russell of Marblehead Executors of th
2 CONC is my last will &  Testament, thereby revoking & annulling all former Wills by me  made & Executed. In witness to which & every forementioned in  this sheet of paper, I do by these presents set to my hand & seal  the fifth day of 
2 CONC february ye year of our Lord one thousand seven  hundred eighteen and nineteen, and in the fifty year of ye Reign  of our Sovereign Lord King George &c. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward Brattle." 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From Lieutenant Joshua Hewes: A New England Pioneer, and Some of His ..., Volume 1 By Eben Putnam [https://books.google.com/books?id=8W4-AQAAMAAJ&lpg=PA555&ots=2v4qzpZqek&dq=Samuel%20Norden%20joanna%20copp&pg=PA555#v=onepage&q=Sam
2 CONC uel%20Norden%20joanna%20copp&f=false page 555] "He [Capt. Nathaniel Norden] was married to JANE LATTIMER, a daughter of Christopher Lattimer of Marblehead, prior to 1686. She died 20-2-1722, and he married again 20 Sept., 1722, MA
2 CONC RY LEGO, then widow of Capt. Edward Brattle, and who after his death married, 24 July, 1728, Col. Edmund Goffe of Cambridge."
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
