1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Son of Simon Eyre and his second wife, Martha (Hubbard) Whittingham
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://www.greatmigration.org/sample_sketch_simon_eyre.html
2 CONT
2 CONT >JOHN EYRE  b. 19 February 1653[/4?] [sic] [BVR 41], bp. Boston 22 January 1653/4 [BChR 326]; m. 20 May 1680 Catharine Brattle, daughter of Thomas Brattle (this marriage date is given by Savage and by many secondary sources, but h
2 CONC as not been found among published vital records; eldest known child b. Boston 27 March 1682 [BVR 157]; on 13 March 1683/4, Nathanel Oliver, John Eyre & Joseph Parson in right of their wives (three of said children) participated in
2 CONC  the distribution of the estate of Capt. Thomas Brattle late of Boston deceased [SPR NS 2:392-95]).
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From [https://books.google.com/books?id=b4k-AAAAYAAJ&lpg=PA336&ots=NPDx_0D_zq&dq=simeon%20eyre%20plymouth%20colony&pg=PA336#v=onepage&q=simeon%20eyre%20plymouth%20colony&f=false page 336] of Hudson-Mohawk Genealogical and Family M
2 CONC emoirs: A Record of ..., Volume 1 edited by Cuyler Reynolds
2 CONT
2 CONT >Dr. Oliver Noyes married (first), 1702, Ann, daughter of Governor Belcher; (second) February 6, 1718, Mrs. Katherine (Eyre, Eire, Eyers) Jeffries, born July 20, 1694, died May 6, 1760, daughter of John and Catherine (Brattle) Eyr
2 CONC e, who were married May 20, 1680, and had children: Katherine, Bertha and John Eyre. Dr. Oliver Noyes died March 16, 1721.
2 CONT
2 CONT >John Eyre was the youngest son of Simon Eyre, a surgeon of Watertown, Massachusetts, who came to America in the ship "Increase," from London, embarking April 15, 1635, with wife Dorothy, aged thirty-eight, and children: Mary, age
2 CONC d fifteen; Thomas thirteen; Simon, eleven; Rebecca, nine; Christian, seven; Ann, five; Benjamin, three; Sarah, three months. John, his youngest son, was born in Massachusetts, probably at Watertown. Simon was representative, selec
2 CONC tman and clerk of the town.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------
