1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =David Linnell=
2 CONT * BIRTH 1627 London, City of London, Greater London, England
2 CONT * DEATH 14 Nov 1688 (aged 60–61) Barnstable, Barnstable County, Massachusetts, USA
2 CONT * BURIAL Lothrop Hill Cemetery, Barnstable, Barnstable County, Massachusetts, USA 
2 CONT * MEMORIAL ID 176106283  & 139191015 David Linnel, Note by Patsy (Mann) Holtz, Bio by: Ken Kelly
2 CONT
2 CONT David was 25/26 years old when he married Hannah, a lass of 16 years. Otis notes that "our ancestors encouraged early marriages. He who married at eighteen was admitted to all the privileges, and required to perform all the duties
2 CONC  of a citizen; while, he that remained single, had to tarry till he was twenty-four to be enrolled as a townsman" (Otis, Gen.Notes Vol.2, p.154) While David had been listed as able to bear arms for ten years, he had waited to marr
2 CONC y for some time. The romance between David and Hannah had blossomed; it had received the public censure of public whipping and exclusion from the church rolls and privileges; yet they had moved on into marriage. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hannah was the daughter of Robert and Judith (Garnet) Shelley. Otis describes Robert as "an easy, good-natured man, who cared little how the world moved. He was, however, an honest man, a good neighbor, and a sincere Christian. Hi
2 CONC s wife Judith Garnet was before her marriage a Boston woman-- a member of the church there, proud, tenacious of her own opinions, and had little control over her tongue, which ran like a whip-saw, cutting everything it came in con
2 CONC tact with" (Otis, Gen.Notes, Vol.2, p.156). Judith's tongue got her into problems with the women of the community, and an excommunication by the church on June 4,1649 for speaking ill of other women. It was in this environment tha
2 CONC t Hannah grew up and out of which she married David. Clearly, she was the independent daughter of an independent mother. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Little mention is made of David's mature life. He was able to bear arms in 1643  and "hayward of the General field" in 1679 [2] but seems to have held no other function for the general good of the community. It seems probable that
2 CONC  his estate was very small at the time of his death. 
2 CONT
2 CONT David's will is dated 17 Nov. 1688 and was proved 6 March 1689. To his sons Samuel and Elisha, and to his daughters Hannah Davis, Experience, Susanna, Abigail and Mary, he gave one shilling. The land was to be divided in thirds am
2 CONC ong Jonathan, John, and David's wife Hannah. She was to have the lower room in "my now dwelling house during her widowhood and all the Rest of my Estate in what kind so ever it be to be at her own dispose. She to be sole executrix
2 CONC ." Jonathan was to have the house and to pay his brother John half of its worth. It is probable that Hannah continued to live in Barnstable until her death in 1708 but that Jonathan had already moved to Eastham as early as 1687. H
2 CONC ow early John moved to Hyannis Port is not known but his marriage to Ruth Davis in 1695 may indicate the time of establishing residence there. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hannah's will, dated 2 Feb.1708/09, probated 5 Apr.1709, mentions as beneficiaries Abigail Linnel; Mary Sergeant [Sargent], wife of John; Experience, wife of Jabez Davis; Susanna, wife of Eben. Phinney; and her grandaughter Hannah
2 CONC  Davis; daughter of Dolar. She signed with a mark and appointed John Phinney, Jr. as executor. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Notes==
2 CONT In David Linnel's will [3] he signed his name as David Linnel (one L). David also states the name of each of his children. Each one spelt Linnel. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Parents'''
2 CONT Robert Linnell 1584–1662
2 CONT Peninnah Howes Linnell 1596–1679
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Spouse'''
2 CONT Hannah Shelley Linnell 1637–1709
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Siblings'''
2 CONT Abigail Linnell Lombard 1633–1689
2 CONT Bethia Linnell Hopkins 1640–1726
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children''' all born in Barnstable
2 CONT Mary Linnel Sargeant 1660–1754
2 CONT Hannah Linnell Davis 1660 – unknown
