2 CONT
2 CONT Hannah Davis formerly Linnell aka Lynnell [
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 17 Apr 1625 in London, Middlessex, England
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughter of Robert Linnell and Jemimah Howse
2 CONT
2 CONT Sister of Sarah Linnell, David Linnell, Abigail (Linnell) Lombard, Mary (Linnell) Childs, Shubael Linnell [half], Samuel Linnell and	Bethiah (Linnell) Atkins
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife of John Davis — married 15 Mar 1648 in Barnstable, Plymouth Colony, New England
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother of John Davis Jr, Samuel Davis, Hanna (Davis) Jones, Mary (Davis) Hinckley, Benjamin Davis, Joseph Davis, Simon Davis, Dollar Davis, Jabez Davis, Mercy Davis, Timothy Davis and Ruth (Davis) Linnell
2 CONT
2 CONT Died about 1701 in Barnstable, Province of Massachusetts Bay
2 CONT
2 CONT Profile managers: Chris Hoyt, Puritan Great Migration Project WikiTree, Loren Fay, Gerald Smith, Wiley Walters , and Elizabeth Craig 
2 CONT
2 CONT Profile last modified 13 Jan 2018 | Created 7 Feb 2011 s. The Puritan Great Migration. Hannah (Linnell) Davis migrated to New England during the Puritan Great Migration (1620-1640).
2 CONT
2 CONT [Note: Disputed mother: From various court records, Peninnah House/Howse appeared to take oath with her brother Samuel Howse. In 1643 she is Peninnah Lynell in the will of her brother. Therefore she was the second wife of Robert L
2 CONC ynnell, marrying about 1638. See: TAG: Vol. 52: 1975: pp. 35-6 and NEHGS: Vol. 69: 1915: p. 284]
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Hannah Lynnell who married John Davis, son of Dolor, March 15, 1625, was a daughter of Robert Lynnell who came in 1638 first to Scituate; removing to Barnstable the following year. He was born about 1584 and his will was proved Fe
2 CONC b 22, 1662-3. (p. 21)[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Robert Linnel, in his will dated 23d January 1662-3, gives to his wife Jemimah (Penniah) Linnel, the use and improvement of his house mid homelot so long as she remains a widow and his furniture, a plow a cart, two cows and a 
2 CONC calf forever. (p. 152)
2 CONT
2 CONT To John Davis, who married his daughter Hannah, his two oxen, on condition that be provided his wife with wood, plowed her grounds, and mowed her meadow two years, if she remained a widow so long, if not then to be free.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Linnel only names three of his children, David, Abigail, and Bethia. He names John Davis, who married his daughter Hannah, then living, but does not call her by name. (p. 153([2]
2 CONT
2 CONT John Davis, son of Dolor and Margery, b. in England about 1626, m. March 15, 1648-9, Hannah, dau, of Robert Lynnell of Barnstable. Davis lived in Barnstable, and died there in 1703;
2 CONT
2 CONT His will is dated May 10, 1701, and speaks of his wife as living, also of sons John, Benjamin, Samuel, Dollar, Timothy, and Jabez, of daughters Mercy, Ruth Lynnell, and Mary Hinckley ; also of Hannah Jones's five children, son Joh
2 CONC n's four eldest sons, granddaughter Mary Goodspeed, and grandson Joseph Davis. Will proved April 9, 1703. Amount of inventory, £268 12s. 4:d. (p. 33)
2 CONT
2 CONT Children: (p. 33-4)
2 CONT
2 CONT John b. January 15, 1649-50; lived in Barnstable; rem. 1710 to Falmouth, d. there 1729, leaving a handsome estate; 12 children. He m. 1st wife February 2, 1674, Ruth Goodspeed; m. 2nd wife February 22, 1692, Mary Hamlin; she d. No
2 CONC vember, 1698; m. 3rd wife 1699, Hannah, widow of Nathaniel Bacon. (p. 34) Samuel, b. December 15, 1651, lived in Barnstable; d. 1711; unmarried. (p. 34) Hannah (twin), b. January 3, 1653-4; m. March 18, 1681, Jedediah Jones; lived
2 CONC  in Barnstable; 5 children perhaps more; she d. probably before 1701, certainly before 1718. Mary (twin), b. January 3, 1653-4; m. 1st, 1676 or 1677, Benjamin Goodspeed; 1 child; m. 2nd, November 24, 1697, John Hincley; she d. bet
2 CONC ween 1711 and 1718. Joseph (twin) b. June, 1656; m. March 28, 1682, Mary, dau. of James Claghorn; lived at Chequaquet. He d. about 1690; she d. 1706; 4 children. Benjamin (twin) b. June, 1656; d. 1718; unmarried. Simon, b. July 15
2 CONC , 1658; d. young. Doller, b. October 1, 1660; m. August 3, 1681, Hannah, dau. David Lynnell; lived in Barnstable; rem. to "South Sea"; will dated March 2, 1719, mentions 8 children but no wife. Jabez b. ___; m. August 20, 1689, Ex
2 CONC perience, dau. David Lynnell; 10 children; he d. 1710. Mercy b. __; d. unmarried 1733, aged about 70. Timothy b. ___; m. March 7, 1690, Sarah, dau. of Edward Perry; he was living in 1718; 6 children; lived in Rochester, Mass. Ruth
2 CONC , b. 1674; m. 1695, John Lynnell; lived in Hyannis; 7 children; she d. May 8, 1748.[3] The Will of Thomas Howse of the parish of St. Stephen in Coleman street London, a Citizen and Brownbaker of London 18 October 1643 includes: To
2 CONC  my sister Pininna Lynnell. (p. 260)
2 CONT
2 CONT The testator whose will is given above is clearly Thomas Howse, son of the Rev. John Howse, who was rector of Eastwell Co., Kent and brother of Samuel Howse of Scituate, Mass. This testator is the one referred to in the Aspinwall 
2 CONC Notarial Records pp. 10 224 5 although he is there called Watchmaker instead of Brownbaker as in the will. The sister Pininna Lynnell was the wife of Robert Linnell of Scituate and Barnstable, Mass., immigrant ancestor of the Linn
2 CONC ells of New England. Rev. John Lothrop in his Scituate Church Records calls Linnell my brother, because the latter's wife and Lothrop's first wife were sisters. (p. 261)[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Source notes
2 CONT
2 CONT Barnstable Marriages [p. 409]
2 CONT
2 CONT John Davis & Hannah Lynnel Married 15 March 1648. Barnstable Births [p. 409] John Davis & Hannah Lynnel
2 CONT
2 CONT His son John born About ye Midst of January 1649 Samuel Midst of Decembr 1651 Twins Hannah & Mary 3 January 1653 Twins Joseph & Benjamin June 1656 Simon Midst July 1658 Doler beginning Octor 1660 Jabez (Vol.4:p. 223)[5] [NOTES: LI
2 CONC NNELL:LYNNELL Robert b. London Eng 1584; settled at Scituate, Mass; 1638 removed to Barnstable Mass 1639. CH] [6]
2 CONT
