1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Descendant of Mayflower passenger Thomas Rogers
2 CONT ----
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Phinney-86
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Davis formerly Phinney
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 11 Apr 1690 in Barnstable, Barnstable, Province of Massachusetts Bay
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughter of John Phinney and Sarah (Lumbart) Phinney
2 CONT
2 CONT Sister of Mary Phinney, John Phinney, Thomas Phinney, Hannah Phinney, Sarah (Phinney) Adams, Patience (Phinney) Coleman, Martha (Phinney) Lumbert and	Jabez Phinney
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife of Nathan Davis — married 25 Nov 1714 (to 17 Sep 1785) in Barnstable, Barnstable, Province of Massachusetts Bay
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother of Jabez Davis, Sarah Davis, Elizabeth (Davis) Butterfield, Isaac Davis, Sarah (Davis) Hopkins and Nathan Davis
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 30 Dec 1786 in Harwinton, Litchfield, Connecticut, United States
2 CONT
2 CONT Profile last modified 29 Aug 2019 | Created 5 Jul 2011
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth (Phinney) Davis was related to a passenger on the Mayflower.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Phinney, daughter of John and Sarah (Lumbart) Phinney, was born on 11 April 1690 in Barnstable, Barnstable, Province of Massachusetts Bay.[1][2] She was baptized in the Barnstable church on 10 May 1691.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT She married Nathan Davis, son of Jabez and Experience (Linnell) Davis,[4] on 25 November 1714 in Barnstable. [5] Their first four children were recorded in Barnstable by 1720.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Mayflower Society there is no absolute proof that Nathan and Elizabeth of Barnstable are the same Nathan and Elizabeth who had a child Nathan in Harwinton (1635) and were buried there in 1785 and 1786 with ages re
2 CONC ported consistant with the known births of the Barnstable couple.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT There is documentation for Nathan in Barnstable until 1726, but not after that date.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT The will of Nathan of Harwinton, dated 8 Dec 1783, names his wife Elizabeth, son John, son Nathan (executor), daughters Sarah Hopkins and Martha Preston and grandsons Nathan and Josiah (no surnames).[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Nathan did not name Jabez, Elizabeth, or Isaac, his known children in his will. Jabez and Isaac died before his will and Elizabeth, who may be the Elizabeth who married Jonathan Butler in Harwinton, had children, but her death is 
2 CONC unrecorded. Of the children mentioned in the will: Events in Harwinton, Nathan has a birth record in 1635; a John Davis married Ruth Davis 1748; Sarah Davis married Hezekiah Hopkins 1742; a Martha Davis married (1) 1755 Amos Catli
2 CONC ng and (2) 1771 Ebenezer Preston (m. in Winchester, Connecticut). Estimated births of these three children fit in to the gap between Isaac and Nathan.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Nathan, of Harwinton, died 17 September 1785 aged 96 years and was buried in the Old Cemetery in Harwinton, Litchfield, Connecticut.[8][9][10] Elizabeth died on 30 December 1786,[2][11] or 30 June [or July] 1786,[12] in her 97th y
2 CONC ear, and was buried next to Nathan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Jabez, b. 7 Oct 1715 Barnstable[5] Sarah, b. 12 Aug 1717, d. 23 Aug 1717, Barnstable[5] Elizabeth, b. 15 Sep 1718 Barnstable[5] Isaac, b. 1 Jan 1720 Barnstable[5] Probably Sarah Davis b. probably at Barnstable about 1722 based on 
2 CONC age 68 at 1790; m. Hezekiah Hopkins 3 June 1742, Harwinton. Probably John Davis, probably b. at Barnstable 1723-1727, based on 1 Dec 1748 marriage to Ruth Lewis in Farmington Probably Martha Davis b. probably at Harwinton 1730-173
2 CONC 4 based on 10 Dec 1755 m. to Amos Catling. Probably Nathan, b. Harwinton CT 10 Aug 1735 Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT ↑ "Massachusetts, Town Clerk, Vital and Town Records, 1626-2001," database with images, FamilySearch : 13 July 2016, Barnstable > Barnstable > Town records 1640-1774 vol 1-2 > image 247 of 663; town clerk offices, Massachusetts. B
2 CONC arnstable Town Records, Vol. 1:378. John Phinney & Sarah Lumbart married 30 May 1689 Their Daughter Elizabeth born 11 of April 1690 ↑ 2.0 2.1 Ann Reeves and Alice W.A. Westgate, Mayflower Families Through 5 Generations, Thomas Rog
