1 NOTE {geni:about_me} MORGAN GENEALOGY: A History of James Morgan, of New London, Conn., and His Descendants; from 1607 to 1869, By Nathaniel H. Morgan, Hartford, Press of Case, Lockwood & Brainard, 1869, p. 30
2 CONT ----
2 CONT *'''Morgan genealogy : A history of James Morgan, of New London, Conn., and his descendants; from 1607 to 1869 ... With an appendix containing the history of his brother, Miles Morgan, ....
2 CONT *https://archive.org/details/morgangenealogyh00morg
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/morgangenealogyh00morg#page/17/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.17
2 CONT *   JAMES MORGAN, the common ancestor of a numerous family now scattered widely over nearly or quite, every state and territory of the United States, was born in Wales, in 1607, but in what precise locality our honest progenitor f
2 CONC irst saw the light is uncertain, though probably in Llandaff, Glamorgan Co. The family appears to have removed from Llandaff to Bristol, Eng. on the opposite side of Bristol Channel, a short time at least, perhaps a few years, pri
2 CONC or to 1636. The name of his father is unknown, but there is some traditionary evidence that it was William.* 
2 CONT *   That year, 1636, in the month of March, he and two younger brothers, John and Miles, sailed from Bristol and arrived at Boston, Mass. in April following. 
2 CONT *   JOHN MORGAN, his next younger brother, who from tradition appears to have been a high churchman and to have exceedingly disliked the austerity of the Puritans, left Boston in disgust for more congenial society in Virginia, soo
2 CONC n after their arrival. How far the Morgans of Virginia are descended from him I am unable to say. 
2 CONT *   MILES MORGAN, the youngest brother, born in 1615, on his arrival at Boston, or soon after, joined a party of emigrants, mostly from Roxbury, of whom Col. Wm Pyncheon was at the head, and founded the settlement of Springfield, 
2 CONC Mass. 
2 CONT **	* See William, No. 46.
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/morgangenealogyh00morg#page/18/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.18
2 CONT *   He is said to have been under 21 years of age at the commencement of this settlement, and to have suppressed the fact of his minority in order to share in the drawing for house lots, which minors were not privileged to do. It 
2 CONC is certain that he drew a house lot and afterwards built upon it ; and it was the homestead of himself during his life, and of his descendants for many years after. It was situated upon the south side of "Ferry Lane," and in 1845 
2 CONC was sold by the Brewer family to the Conn. River Railroad Co. ; their tracks now covering the original lot, and their repair shop standing upon the site of the old Morgan homestead. 
2 CONT *   He married, about 1643, Prudence Gilbert, of Beverly, Mass., who was a fellow passenger with him in the voyage from England. Of this courtship and marriage, an interesting and curious account is preserved. He had 8 children by
2 CONC  this marriage, 4 sons and 4 daughters ; and his wife, Prudence, dying 14 Nov. 1660, he next married Elizabeth Bliss, of Springfield, 15 Feb. 1670, dau. of Thomas, by whom he had 1 son only. His children by Prudence were, Mary, b.
2 CONC  14 Dec. 1644; Jonathan, 16 Sept. 1646; David, 23 July, 1648; Pelatiah, 17 May, 1650; Isaac, 17 March, 1652; Lydia, 8 Feb. 1654; Hannah, 11 Feb. 1656; Mercy, 18 May, 1658 ; and by 2d wife, Nathaniel, 14 June, 1671. 
2 CONT *   This family of Miles Morgan* has numerous ....
2 CONT **	* See Appendix.
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/morgangenealogyh00morg#page/19/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.19
2 CONT *   JAMES MORGAN, the elder brother, and our lineal ancestor, may have settled first at Plymouth ....
2 CONT *   Wherever he settled at first, he is found in Roxbury, near Boston, before 1640. That year, Aug. 6, 1640, he married there, Margery Hill, of Roxbury. His eldest daughter, Hannah, was born there 18 May, 1642, and all his 5 other
2 CONC  children, except perhaps the youngest, who d. in infancy, were also probably born there. He was made a freeman there 10 May, 1643. He is named as a resident there in the inventory of John Graves, 1646, and was a freeholder there 
2 CONC as late as 1650, the same year that he removed to Pequot, (now N. London,) and had a houselot assigned him there. 
2 CONT *   It has been heretofore supposed, by myself as well as others, that James Morgan was one of the party of emigrants called the "Cape Ann Company," who came ....
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/morgangenealogyh00morg#page/22/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.22
2 CONT *1. JAMES, born in Wales, 1607, m. Margery Hill, of Roxbury, Mass. 6 Aug. 1640, died 1685, age 78. He was settled in Roxbury at first, and all his children except the youngest dau. were probably born there. 
2 CONT *CHILDREN. ....
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/morgangenealogyh00morg#page/23/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.23
2 CONT *  Capt. JAMES, (3) b. 3 Mch, 1644, s. of James, m. "Mary Vine of Old England," Nov. 1666, died 8 Dec. 1711, age 68. His wife Mary died in 1689, of the ''throat distemper," so called, a terrible epidemic which prevailed throughout
2 CONC  the country that year, especially in the months of July and August, visiting nearly every family and carrying consternation and death in its trail. She was born in England in 1641, and was 48 years old when she died. After her de
2 CONC ath he married 2d wife Hannah --- , born in 1640, who d. in 1711, aged 71, a few days only before his own death, but after the date of his will, in which he calls her his "dear and loving wife." ....
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/morgangenealogyh00morg#page/24/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.24
2 CONT *		CHILDREN, ALL BY MARY VINE. 
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/morgangenealogyh00morg#page/30/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.30
2 CONT *  Deacon WILLIAM, (9) b. 4 March, 1669, s. of James 2d, m. Margaret Avery, dau. of Capt. James, of Groton, 17 July, 1696. Both were members of the church at New London before the church at Groton, then New London, was founded, an
2 CONC d their four elder children were baptized there by the Rev. Gurdon Saltonstall, afterwards governor of Connecticut. When the first church was established in Groton, 8 Nov. 1704, he was chosen one of the deacons, and the births of 
2 CONC his later children are recorded there on the first records of the town. He died 25 Dec. 1750, aged 82, and his widow, Margaret, died in 1755, and her estate was settled that year. 
2 CONT *CHILDREN. 
2 CONT **	101. William, 7 April, 1697, m. Hannah Stanton, 21 Sept. 1721. 
2 CONT **'''	102. Margaret, 10 Sept. 1698, m. Samuel Davis, 29 Jan. 1719. 
2 CONT **	103. Deborah, 14 July, 1700. 
2 CONT **	104. Hannah, 1702. 
2 CONT **	105. Jerusha. 14 Jan. 1704, m. Humphrey Avery, 5 Feb. 1724. 
2 CONT **	106. Joseph, 10 Aug. 1706, m. Dorothy Avery, 4 Dec. 1735. 
2 CONT **	107. Solomon, 5 Oct. 1708, m. Mary Walworth, 1 July, 1742. 
2 CONT **	108. Elizabeth, 10 July, 1710, m. John Avery, jun. 
2 CONT **	109. Mary, 5 Jan. 1714, m. Jonas Belton, 7 Aug. 1737. 
2 CONT *'''  MARGARET, (102) m. Samuel Davis, of Groton, 29 Jan. 1719, and had 4 children, viz. — 110 Samuel Davis, born 2 Nov. 1719, d. 17 Feb. 1720 ; — 111 Hannah Davis, 15 Feb. 1721, died 17 May, 1721; — 112 Margaret Davis, 4 Aug. 172
2 CONC 2, died 26 May, 1723 ;— 113 Lucy Davis, 15 June, 1724, m. Ebenezer Avery, of Preston, had 4 daughters, and died 1756. Margaret, wife of Samuel, died 15 Aug. 1724, and he m. 2d, Sarah, widow of John Gardner, of Gardner's Island, an
2 CONC d dau. of Gov. Saltonstall ; had 2 children by her, and died soon after.  ______________________________
2 CONT *(ISSUES WITH DATES OF WILLIAM MORGAN & HIS CHILDREN)
2 CONT *'''A history of the family of Morgan, from the year 1089 to present times ([1902?])
2 CONT *https://archive.org/details/historyoffamilyo00morg
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/historyoffamilyo00morg#page/100/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.100
2 CONT **		 MORGAN OF CILFYNYDD.
2 CONT **		(A Branch of Tredegar.)
2 CONT *  I. William Morgan, of Llanvabon, born 1571 (his will describes him as late of Eglwysilian). He bore arms, CILFYNYDD, (see page 11). Issue: 1. Evan. 2. 
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/historyoffamilyo00morg#page/101/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.101
2 CONT *Thomas. 3. John. 4 James. 5. Watkin. 6. Johnathan. 7. William. Of the above, Evan married Mary Friswyth, and had six children. THOMAS MORGAN, of Bedwas, County Monmouth, after of Eglwysilan, and then of Monmouth, and so described
2 CONC  in the will of Janet Morgan, in 1779. He sold Bredwenarth to his brother Watkin, while the elder branch retained Cilfynydd, and the younger settled at Llandough. He married Mary, named in a post-nuptial settlement in 1716, and di
2 CONC ed 27th March in 1761. They had : 1. Thomas Morgan. 2. John. 3. Mary. 4. Margaret. JOHN MORGAN, called of Cowbridge in his sister's will died in 1775. He married Cecil Williams, of the Beach, Llysworney. They had : 1. William. 2. 
2 CONC Watkin, died 20 October, 1793. 3. Jonathan. 4. Ann, who inherited Bredwenarth under her aunt's will. (Died 9th July, 1822, aged 77. Buried at Llandough. She married John Basset, of Bonvileston and had Thomas Basset.) 5. Mary Morga
2 CONC n. JONATHAN MORGAN, D. D., rector of Hedley, County Surrey. Presented 29th Nov., 1791, resigned 1818; married Elizabeth, eldest daughter of Richard Dunford, of Woodmanstone, County Surrey, and had : 1. William. 2. Ann Morgan marri
2 CONC ed her cousin Thomas, son of John Basset and Ann Morgan, who was of Bonvileston and Bredwenarth. Issue: Richard Basset, of Bonvileston, and others. WILLIAM MORGAN, M. D. , M. A., was fellow of Magdalen College, Oxford. SIR THOMAS 
2 CONC CHARLES, son of Thomas, of Basset, was born in London, 1783; knighted, 1814; died London, 1843 He married, 1812, Miss Sydney Owenson, who, as Lady Morgan, earned in her day a distinction in literature which is still recorded in ca
2 CONC talogues of English letters. She wrote poems, novels, biography, and an autobiography. But her reputation was won by her political novel, "The Wild Irish Girl," the title of which became her own soubriquet. In 1817. 
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/historyoffamilyo00morg#page/102/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.102
2 CONT *and 1821 she essayed historical writing, and succeeded in creating genuine sensations by her "France Under the Bourbons" and "Italy," which appeared respectively in the last-named years. . . 
2 CONT **	LINE of JAMES MORGAN, OF CONNECTICUT. 
2 CONT *  I. JAMES MORGAN ( 4 of I above), sailed from Bristol, in the ship Mary, with a kinsman, ROBERT MORGAN (see line of ROBERT, post.), in the summer of the year 1636, and landed in Boston, Massachusetts Bay. Settled first at Sandy 
2 CONC Bay, near Gloucester, on Cape Ann, but found the coast bleak and the Indians troublesome. He married in Roxbury, where his first child was born, May 18, 1642. Finding fertile and desirable plantations at the mouth of the river Tha
2 CONC mes, at New London, in Connecticut, he, with the Sandy Bay colony, headed by its pastor. Rev. Richard Bliman, removed and settled there in 1649. Here he was assigned, February 10, 1650, lands granted him, "on the path to New stree
2 CONC t, being six acres of upland where the wipwams are, in the path that goes from his house toward Culvers, among the Rocky Hills," (New street is now Ashcroft street, in the city of New London, but it was called "Cape Ann street," i
2 CONC n honor of the Cape Ann colony, for more than a century). In 1661 he was one of a committee "to layout the bounds of N. London on the east side of the Great River." In 1662 it is recorded that "James Morgan, Mr. Tinker and Obiadia
2 CONC h Brown are chosen to seat the people in the meeting house, which they doing the inhabitants are to rest silent." In 1662 he is appointed one of a committee to contract to build a house for the ministry at New London, and signs hi
2 CONC mself "James Morgan, Senior, of New Lon- 
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/historyoffamilyo00morg#page/105/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.105
2 CONT *don." In this year, 1662, he stands third highest in amount among the taxpayers, he certifying to a holding of £250. He died in his homestead, about three miles from Groton. (occupied as late as 1869 by Elijah S. Morgan, a descen
2 CONC dant), on the road to Poquonoc Bridge, about three miles from Groton, in 1685, aged seventy-eight years. He married Margery Hill, of Roxbury, August 6, 1640. Issue: 1. Hannah, born May 18, 1642, (married Nehemiah Royce, Nov. 20, 1
2 CONC 660). 2. James, born March 3. 1644, married Mary Vine, Nov., 1666. 3. John, born March 30, 1645 (married, (1) Rachel Dymond; married (2) Elizabeth Williams, widow). 4. Joseph, born Nov. 29, 1646, married Dorothy Park, April, 1670.
2 CONC  5. Abraham, born Sept. 3, 1648, died Aug., 1649. 6. A daughter died in infancy, born Nov. 17, 1650.
2 CONT *  II. JAMES (2 of I. above), March 3, 1644, m., Nov., 1666, (1) Mary Vine; married (2) Hannah Cromwell. He was deacon of the first church in Groton until his death; captain of the train band, 1692, by order of the Governor and Co
2 CONC uncil; deputy to the General Court from New London, 1689 to 1700, and from Groton in 1706; commissioned to advise and direct the Pequots ten years. His will is dated, Groton, June 25, 1708; probated Jan. 22, 1712. Issue by last wi
2 CONC fe: 1. James, born 6 Feb., 1667, married Hannah --- , and second wife Anna --- . 2. William, born March, 1669, married Margaret Avery, 17 July, 1696. 3. Mercy, born March 20, 1670, married Thomas Starr, Jan. 1, 1695, 4. Hannah, bo
2 CONC rn June 8, 1674, married William Latham, June 30, 1698. 5. Elizabeth, born Sept. 9, 1678, married Jonathan Starr, Jan. 12, 1698. 6. Jerusha, born 1682, was living in 1712, at date of father's will. MERCY, who married Thomas Starr,
2 CONC  of Groton, son of Samuel Starr, of New London, Jan. 1, 1695, had issue: .... 
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/historyoffamilyo00morg#page/111/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.111
2 CONT *  VI. Deacon William, b. March 4, 1669, (2 of II. p. 105); married Margaret Avery, dau. of Captain James, of Groton, July 17,1696. Deacon of the first church, Groton, Nov. 8, 1704. d. Dec. 25, 1750. Issue: 1. William, born April 
2 CONC 7, 1697; married Hannah Stanton, Sept. 21, 1721. '''2. Margaret, born Sept. 10, 1698; m. Samuel Davis, Jan. 29, 1719'''. 3. Deborah, born July 14, 1700. 4. Hannah, born 1702. 5. Jerusha, born Jan. 14, 1704; m. Humphrey Avery, Feb.
2 CONC  5, 1724. 6. Joseph, b. August 10, 1706; m. Dorothy Avery, Dec. 4, 1735. 7. Solomon, born Oct. 5, 1708; m. Mary Walworth, July 1, 1742. 8. Elizabeth, b. July 10, 1710; m. John Avery, Jr. 9. Mary, b. Jan. 5, 1714: m. Jonas Belton, 
2 CONC Aug. 7, 1737. '''Margaret, m. Samuel Davis, of Groton, Jan. 19, 1719. Issue: 1. Samuel, b. Nov. 2, 1719: d. Feb. 17, 1720. 2. Hannah, b. Feb. 15, 1721; d. May 17, 1721. 3. Margaret, b. Aug. 4, 1722; d. May 26,1723, 4. Lucy Davis, 
2 CONC b. June 15, 1724; m. Ebenezer Avery, of Preston, had four daughters, and d. 1756. Margaret, wife of Samuel, d. Aug. 15, 1724, he m., 2d, Sarah, widow of John Gardiner, of Gardiner's Island, dau. of Gov. Saltonstall; had two childr
2 CONC en by her'''. Jerusha, m. Humphrey Avery, of Groton, Feb. 5, 1724. Issue: 1. Humphrey, b. 1725. 2. William, b. 1726. 3. Solomon, b. 1728. 4. Solomon, 2d, b. 1729. 5. Samuel, b. 1731. 6. James, b. 1733. 7. Jerusha, b. 1735. 8. Palm
2 CONC er, b. 1737. 9. Christopher, b. 1739. Elizabeth, m. John Avery, Jr., of Groton. Issue: 1. Elijah, Caleb and Aaron. Mary m. Jonas Bolton, of Groton, Aug. 7, 1737, Issue: Mary, b. 1739.  _______________________________________
