2 CONT
2 CONT DAR Ancestor - Jacob Davis Sr & Jr. Served in the Militia during the Revolutionary War. 
2 CONT    1.  JAMES STANDCLIFF book pages 332-333.  Jacob Davis Jr of Simsbury had his tax abated for Military Service in 1778 [Connecticut Archives, Military Volume VI, document 38a]  A Jacob Davis served in troops of the Connecticut Li
2 CONC ne.  The National Archives confirms this fact but the records contain no real information on this man.
2 CONT    2.   HARWICH, MA VITAL RECORDS page 70  Massachusetts, Town & Vital Records,1620-1988.  Harwich - First Book at Records, 1698-1790, Book 1. [i] Keziah Davis Daughter of Jacob and Keziah Davis his wife was born June the 16 Day 1
2 CONC 732. [ii] Mary Davis Daughter of Jacob Davis and Mary Davis his second wife was born in June the 12 Day 1736. [iii] Susanna Davis Daughter of Jacob and Mary Davis was born in December the 10 Day 1738. [iv] Jacob Davis son of Jacob
2 CONC  and Mary Davis was born in November the 16 Day 1741.
2 CONT    3.  Rolls of Connecticut Men in the French & Indian War 1755-1762. Volume I 755-1762.  [Collections of the CT Historical Society. Vol IX-Hartford 1903] Page 211.  Militia Company- Capt. Trumble's Company.  A pay Role for the me
2 CONC n under the Command of Captain Ammy Trumble in the Alarm in the year 1757 in their march to the relief of Port Edward and Parts Adjacent have nave not been Paid included the name JACOB DAVIS JR.
2 CONT    4.   Information:  Robert C & Sherry S Stancliff.  recently obtained information proves that Jacob Davis, Jr., served during the "Lexington Alarm" enlisting April 21, 1775 from Simsbury, CT under the Command of Elisha Phelps & 
2 CONC Job Case.  Which means that descendants of Stanborugh and Sibble are eligible for membership in the DAR.
2 CONT    5.  Page 332-333.  DAVIS FAMILY I  Parents of Sibbel Davis.  JACOB DAVIS JR [Jacob, Jabez, John, Dolar].  Son of Jacob Davis Sr, but it is not certain which of Jacob's wives was his mother, and no record of his exact birth date
2 CONC .  His father Jacob Davis Sr, married Harwich, Barnstable County, MA 1730 Keziah Crosby who died there Dec. 31, 1732, married Eastham, Barnstable County MA June 14, 1733 Mary Rogers daughter of James and Susannah [Tracy] Rogers bo
2 CONC rn Eastham, MA November 12, 1698, married 3 before 1747 Ann ______ and we know of her existence only through a gravestone cut by James Stancliff Jr for a child buried in Simsbury which reads "Silas Davis, born 1747 died 1754, son 
2 CONC of Jacob and Ann".  So this indicated that the Davis family was in Simsbury by 1754.  No records of Jacob Senior in the Simsbury Vital Records, but on May 7, 1763, Jacob Davis and Jacob Davis Jr both signed their names as inhabita
2 CONC nts of Simsbury to a petition by the inhabitants of Farmington, Windsor and Simsbury, CT to control the fishing at the mouth of the Windsor Ferry River [now called the Famington River] James Stanclift also signed the petition.
2 CONT   Jacob Jr was born before 1742, since he was a freeman and inhabitant of Sinsbury in 1762 and therefore at least age 21 at that time.  He married Simsbury, CT March 27, 1760, Elizabeth Lewis "of New Hartford", Litchfield County, 
2 CONC CT, this date daughter of Thomas and Mary [Rowley] Lewis baptized Westchester Parish, Colchester, New London County, CT in 1743.  While Elizabeth was married in Simsbury, just as sister Mary Lewis had been, she was listed as "of N
2 CONC ew Hartford" this date.  Her sister Mary [Lewis] Stanclift was living in the Wintonbury area of Simsbury and James Stanclift was cutting stones in New Hartford, Litchfield County, CT.  Another sister Esther [Lewis] Wright, wife of
2 CONC  Earl Wright was also living New Hartford at this time.  Elizabeth was probably living with on  of those families at the time of her marriage.
2 CONT   The family belonged to St. Andrews church in Simsbury.  St. Andrews was part of the Church of England and the Pastor, Roger Viets, was imprisoned as a Tory during the Revolutionary War.  Jacob Davis Jr evidently did not adhere t
2 CONC o the political views of his pastor for Jacob Davis Jr of Simsbury had his tax abated for Military Service in 1778 [Connecticut Archies, Military Volume VI, document 38a].  A Jacob Davis served in troops of the Connecticut Line.  
2 CONC The National Archives confirms this fact but the records contain no real information on this man.￼
