3 STAE Province of Massachusetts
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Not named in the list of children children of John Phillips and wife Joanna in Robert Charles Anderson, Great Migration Begins, Boston, MA: NEHGS (1995), p. 1455.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Philips-166
2 CONT
2 CONT Unknown Origin
2 CONT
2 CONT Joanna was born c. 1628, probably in England. Her parents and maiden name are unknown.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth date is approximate, based on her age at death as reported in the Milton Vital record.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: Profile previously reported birth place Wivenhoe, Essex Co., England, (without evidence or appropriate source).
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT Joanna married about 1650 to Robert Badcock.[1] Robert died on 12 November 1694 at Milton (p 204).[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Death and Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT Joanna died on 4 December 1700 at Milton, Suffolk (now Norfolk) County, Massachusetts, aged 71 years old.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT BABCOCK, Johanna, relick of Capt. Robert, Dec. 4, 1700, 71 yrs. (p. 205)[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Robert and Joanna:[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT # Samuel Badcock b. at Dorchester in 1650; d. Sept. 17, 1690. m. July 1, 1674, Hannah Eames.
2 CONT # Jonathan Badcock b. at Dorchester in 1652; d. Coventry, CT Jan. 5, 1632; m. 1st, August 1, 1676, at Milton, Mary Curtis ; m. 2nd Oct. 19, 1719 Mary Hebard.
2 CONT # James b. 1654.
2 CONT # Abigail Badcock b. 1656 married John Barber.
2 CONT # Nathaniel Badcock b. 14 March 1658. d. January 7, 1719. married Hannah.
2 CONT # Caleb b. 14 Aug. 1660.
2 CONT # Ebenezer Badcock b. 2 Oct. 1662. married Hannah.
2 CONT # Hopestill Badcock. b. 1663.
2 CONT # Hannah Badcock b. 8 Feb. 1664/5 married Joseph Morse.
2 CONT # Elizabeth Badcock b. 24 Dec. 1666 married Henry Vose.
2 CONT # Thankful b. 18 Feb. 1669.[4]married (1) George Lyon married (2) Richard Smith Sr
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Research Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT The Milton Records ... reports the births of the four of the five youngest children, but not Hopestill (p 2) and the deaths of Robert Badcock and Joanna (pp 204-5).[3]
