2 CONT 12:49: Patricia (Walsh) Tidwell edited the Biography and Death Place for Thomas Lapham Sr (1608-1648). [Thank Patricia for this]
2 CONT
2 CONT This page has been accessed 1,837 times. The Puritan Great Migration. Thomas Lapham Sr migrated to New England during the Puritan Great Migration (1620-1640). Join: Puritan Great Migration Project Discuss: PGM Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Lapham came from Tenteden, Kent, England. Calculating from the date he was married, Thomas would have been born about 1612, but some have dated his birth as July 18, 1608.[1][2][3][4] He sailed to Massachusetts on the Hercu
2 CONC les of Dover in March 1634/5 as a servant to Nathaniel Tilden.[5][6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Lapham was in Scituate in by 1637, when he built his house. His residence was near the harbour.[7] On March 13, 1636/7, he married Mary, daughter of Elder Nathaniel Tilden (one of the founders of Scituate) and Lydia Huckste
2 CONC p.[8] He and Mary had at least six children: Elizabeth b. 1638; Mary Lapham b. 1639; Thomas and Lydia, twins, born 1643; Rebecca b. 1645; and Joseph b. 1648. Thomas' twin Lydia was the wife of Samuel Bates of Hingham m. 1666.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is number 34 on the list of members of the church at Scituate, showing that he joined on April 24, 1636. [9] Plymouth Colony Records show that he requested to be made a freeman on March 5, 1638/9.[citation needed] In August 164
2 CONC 3, he was shown on the Scituate list of men capable of bearing arms.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas died in 1648. His will was dated two years before, but it was allowed to be proved at court on June 5, 1651.[11][12]
2 CONT
2 CONT LAST WILL AND TESTAMENT OF THOMAS LAPHAM OF SCITUATE
2 CONT
