1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Inscribed on John Washburn's Tombstone;  "John Washburn was born 1646, married Rebecca Lapham 1679, died 1719, aged 73 years. He gave the land for this burying yard and meeting-house."
2 CONT
2 CONT To rest by a subsequent articles in NEHGR and TAG, NEHGR 115:83 questioned a marriage to Rebecca Lapham and the accuracy of Bridgewater VR to 1850, which gives marriage and cemetery information for this marriage. This study, propo
2 CONC sed her to be possibly the wife of Samuel White who was born (or baptized) on the same day as she and and whose wife Rebecca is believed to have died in 1717. While the articles does not conclude that Rebecca Lapham was not John's
2 CONC  wife, it asks for the conclusion that Rebecca Lapham as John's wife is not proven. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel White and wife Rebecca continued having children after Rebecca Lapham married John Washburne.  There is extensive history on Rebecca Lapham and John Washburne, suggesting that Rebecca Lapham was not the wife of Samuel White
2 CONC .
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT [browermd2 (Mary Demoranville, Margaret Crapo web page I34939).FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT !An article in "New Eng. Hist. and Gen. Reg." V. 115 pg 83 entitiled "Rebecca Lapham, wife of Samuel White or John Washburn?", by Mrs. John E. Barclay, F.A.S.G., of Whitman, Mass, gives evidence that the wife of Samuel White was n
2 CONC ot daughter (or grand-daughter) of James Green as some sources have stated. She says "it has been thought that she was the grand-daughter of James Greene who died in Barnstable in 1731, naming in his will his grand-daughter Rebecc
2 CONC a White. ... If a thorough search of this family had been made, it would have been found that James Greene had a son James born 15 Dec 1665, who married about 1687 Anna Greenwood, and that their daughter Rebecca, born 19 May 1688,
2 CONC  married ...Isaac White of the Brookline White family." She suggests she may have been daughter of Thomas Lappham. The most compelling evidence is in the original records of the Town of Rochester, Vol. 1 pg 4, wherein it says "Sam
2 CONC uel White, Sr, and his wife were born in March about ye 13 in ye year 1646 taken from his own record with ye births of his children". In searching Scituate Church records she found, on the first page, the following: "Baptisms, 15 
2 CONC Mar 1646: Samuel White, ye son of Resolved Rebecca Lappham, ye daughter of Thomas". She further shows that there is no authentic evidence that Rebecca, the daughter of Thomas Lappham, married John Washburn as the original record o
2 CONC f the marriage did not give the name of the bride, only the name of the husband. She said the printed records of Bridgewater added the name of the bride giving as sources of the information an Old Cemetery gravestone record and a 
2 CONC version of the marriages published in NEHGR Vol. 21 pg 225. The gravestone record calls her only Rebecca, died 1717, and does not give her age. The marriage records in NEHGR was by Bradford Kingman in which he had added names of b
2 CONC rides with no reference to the source of his information. She said it is strongly suspected that he "gleaned these names from Nahum Mitchell's 'History of the Early Settlement of Bridgewater' (1840), some of which are now known to
2 CONC  be incorrect." She says John Washburn was married in 1679 when Rebecca would have been 33 years old, which seems to her unusually old for a first marriage. On the other hand, Samuel White and his wife Rebecca married about 1668 w
2 CONC hen they were both 22. The author states "If we are unwilling to accept their baptismal records as proof that Rebecca Lapham married Samuel, then we should at least concede that the parentage of Rebecca, wife of John Washburn, as 
2 CONC well as that of Rebecca, wife of Samuel White, is unknown." The research on the William White family in "Mayflower Descendants Through Five Generations" Vol 1, says "prob. the daughter bp. to Thomas Lapham at Scituate 15 Marcch 16
2 CONC 45/6...." There are family group sheets in the FGRA collection in the Family History Library of Salt Lake City for Thomas Lapham and Mary Tilden with Rebecca as the 5th of 6 children and it gives her husband as John Washburn. (see
2 CONC  RIN 9880-9894, unconnected) It gives the sources: "Mass. Will date 15 June 1664," (this is the will of Thomas Lapham, written when Rebecca was just 8 years old), "Deans Hist. Scituate, Plymouth, Colony, Records". It shows that Ma
2 CONC ry married (2) William Bassett. There are no parents given for Thomas but Mary's parents are given and there are FGRA sheets for them, their parents and grandparents. There is an earlier LDS proxy baptism, 6 Sep 1930 SLAKE, in the
2 CONC  1997 IGI with birth abt 1648, no parents, Relative: Gertrude C. P. Nelson. Also, there are several entries for Rebecca Lapham, one with birth abt 1650, Duxbury, no parents, Relative: Susannah W. Bowles, LDS proxy baptism 11 Jun 1
2 CONC 912 MANTI, endowment 10 Dec 1915 MANTI, sealing to parents "Pre-1970". from temple record. Several with birth abt 1645, daughter Thomas Lapham and Mary Tilden, the earliest proxy ordinances 12 Jun 1951, no temple, 26 Jun 1952 ALBE
2 CONC R, sealing to parents 19 Apr 1954 LOGAN, family group submission. Several with those parents, later ordinances.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Inscription: Rebecca, wife of John Washburn died about 1717. Her age is unknown. She was the first person buried in this yard. Rebecca Lapham Birth: Mar. 13, 1645 Scituate Plymouth County Massachusetts, USA Death: 1717 Bridgewater
2 CONC  Plymouth County Massachusetts, USA Rebecca Lapham, daughter of Thomas Lapham and Mary Tilden, was the wife of John Washburn (whose g grandfather, Francis Crooke came over on the Mayflower). Burial: Old Graveyard West Bridgewater 
2 CONC Plymouth County Massachusetts, USA Plot: Section A, Grave 59 Source: Photograph by Randall Younker 10 Apr 2005 http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr
