2 CONT Note: 
2 CONT Ann Holland married for her second husband Hugh Burt, and accompanied him on the voyage to New England in 1635 on the ship "Abigail", bringing with them her son William Bassett age 9 and other children.
2 CONT Was she really a Burrell? Called Francis Burrell her brother. He had wife Ann also. Francis Burrell could have been her brother-in-law (brother of her sister but seems not likely if his wife was named Ann also. Perhaps husband of 
2 CONC her huband Hugh Burt's sister?)
2 CONT The feat and hysteria of witchcraft, which gripped New England and culminated in the infamous witch trials at Salem in 1692, had been a sinister undercurrent for many years. In 1669 a complaint against Widow Burt for witchcraft wa
2 CONC s made in Salem Court. It is not certain whether this was Widow Ann Burt, mother of William Bassett, or Sarah Burt, widow of Hugh Jr. The younger Hugh would have been 49 at this time, had he lived, and his wife possibly younger. T
2 CONC he terms "awld wich" and "old goody Burt" might have been applied more aptly to Ann. At any rate, the ridiculous charges offer an interesting sidelight on the life and superstitions of the time. The following appeared in the Quart
2 CONC erly Court records of Salem, November 20, 1669 (IV:207)
2 CONT "Bethiah Carter, aged 23 years, dwposed that she heard Sarah Townsan say when she was a maid & lived with Goodwife Burt that the latter told the said Sarah if she could believe in her God she would cure her body and soul, but Good
2 CONC wife Burt said she could not cure her own husband because he would not believe in her God, but her maid did and was cured. Since them the said Sarah had been sorely afflicted with sad fits "Crying out and Rayling against me saying
2 CONC  My father carryed me to boston but Carryed her too Lin too and awld wich." Sara told her further that she had seen the said Burt appear often at her bed's feet in the day and night."
2 CONT "Phillip Reade, Physician, aged about 45 Years, deposed that he bing sent for three several times to see Sara townsan and her sister Carter, buth being very ill, "Sd Sarah townsend being in a more sadder Condiccion he had noe oppe
2 CONC rtunyty to Examine her Condiccion but did playnly percieve there was no Naturall caus for such unnaturall fits but being sent for the 4th time and finding her in a meat Capassity to Give information of her agruanc and Caus of her 
2 CONC former fits she tould me the abovesd Burt had afflict her wors one hower after she had a sadder fit than any ever sha had afore: then I askt her whoe afflict her Now and what the matter was she Replide with a great scrich she had 
2 CONC tould me already and that she did Now Suffer for it."
2 CONT "John Knight aged about 47 years deposed that he "was goinge to fetch some things for his wife and he saw old goody burt coming out of a swamp and shee was in her smok sleeves and a blake hancacher and black cap on her head and he
2 CONC e looked upp and suddenly shee was gone out of sight and I looked aboute and chould not see her, when I came into the house I found her in the same habit as I saw her and he said unto her did I not see you in the swamp even now an
2 CONC d she said noe I was in the house and he tould her she was a light headed woman."
2 CONT "Jacob Knight, aged about 24 or 25 years, deposed that "I boarded in the house of Mr. Cobit with my brother wornwood: in which house widdow Burt lived at that tyme; ,my brother & my sister being gone to Bostone: there being no fir
2 CONC e in my brothers roome, I went into sidow Burts roome to Lite my pipe, & tould her I had a paine in my head & soe went into my Lodging Roome which was through five dores (& stoopinge downe to Loose my shoe Looking upward there was
2 CONC  widdow Burt with a glasse bottle in her hand, & shee tould me there was something would doe my head good, or cure my head, and gave me the botle in my hand, when I had drunke it, I was worse in my head) but Concerning the five do
2 CONC res I passed through into my roome I think they were all shutt after mee, but howeer there is one flore yt must be passed over to Come into my Roome, that was so Loose yt it would make such a noiyse yt might in an ordinarye waye b
2 CONC e heard when passed over it, but I hear nothing & her sudden being with mee put mee into afright, & soe remained while the next morning though shee presentlye Left me & so the next morning but one, I being to go to Salem, intended
2 CONC  to tell my sister wormwood of it, before I went, but widdow Burt Comeing into my sister wormwoods Roome, sd I had a minde to saye something to my sister yt I would not have her heare, & this was before I had said anything & soe I
2 CONC  went out of the house, & then I tould my ister and goeing to Salem, I saw a Catt which being out of sight againe, I presently saw a dogg, it being Likewise prenentlye out of sight I saw one before me Like unto widdow Burt goeing 
2 CONC before mee down a hill as I was goeing upp it & soe I lost sight of her, the night following I lodged at my brother Knights at Salem I looking out of the chamber, it being a cleare moone light night, I saw widdow Burt uppon a gray
2 CONC e horse or mare in my brothers yard, or one in her shape & soe I awakened my cozen John Knight ht Lodged with mee & tould him of it, then neether hee nor I could see anything soe when hee was asleep againe shee appeared to mee in 
2 CONC the chamber, & then I tooke upp a peece of a barril head & thre it at her & as I thinke hit her on the brest & then could her ner noe more at that tyme."
2 CONT (Note: this might be Jacob, son of William Knight of Salem. Jacob must have overcome his fear of the Widow Burt. He is said to have married Sarah Burt on 25 December 1668. However he says his "cozen John" which might mean that he 
2 CONC is a son of one of William Knight's brothers.)
2 CONT "John Pearson, aged about 19 years, and Mary Burnop, aged about 26 years deposed that "Goodwife Burt Coming into the Roome wheare Sarah Pearson was asked her how shee did and she said the worse for her the said Burt seat down and 
2 CONC Laughed at ye said Sarah shee coming towards her said doust thou laugh and knowest thow heast done me a mischiefe I could find in my heart to baste they side the said Burt siad doe if thou durst and I will pay they side." Also sai
2 CONC d Sarah "Should spake much against her friends as evre shee did against her."
2 CONT Maddelene Pearson, aged about 50 years, deposed that she heard Sarah Pearson say when her father had her down to Goodwife Burts to be cured of her sore that the first night she was there the said Burt put her to bed, Etc. Burt sai
2 CONC d "Sarah will you smokit and giveing of the pipe she smokit it," and Sarah fell into fits again and said Goodwife Burt brought the devil to torment her."
2 CONT "Thomas Farar, aged above 50 years, deposed that his daughters Sarah and Elizabeth were "in former time sorely afflicted and in ther great extremety they would cry out and roare & say that they did see Goody Burt & say ther she is
2 CONC  doe you not see her kill her ther she is & that they said severall times and I have a son now in extreem misery much as the former hath bin and the docter says he is bewiched to his understanding."
2 CONT Court held at Salem. Judges Worshipful Mr. Simon Bradstreet, Samuell Simonds, Majr Daniell Denisson, Majr William Hathorne.
2 CONT The Quarterly Court records give no indication that any action was taken against Widow Burt.
2 CONT Her will as given in the Records and Files of the of the Quarterly Courts of Essex County, Massachusetts at Salem, Vol. V, 1672-1684:554, is as follows:
2 CONT "Will of An Bort dated June 8, 1664. My Wil is that Willyam Bassit Juner should have won of my kowes and John bassit should have won Cowe and Elisha bassit should have won kowe and that samewell bassit should have the steere. And 
2 CONC it is my will that theas Children should have the profitt of these Cattel and the prinsepell when they be eightene yeares ould and I give to Elisabeth bassit a new feather bed & boulster and a pillow and a pillow beare & blankit a
2 CONC nd a rouge and i give Sarah bassit my ould feather bed & boulster and pillow and A pillow beare A blankit and A tapsterri Covering and i give to meriam bassit a Copper ketel, A tabel Cloth and half a dosen of napkins and a ew shee
2 CONC p, hand towel and i give to Mary bassit my biggest eiron pot a long tabel Cloth and four napkins and a han towel a ew sheep And I give to hannah bassit tow eiron pots and a warmin pan and a pare of shetes and a pare of pillow bear
2 CONC es and a ew sheep. And i give to ellin bartom a ew lam and to hanna battrom an ew lam and I give to the wife of Willyam bartrom my black broadcloth sute and one puter basson and i give to liddi Burrill five shillens or a we lam an
2 CONC d my wil is that ther goodes should not be used till the Childerren doth reseve them & that these gearls should have the profitt of theas shep & the prensepel when they come to age. Witnesses: Francis Burrill, William Crofts. An (
2 CONC X) Bort
2 CONT "I doe desier my Brother Francis Burrill and good man Croft would see that this my wil be fulfilled."
2 CONT Inventory of estate of Ann Burt taken 18 March 1672/3.
2 CONT In the book The Devil Discovered Salem Witchcraft 1692, pub. by Hippocrene Books, NY 1991, p. 282
2 CONT "Because Ann Burt a midwife, had acquired a local reputation for ministering to the sick, she had been presented on charges of witchcraft in 1669. "Widdow Burt practiced healing. Like certain other women who had this skill, she wa
2 CONC s vulnerable to accusations of witchcraft from disappointed patients or suspicious neighbors."
