1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Martha Fitch''' (September 25, 1704 - March 6, 1763), born in Boston, was the daughter of Col. Thomas Fitch. Her first husband was James Allen. After his death, she married in 1755, as his second wife, William B
2 CONC rattle (1702-1776). He was a preacher, lawyer, and physician, a major general of militia, a loyalist, and friend of General Gage. He embarked with the British troops for Halifax in 1776. Martha Fitch Allen Brattle had no children.
2 CONT
2 CONT - From [http://arcade.nyarc.org/record=b1080334~S7 Frick Art Reference Library]
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From "The Loyalists of Massachusetts and the Other Side of the American Revolution" By James Henry Star [https://books.google.com/books?id=jkgSAAAAYAAJ&lpg=PA297&ots=EieVItiMqd&dq=william%20brattle%20katherine%20saltonstall&pg=PA2
2 CONC 94#v=onepage&q&f=false page 294-295]
2 CONT
2 CONT William Brattle, son of the former, was baptized by his father in 1706. He graduated from Harvard College in 1722, and was a member of the Ancient and Honorable Artillery Company. He was a theologian, and as a physician he was wid
2 CONC ely known, and no higher tribute to his eminence as a barrister need be sought than in the years 1736-7, when, only thirty years of age, he was elected by the House and Council to the office of Attorney General.
2 CONT
2 CONT He possessed strong peculiarities, and Sabine says of him that "A man of most eminent talents and of greater eccentricities has seldom lived." He inherited a large and well invested property, and had ample means to cultivate those
2 CONC  tastes to which, by his nature and education, he was inclined. He was for many years Major General of the Province, and afterwards Brigadier General. His large and beautifully situated house, which now exists in Cambridge, though
2 CONC  greatly transformed, known as the "Old Brattle House" was the resort of the fashion and style of this section of the country. At the age of twenty-one he married Katherine, the daughter of Governor Gurdon Saltonstall. She died at
2 CONC  Cambridge in 1752, and he married again in 1755, '''Mrs. Martha, widow of James Allen, and daughter of Thomas Fitch.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT From New England Life in the Eighteenth Century: Representative Biographies from ... By Clifford Kenyon Shipton [https://books.google.com/books?id=lxOxa8LCrAYC&lpg=PA204&ots=JyPSSZ7iZ7&dq=Martha%20(Fitch)%20Brattle&pg=PA204#v=onep
2 CONC age&q=Martha%20(Fitch)%20Brattle&f=false page 204]
2 CONT
2 CONT "When he [William Brattle] was quarreling with George Whitfield, his wife and daughter lay sick of throat distemper (19), probably in this instance diphtheria which he had brought into his home from the bedside of some patient.  F
2 CONC ive small daughters and a son were carried from the mansion to the burial ground.  Katherine Saltonstall Brattle survived these blows only to succumb to the smallpox on April 28, 1752.  The Major was married again on November 2, 1
2 CONC 755, this time to Martha Fitch Allen, widow of James Allen (A.B. 1717).  She died on August 26, 1763."
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:  Sibley's Harvard Graduates, Class of 1722
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://www.faimlytreemaker.genealogy.com/forum/surnames/topics/fitch/698/
2 CONT
2 CONT You may have interest in the compilation by: Roscoe Conkling Fitch, "Fitch Family History", (Privately Printed by the Fitch Family @ Record Publishing Company, Mass., 1930), Vol. I, Chapter XXI, Six Generations of the Descendants 
2 CONC of Deacon Zachary Fitch of Massachusetts, specifically page 229.
2 CONT
2 CONT Therein lies: "iv. MARTHA, b. Sept. 25, 1704; m. July 29, 1725, h. James Allen".
2 CONT
2 CONT Should this be your MARTHA, et JAMES, the ancestry (according to RC Fitch) would be:
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1. Zachary Fitch, m. Mary ______
2 CONT * 2. Thomas Fitch, m. Martha Fisk
2 CONT * 3. Thomas Fitch, m. Abiel Danforth
2 CONT * 4. Martha Fitch, m. James Allen
2 CONT
