1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT Merchant in Boston.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hon. (Judge) John WALLEY born 11 Sep 1691 Bristol, RI. Died 6 March 1745 Boston. Married 1714 Bethia EYRE (she born 1695). John and Bethia had issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. John, b. Oct. 6, 1716. Preacher, d. at Roxbury, March 2nd, 1784 — married an Elizabeth APPLETON, no issue. 2. Katherine,* b. Oct. 5, 1719, d. at Ipswich, unmarried, Dec. 5th 1782. 3. Sarah, b. Jan. 18, 1720/1, died in infancy. 
2 CONC 4. Sarah, b. Nov. 8, 1722, died at Ipswich, unmarried, May 30th 1748. 5. Bethia, b. March 3, 1723/4, died at Ipswich, unmarried, Jan. 21, 1748. 6. Thomas, b. Nov. 1, 1725. 7. William, bapt. June 29, 1729, died young. 8. Elizabeth,
2 CONC  b. April 28, 1731, died at Boston, unmarried, Aug. 31, 1754. 9. Mary, b. Feb. 11, 1732/3, d. at Ipswich Nov. 20, 17S6.
2 CONT
2 CONT * [Not John, as erroneously entered in Boston Records]
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: "An account of some of the descendants of Capt. Thomas Brattle" (Clapp & Son, Boston, 1867) by Edward Doubleday Harris.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Samuel Eliot Morison on the Plymouth Plantation:
2 CONT
2 CONT Morison says that in 1690 there was a Plymouth expedition to capture Quebec; that the commander-in-chief was (Sir) William Phipps and that the second in command was John Walley of Bristol {R.I.}age 46, son of Rev. Thomas Walley of
2 CONC  Barnstable. It appears that General Walley, despite service in the militia, was not qualified for the command, and Morison says he committed "many blunders"; the expedition was a costly failure.
2 CONT
2 CONT
