1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Source of some information of George Rood, his wife Hannah and their children; http://dunhamwilcox.net/ct/norwich2.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Source 2: http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GSln=rood&GSfn=george&GSbyrel=all&GSdy=1744&GSdyrel=in&GSob=n&GRid=151962027&df=all&
2 CONT
2 CONT ====WIKITREE: [https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Rood-230 George Rood (1672–1744)]
2 CONT * George Rood was the son of Thomas Rood who immigrated to the American Colonies from Glastonbury, Somerset, England, and Thomas' oldest daughter, Sarah. It being the first case of incest proved in New England, by the admission of
2 CONC  both Thomas and Sarah. Thomas was tried and condemned to be hanged in New London, and Sarah was to be whipped.
2 CONT * George was born in 1672, the year of his father's hanging.[1]
2 CONT * In 1675, the court ordered Thomas Leffingwell of Norwich to “keepe the child [George] of Sarah Rood until he reached the age of twenty-one. His mother Sarah moved to New London, situated at the mouth of the Thames River.[2]
2 CONT * "According to this, after Thomas was executed, Sarah was taken in by Dr. George Bushnell, which may explain why the son was named George..." http://wsite.site44.com/Genealogy/Rood-Thomas.html
2 CONT * Hannah Bush was new to the area of Norwich, when George began to court her, unaware that she was pregnant. In an attempt to cover the pregnancy, Hannah's new stepfather, Thomas Hall, encouraged George to spend the night with Han
2 CONC nah, to which he refused. [3]
2 CONT * George, age 30, married Hannah Bush, age 20, in Norwich, Connecticut in July 1702. [4][1]
2 CONT * In October 1702, Hannah appeared before the magistrate, Richard Bushnell in the New London County Court. It was revealed to the public that she was pregnant at the time of her marriage, due to an incestuous relationship, forced 
2 CONC upon her by her new stepfather, Thomas Hall and with her mother's full support and help. So once again, 30 years later, George Rood reappears in court records, this time in connection with a case of incest involving his wife Hanna
2 CONC h and her step-father, Thomas Hall. [3]
2 CONT * "On May 29th, the Halls were both sentenced under Connecticut’s new marriage law passed in 1702, which eliminated the death penalty for sexual crimes. Instead, Thomas and Susannah were to stand at the gallows with a rope around 
2 CONC their necks for one hour, receive a whipping not to exceed forty stripes, and wear a capital “I” two inches long and “proportional bigness in a contrasting color” for the rest of their lives. Hannah, having already confessed, but 
2 CONC found to have concealed the crime for “So Long a time” was to be whipped in Norwich."[5]
2 CONT * George and Hannah had the following children, all born in Norwich, Connecticut:
2 CONT ** Ebenerer [Ebenezer] Rood b 21 February 1704/5[4]
2 CONT ** Isaac Rood b 26 March 1706/7[4]
2 CONT ** Solomon Rood b _____[4]
2 CONT ** Hanah Rood b 16 July 1712[4]
2 CONT ** Simeon Rood b 16 August 1714 d July 1727[4]
2 CONT ** Keziah Rood b latter end of December 1716[4]
2 CONT ** Experience Rood b 20 January 1719/20[4]
2 CONT ** Thankfull Rood b 2 February 1721/2[4] 
2 CONT * His wife, Hannah died on 1 Jan 1743/4 in Norwich, Connecticut.[4]
2 CONT * George died 10 March 1744 also in Norwich, Connecticut.[4] 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==== Research Notes
2 CONT # This unsourced statement was previously a part of George's profile, "They lived in Norwich, and later removed to Lisbon, where they lived the rest of their lives." It does not appear to be correct, given they both died in Norwic
2 CONC h. This information is copied from his Find a Grave Memorial on which no source information is provided.
2 CONT # George Rood was baptized, as an adult, on 3 June 1709 in the Newent-Lisbon Church. It is presumed that he was a farmer. [citation needed]
2 CONT # The death location of Lisbon, New London County, Connecticut also comes from FindAGrave. '''No source''' is provided. Again George and Hannah's death is recorded in the Norwich, CT vital records, as cited on this profile. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Sources
2 CONT # George was 30 when he married Hannah. Alicia Desiree Martin-Cowger ''A Great Appearance of Force: Puritan Family Government in Colonial Connecticut, 1672-1725''. Thesis. August 2010, p. 65
2 CONT # ''Alicia Desiree Martin-Cowger A Great Appearance of Force: Puritan Family Government in Colonial Connecticut, 1672-1725''. Thesis. August 2010, pp, 64-65
2 CONT #  Alicia Desiree Martin-Cowger ''A Great Appearance of Force: Puritan Family Government in Colonial Connecticut, 1672-1725''. Thesis. August 2010, pp. 65-66, 69
2 CONT # Norwich (Conn.); ''General Society of Colonial Wars (U.S.). Connecticut. Vital records of Norwich, 1659-1848''. 1913, pp. 41-42
2 CONT # Alicia Desiree Martin-Cowger ''A Great Appearance of Force: Puritan Family Government in Colonial Connecticut, 1672-1725.'' Thesis. August 2010, p. 72
2 CONT # Norwich CT: ''Vital Records of Norwich, 1659-1848''. (Online database. AmericanAncestors.org. New England Historic Genealogical Society, 2015.) Originally published as: ''Vital Records of Norwich, 1659-1848. Hartford, Society of
2 CONC  colonial wars in the state of Connecticut'', 1913. Vol.: Norwich-V1, pp. 41-42. 
