1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==notes==
2 CONT The name Lovitt is spelled differently by various members of the family. It can be seen as Lovet, Lovett, Lovit, and Lovitt. All of these names tied together within this genealogy area, regardless of the spelling of last names, ar
2 CONC e indeed from the same family.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==comments==
2 CONT Date and place of birth have also been (erroneously?) reported to be 1758 in Whitley County, Kentucky (year attested on his Find A Grave memorial, place unattested and not yet organized in that year, conflicts with his WIkiTree re
2 CONC cord).
2 CONT
2 CONT ==links==
2 CONT * https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/53740767/edward-needham-lovitt
1 FAMC @F6000000184312943861@
1 FAMS @F6000000005029276321@
1 RFN geni:6000000024388408602
1 SUBM @S6000000002144872527@
1 OBJE
2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p13/8a/69/ba/44/5344484a354a5d21/httpswww_original.jpg?hash=7227c66f6d787438dbb4335e262da8570c4dcae278f7c5807f7b0a2defe219ea.1793951999
2 FORM jpg
2 _PRIM Y
1 NOTE sold 69 acres located in Dobbs Co., North Carolina to JamesForehand onMay 6, 1783, as stated in an Abstract of Deed Book,Wayne Co., NorthCarolina.
1 NOTE Decendent was Joe Lovett (Aug 22, 1881 - Nov 6 1965).  Edward was listed on the 1790 NC census.  Turn of the century, Edward settled in what is now McCreary County.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: sold 69 acres located in Dobbs Co., North Carolina to James Forehand on May 6, 1783, as stated in an Abstract of Deed Book,Wayne Co., North Carolina
2 CONT
2 CONT     The earliest record found shows Edward Lovitt had settled, by 1780, in
2 CONT     Dobbs County, NC and remained there until after 1790. Dobbs County was
2 CONT     formed in 1758 from Johnston. By 1791 Dobbs County was abolished.
2 CONT     In the 1810 Knox County Census it shows the Lovitts living next to the
2 CONT     Wilson Oliver family. Anna Charity Lovitt, daughter of Edward Lovitt, was
2 CONT     married to Wilson Oliver.
2 CONT     A descendant of Edward Lovitt has stated "I was told by my grandfather,
2 CONT     that Edward Lovitt served in the Revolutionary War under General Francis
2 CONT     Marion. " General Marion was know as the "Old Swamp Fox".
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT GEDCOM: From a GedCom supplied by Charlotte Mayfield Jackson dated Jun, 1997; E-Mail: CAJ1936@aol.com.
2 CONT
2 CONT I have seen the Lovett and Lovitt name spelled both ways when it pertains to Edward Needham. Needs more research.
2 CONT
2 CONT From the "Journal of North Carolina Genealogy" by William Perry Johnson, Editor, Summer 1966, Vol. X II, No. 46, PP 1663 and 1670.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some say the Lovitt's arrived in America, possibly by way of Canada, and Nova Scotia. This remains to be discovered. The original spelling and pronunciation of Lo Vette is French -- the first syllable "LO" pronounced with a long O
2 CONC , the last five letters in the second syllable pronounced "Vay" with a long A.
2 CONT
2 CONT All too often for it to be coincidental, correspondents have stated, "We have been told by family members our Lovett line originated in France." Further confirmation of French ancestry is the olive complexion, black hair, and dark
2 CONC  eyes. There are strong indications of connections with the Cherokee Indians, as will be related. That would account for the dark features but certainly not the name. The question has been raised whether Edward Lovitt's wife was I
2 CONC ndian, also Cherokee. This was not an unusual situation as many Frenchmen migrating from Canada into America during the Colonial days did marry Indian women.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thus far, the earliest record found shows an Edward Lovitt had settled, by 1780, in Dobbs County, North Carolina and remained there until after 1790. Dobbs County was formed in 1758 from Johnston. By 1791, Dobbs County was abolish
2 CONC ed, having been divided into four other counties: Wayne -- 1779,
2 CONT Jones -- 1788, Lenoir and Glasgow -- 1791. Glasgow's name changed to Greene County in 1799.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dobbs County 1780 Tax List: (present day Greene and Lenoir County, North Carolina) Indicates that Edward Lovitt was 400 lbs.
2 CONT
2 CONT District No. 1 -- Lenoir County -- From Kingston west, but north of Neuse River Order in which taxpayer appears, Name and Value of Property. Lovitt, John 334 lbs. Lovitt, David 122 lbs.
2 CONT
2 CONT District No 10 -- Greene County -- From Snowhill to south as far as Wheat Swamp, including Hookerton vicinity and westward to include Arba. Lovitt, Morris 400 lbs.
2 CONT
2 CONT * The relationship, if any, of John, David and Morris to Edward is not known.
2 CONT
2 CONT FIRST U.S. CENSUS, 1790 NEWBERN DISTRICT, DOBBS COUNTY, NORTH CAROLINA
2 CONT
2 CONT LOVIT, EDWARD 1 male over 16 (Edward)
2 CONT 2 males under 16 (#2 and #3 sons)
2 CONT 3 free females (wife, #4 daughter and Anna)
2 CONT
2 CONT Researchers have been unsuccessful in locating this family in an 1800 census or tax list or proof indicating who were Edward's wife, #2-son and #4 daughter. One source has stated Edward had a daughter named Marie.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1790 to 1804, Edwards whereabouts are unknown, but believed him to be the same man found in Knox, County, Kentucky tax records of 1805 and 1806. Also, on March 26, 1808, Edward Lovett paid taxes on land; on July 10, 1808 Edmu
2 CONC nd Lovet paid taxes on land; on July 10, 1808 *Nathaniel Lovet paid tax on one horse.
2 CONT
2 CONT * This is the only early record of a Nathaniel Lovet who would be at least 21 years old and fits the age category of either son.
2 CONT
2 CONT A careful study of records indicates the possibility of Edward Lovitt having married at least twice. If so, the older children by a previous marriage did not relocate (at least for any length of time) in Kentucky.
2 CONT
2 CONT U.S. FEDERAL CENSUS - 1810 KNOX COUNTY, KENTUCKY page 650
2 CONT Edward Lovitt 1 male 45 and over (Edward)
2 CONT 1 male 16-26 (Jonathan)
2 CONT 1 male 10-16 (Edom)
2 CONT 1 female 45 and over (wife)
2 CONT 1 female 10-16 (Celia)
2 CONT 1 female under 10 (Jemima Permelia)
2 CONT
2 CONT The 1810 Knox County Census show the Lovitt's living next door to the Wilson Oliver family whose wife is Anna Charity, daughter of Edward Lovitt.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Knox County in 1818, Whitley County is created where Edward pays taxes beginning in the year 1819 on "Wolf Creek" water course, living there until his death in 1845. He is listed in 1830 and 1840 in the Whitley County census 
2 CONC with his wife and son, Jonathan who remained in the household with both his parents until their deaths.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Union College Library at Barbourville, Kentucky is an unpublished manuscript by Elmer Mercer which includes a list of Knox County Revolutionary Veterans from Virginia, North Carolina and other states. Among those named, Edward 
2 CONC Lovitt. Mr. Mercer did not give sources; just stated most of them from Eastern Virginia, the Eastern Carolinas and Maryland all were over 45 years of age in 1810.
2 CONT
2 CONT A descendant of Edward Lovitt has stated, "I was told by my Grandfather, that Edward Lovitt served in the Revolutionary War under General Francis Marion. General Marion, also known as "Old Swamp fox", took his troops through the s
2 CONC wamps of South Carolina into North Carolina. If Edward Lovitt's Revolutionary War Records could be located, there would possibly be valuable information to further aid the search.
2 CONT
2 CONT !SOURCE: This GedCom was received from Charlotte Mayfield via E-Mail: Mayson1936@aol.com Dated: Sat, 9 Jun 2001 15:44:44 EDT. Relates to our Mayfield connections.
