1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A Patriot of the American Revolution for NEW HAMPSHIRE with the rank of LIEUTENANT.  [https://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A072567  DAR Ancestor # A072567]
2 CONT
2 CONT Index of the Rolls of Honor (ancestor's Index) in the Lineage ..., Volumes 57-59 Abel Lyman (1752-1823) was lieutenant, 1776, in Col. Jonathan Chase's New Hampshire regiment and in 1777 assisted in the capture of Burgoyne. He was 
2 CONC born in Lebanon, Conn.; died in Lebanon, N.H.
2 CONT
2 CONT History of the Early Settlers of Sangamon County, Illinois: "centennial Record"
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1775 he was commissioned a first lieutenant, and in the winter of 1775-76 commmanded a company of militia raised in Grafton, NH, and marched to Quebec, by way of Lake Champlain, where he joined the army under Benedict Arnold, a
2 CONC nd was engaged in the unsuccessful attempt to capture the city. He was attacked with small pox just before the army commenced to retreat, but kept up, though suffering greatly from the disease, and by way of Montreal, Lake Champla
2 CONC in and Onion River, arrived in Lebanon in May.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Sept 1776, he again took command of his company, and in 1777 marched to Saratoga and assisted in the Battle and capture of Burgoyne and the army that had driven them out of Canada.
2 CONT
2 CONT He then returned to N.H., and was engaged in guarding the frontiers against the Indians.
