1 NOTE {geni:about_me} "I presume there are a number present who remember my father's  brother George Swetland. In 1884 he lost his life while returning home  from the blacksmith's shop, where he had been to have his horse shod. As  he n
2 CONC eared the railroad track his horse became frightened at the fast ex-  press train from New York to Boston. As he was on the track the engine  struck his carriage and lifted it up and carried it quite a distance, the  number on the
2 CONC  engine struck him on the head just over the ear and in-  stantly killed him. He was a kind hearted, generous man and he repre-  sented his ward in the city government at two different times. He was  highly respected by all who kn
2 CONC ew him. His widow is still living at the  age of 84. He had three daughters and two sons. The daughters have all  passed away. His youngest son Frank lives in the suberbs of our City of  Homes and is the father of 15 children, of 
2 CONC whom only five are living."
2 CONT
2 CONT [exerpt from 1st annual Swetland Family Reunion, spoken by Eli Swetland, Jr., 1896]
