1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Catharine (Seiler) Swetland was the daughter of Dr. Peter and Anna Margaret (Laubach) Seiler. She was the wife of William Swetland. They were married at First Reformed Church, Easton, Pennsylvania September 28, 181
2 CONC 9. She was 94 years, 4 months and 25 days old at the time of her death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Northern Christian Advocate October 25, 1893
2 CONT
2 CONT Catharine Swetland, widow of William Swetland, died at her home in Wyoming, Pennsylvania, October 6, 1893, aged ninety-four years. She was born near Easton, Pennsylvania, May 18, 1799. At the age of twenty she was married to Willi
2 CONC am Swetland and came to live at Wyoming. For nearly seventy-five years she had lived in the lovely Wyoming Valley. She and her husband possessed a generous spirit which made their name generally known and highly esteemed. Their gi
2 CONC fts were wise as well as generous. Swetland Hall of Wyoming Seminary stands to-day as a monument of this fact, and many churches have had more or less help from them and theirs. Sister Swetland was brought into the Methodist Churc
2 CONC h during the labors of the Rev. Thomas H. Pearne, together with her husband, William Swetland, Isaac C. Shoemaker, Payne Pettebone and many others. She lived a Christian life. She leaves one daughter, Mrs. Caroline Matilda Pettebo
2 CONC ne, and two grandchildren, Mrs. Allen H. Dickson, of Wilkes Barre, Luzerne County, Pennsylvania, and R. T. Pettebone, of Wyoming. She was buried in the family plot in Forty Fort Cemetery.  Written by Rev. Gideon C, Lyman 
2 CONT
2 CONT Rev. G. C. Lyman served Wyoming Methodist Episcopal Church, Wyoming Conference, Wilkes-Barre District 1890-1894. 
