2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Curtis, son of Thomas of Ash, Kent, England and brother of Thomas of York, never married (listed as unmarried in Plymouth Probate Records, below). He inherited the family property in
2 CONT S. Scituate which later became Norwell, Massachusetts. The Second Church of Scituate, to which the family belonged, became the First Church of Norwell, later Unitarian in it's beliefs - worshiper's believe in one God rather than t
2 CONC he Trinity.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Early Wills and Deeds - Title to Real Estate March 5, 1679 Settlement of Estate of John Curits - unmarried Plymouth Probate Records Book IV (1), f. 53 Plymouth Court Orders VI, pg. 39-40, June 1, 1680. Haroild, Vol. 1, p.1
2 CONC 5. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >"Deane [5] states that Chambers' step-son, John Curtis, lived at Curtis (now Butternut)* Hill which is about one mile north of the tract just mentioned. The compiler suspects that this was Chambers house-lot...John Curtis, who ne
2 CONC ver married, lived there and received it when Richardene died." *[Buttonwood Hill? I have Richardene's will. NRP]
2 CONT
2 CONT Letter Harold E. Curtis July 9, 1993 with The Four Emigrant Curtis Brothers and their English Background. Intro. to Harold Curtis' 3 Vol's on the Scituate Curtis's.
2 CONT Harold Everdell Curtis Genealogies, The Three Curtis Brother of Scituate, Mass.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vol. 1. Introduction, References & Index [to all 3 Vol.] (171 pages copied)
2 CONT Vol II, Part 1. Descendants of Thomas, William & Richard Curtis (183 pages copied) of Scituate, MA
2 CONT & Vol. II, Part 2; (18 pages copied) [Probably is now included in Vol. II with part 1].
2 CONT Vol III, Part 1. Descendants of William Curtis (103 pages copied). A later Vol. follow later William lines.
2 CONT
2 CONT Complete volumes of Harold E. Curtis copied by Curtis/s Soc. and available from Curtis Society (below)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Deane, he had a house near Curtis Hill which was burned by the Indians in 1676. He left no descendents on receord. (House probably stood on Washington Street in Hanover, near the end of Silver Street, or between that 
2 CONC and East Street.)
2 CONT
2 CONT From [https://archive.org/stream/cu31924070695105#page/n53/mode/2up Plymouth Colony Records], vol 6, pages 39–40 1 June 1680
2 CONT
2 CONT >In reference vnto the dispose of the estate of John Curtice, of Scittuate, late deceased, the Court haue ordered, that it shalbe deuided into fiue partes, wherof two partes therof is disposed vnto his eldest brother, Thomas Curti
2 CONC ce, liueinf att Yorke, in the Province of Maine, and the other three partes vnto Richard Curtice, and William Curtice, and Elizabeth the wife of Jacob Bumpas, onely the said woman is to haue her parte out of the mouables of the sa
2 CONC id estate, and not to haue any parte of the lands.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
