1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Morse-1176
2 CONT
2 CONT Capt. Joseph Morse Jr
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 26 Sep 1649 in Holiston, Middlesex, Massachusetts
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Joseph Morse and Hannah (Phillips) Boyden
2 CONT
2 CONT Brother of Samuel Morse, Hannah Morse, Mary (Morse) Plimpton, Sarah (Morse) Lawrence, Dorcas (Morse) Clark, Elizabeth (Morse) Lawrence, Nathaniel Boyden [half], Jeremiah Morse and Mary Morse
2 CONT
2 CONT Husband of Mehitabel (Wood) Morse — married 17 Oct 1671 (to 12 Nov 1681) in Medfield, Suffolk, Massachusetts Bay Colony, New England
2 CONT
2 CONT Husband of Hannah (Badcock) Morse — married 11 Apr 1683 (to 9 Nov 1711) in Sherborn, Middlesex, Massachusetts Bay Colony, New England
2 CONT
2 CONT Husband of Hannah (Baxter) Morse — married 17 May 1713 in Sherborn, Middlesex, Massachusetts Bay Colony, New England
2 CONT
2 CONT Father of Joseph Morse, Mehitable (Morse) Breck, James Morse, Hannah (Morse) Coolidge, David Morse, Isaac Morse Sr. and Keziah (Morse) Holbrook
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 19 Feb 1718 in Sherborn, Middlesex, Massachusetts
2 CONT
2 CONT Profile last modified 1 Aug 2020 | Created 30 Jul 2011
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Origin
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Morse was born on September 26, 1649 at Dedham, Massachusetts, the son of Joseph Morse and Hannah Phillips of Medfield,[1] was born on Sept. 26, 1649 (p. 111).v[2] (p. 4) [3] [4]
2 CONT
2 CONT His birth was recorded in the Dedham Records.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1649: Joseph, the son of Joseph and Annis Morse was borne the 26th of the 7 mo. (p. 4). [3] Positions and Property
2 CONT
2 CONT * Capt. Morse led the early patriot settlers of Sherborn and Medfield against the Indians. (p. 172), and signed the petition for the incorporation of Sherborn in 1674 and was among the first received as inhabitants.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was chosen Captain of their only Militia Company, one to treat with a minister to settle in Sherborn to build a parsonage to preserve order and obedience to laws. He personally commanded in the conflicts with the Indians in 167
2 CONC 5/6. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Capt. Morse was also chosen to moderate Town Meetings, served as Selectman, was a representative of Sherborn in the General Court at Boston. He was chosen to locate roads and divide the common lands, and his name very often appear
2 CONC s subscribed to important documents. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Death and Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT Capt. Joseph Morse died February 19, 1718, and was buried in the Morse Lot, Holliston Cemetery. [4] His remains were interred upon an eminence in the ancient Burying Ground in the south part of Sherborn. (p. 20) (p. 10) [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT  Inscription: Hon Joseph Morse Born Sept 26 1649 Died Feb 19 1717-18 His heroic courage and military skill Placed him among the foremost defenders Of the infant Colony From the wily and relentless savage Eminently wise in counsel 
2 CONC And of sterling integrity A just and God fearing man He was a chosen leader in affairs of CHURCH and STATE To perpetuate his Memory So deserving the respect and imitation Of his descendants This tablet is by one of them erected A.
2 CONC  D. 1879 [5] King Phillip's War
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a distinguished Indian fighter as well as a prominent citizen in Civil life.
2 CONT
2 CONT February 21, 1675/6: Capt. Morse was called into action when Medfield, assaulted by Philip at the head of five hundred warriors laid in ashes and eighteen persons massacred. He was at his remote settlement with his wife and her si
2 CONC ster in law, whose husband, being on the other side of the river, fell the first victim to the fierce tomahawk. Mrs. Morse lost her child, and her sister in law being pregnant and in confinement died. The two or three garrisons of
2 CONC  Boggestow consisted of only about ten or twelve men each.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Indian warriors deferred their attack on Boggestow until May 6, when they met the notorious repulse at the stone house at Boggestow Pond.
2 CONT
2 CONT On July 2,1676, there was a new conflict in the nearby forest. So vigorous was Captain Morse's attack, that a large number of the enemy were killed, and the rout was so complete, and the chastisement so severe, that the Indians ne
2 CONC ver dared to show their faces in that vicinity again." (p. 19) [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriages
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph married three times.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph married first on October 17, 1671 Mehitable Wood (p. 159) [7] daughter of Nicholas and Anna (Babcock) Wood. She was born July 22, 1655 in Sherborn, Massachusetts (p. 68)[8] being the first white child born in that town. She
2 CONC  died November 12, 1681. [4] Captain Joseph Morse was married on April 11, 1683, to Hannah Badcock (p. 116).[9] Hannah Badcock (born Milton 8 Feb 1664[/5?]) was the daughter of Robert and Joanna Badcock (p. 2).[10] [11] They settl
2 CONC ed in Boggestow, Indian name for present town of Sherborn about 1670 on the west side of Charles river (p. 19). [4] She died November 9, 1711. (p. 215) [8] [4] Captain Morse married on May 17, 1730, as his 3rd, and as her 2nd, to 
2 CONC Hannah (Baxter) Dyer, the widow of Capt. Joseph Dyer, of Weymouth (p. 67). [12] [4] Hannah born May 6, 1661, was the daughter of John and Hannah (White) Baxter. (p. 10-12) [13] His wife Hannah died Sept. 4, 1727.[4] Children
2 CONT
2 CONT The children of Joseph and Mehitable Wood were:[4] [9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Mehitable Morse, born April 25, 1673; died young. Joseph Morse, born April 3, 1676; died June 12, 1676. Elisha Morse, born December 12, 1677; died young. Joseph Morse, born March 25, 1679; died April 18, 1754; married Prudence Ada
2 CONC ms. Mehitable Morse, born November 2, 1681 (p. 66) [8]; married John Breck. Children of Joseph and Hannah Babcock: [8] [4]
2 CONT
2 CONT James Morse, born July 1, 1686 (p. 67); died June 5, 1725; married Ruth Swain. [4] Jeams Morse and Ruth Sawin (p. 156) [8] Hannah Morse, born April 5, 1689 (p. 67); died December 11, 1774; married Isaac Coolidge, Esq. Sarah Morse,
2 CONC  born April 12, 1692 (p. 69) [8]; married William Barrows. William Barron. (p. 105) [8] David Morse, born December 31, 1694 (p. 66) [8] ; died January 7, 1773; married Sarah Dyer. (p. 155) [8] Isaac Morse, born September 14, 1697 
2 CONC (p. 67) [8]; died 1750; married Elizabeth Drury. Keziah Morse, born June 30, 1700 (p. 68) [8]; died Feb. 18, 1754; married Samuel Holbrook. (p. 156) [8] Asa Morse, born Aug. 24, 1703 (p. 66) [8]; died October 7, 1770; married Mary
2 CONC  Rider. (p. 155) [8] Research Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT Vital Records are found at Holliston,[2] Dedham,[3] Milton,[10]Archive.org (Page 2).</ref> Sherborn,[8], Weymouth,[12] and Medfield. [9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
