1 NAME Lucy /Sprague/
2 GIVN Lucy
2 SURN Sprague
2 _MARNM Tracy
1 SEX F
1 BURI
2 PLAC Smyrna, Chenango, NY
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE 28 JUL 1735
2 PLAC Lebanon, New London Co., CT
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 6 MAR 1826
2 PLAC Smyrna, Chenango Co., NY
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} (Memories of her Grandmother by Flossie Fellows Lee)
2 CONT
2 CONT Abagail Tracy Norton used to tell stories of her Revolutionary Grandmother who was left a widow with a family to rear early in the Revolutionary War.  Her husband, Thomas Tracy, had joined the patriot army and died of smallpox.  S
2 CONC he had two sons and seven daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Both British and American soldiers robbed the settlers of their livestock until very few were left.  Grandmother Lucy Sprague Tracy was a strong pioneer woman.
2 CONT
2 CONT One day some British soldiers came to her door and demanded she tell them where her “arms” were.  She had some guns and ammunition hidden but was determined they should not have them.  Folding her arms across her beast, she looked
2 CONC  the young lieutenant proudly in the eye and said, “Here are my arms”.
2 CONT
2 CONT Angered, he placed his sword against her arm and demanded that she give him weapons. She could feel the prick of the sword point but she never flinched.  Just then a superior officer came and struck down the sword saying, ”We are 
2 CONC not fighting women and children!”
2 CONT
2 CONT Another time a few American soldiers came calling upon the patriots for supplies for the army.  Grandmother was told to have two pairs of knee length socks ready in a short time.  She had only one sheep.  Going to the barn, she be
2 CONC gan to sheer it by the light of a torch held by one of the children.  As soon as a handful of wool was shorn, one of the girls took it to the house and washed it, spread it on the hearth to dry.  As soon as it was dry enough, anot
2 CONC her daughter began to card it to spin, and then another began to spin yarn.  When she had enough, another took two spindles of single yarn and twisted it together.  Then another set up a stocking and began to knit. So they worked 
2 CONC all night.  The poor sheep was robbed of all her wool in the middle of winter.
2 CONT
2 CONT When the soldiers returned, two pair of long woolen stockings were ready for them.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucy  Sprague-Tracy died in the home of Thomas Rix Tracy in Symrna, NY on
2 CONT March 6, 1826, age 89 years and 6 months.
2 CONT
2 CONT . Lucy Sprague was born on 28 Jul 1735 in Lebanon, New London Co., CT. (581)(582) (583)(584 ) She died on 6 Mar 1826 in Smyrna, Chenango Co., NY.(585) (586)(587)
2 CONT
2 CONT She was married to Thomas Tracy (son of Jedediah Tracy and Margaret Rix) on 28 Feb 1754 in Sh aron, Litchfield Co., CT.(588) (589)(590) (591) Thomas Tracy was born on 3 Sep 1724 in Presto n, New London Co., CT.(592) (593)(594) (59
2 CONC 5) He died in May 1777 in Lenox, Berkshire Co., MA. (596) (597)(598) (599) He served in the military in Revolutionary War. (600)(601) (602)(603 ) (604)
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Source ===
