1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A Patriot of the American Revolution for CONNECTICUT with the rank of PRIVATE.   [https://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A090926  DAR Ancestor # A090926]
2 CONT
2 CONT * Birth:  12-14-1757    HEBRON HARTFORD CO CONNECTICUT
2 CONT * Death:  4-24-1833     NEW YORK
2 CONT * Pension Number:  S*W15261
2 CONT * Service Source:  S*W15261
2 CONT * Service Description:  1) CAPT HENRY CHAMPION, COL SAMUEL WYLLYS
2 CONT
2 CONT   I have found no information or documentation on Alexander Porter having married twice.  He married Zerviah [Phelps], she is mentioned in his pension records and will.  She died after him.
2 CONT    1.  GENEALOGICAL ABSTRACTS OF REVOLUTIONARY  WAR PENSION FILES. III: N-Z  by Virgil D. White.  The National Historical Publishing Company, Waynesboro, Tennessee 1992. page 2731.  PORTER. Alexander, Zerviah, Connecticut line W15
2 CONC 261, soldier lived at Hebron, Connecticut at end, soldier applied 14 May 1818 Montgomery County, New York aged 61, soldier married Zerviah Phelps 28 April 1783 at Hebron, Connecticut, soldier died 24 Apr 1833, widow died 12 Dec 18
2 CONC 41 at Holland in Erie County, New York aged about 79 leaving children: Amasa who was aged 16 in 1820 and later married  Eliza[--] and in 1851 was living at Sardinis in Erie County, New York; Hannah born 2 May 1790 and she married 
2 CONC  Ely Hyde on 13 February 1806 and in 1851 was living at Farmington in Trumbull Co., Ohio, Arathusa Battles of Mayfield in Cuyahoga County, Ohio in 1851 and Sophronia Corwin of Machines in Cattaraugus County, New York in 1851.
2 CONT    2.  Rejected or Suspended Revolutionary Pensions [SGS] [Dept of Interior] page 182.  Zerviah Porter, widow of Alexander, resident of Freedom, Cattaraugus, New York. For proof that she is still living - marriage and service allo
2 CONC wed. Claim suspended and applied under act of June 7, 1888.
2 CONT    3. The pension list of 1820.  Revolutionary Pension on the roll under law of 18 March 1818.  page 481.  Alexander Porter, private, Connecticut line placed on roll of New York  Generally would-be pensions appeared before a court
2 CONC  of record in the state of his residence and application statement was called "declaration" was certified by the court and forwarded to the official usually, Secretary of War or Commissioner of Pensions responsible for administrat
2 CONC ing the specific act under which the claim was being made.
2 CONT    4.  INDEX of REV WAR PENSIONS APPLICATION = Alexander Porter, Connecticut  Zerviah W15261. Reuben Barber. Connecticut R485.
2 CONT    5.  1790 Census-New York  only Alexander Porter is listed at German Flats, Montgomery County  1 male over 16 - 2 females and 1 male under 16.￼
2 CONT    6.  WILL.  Surrogate Court, Ontario County, New York.  Letters of Administration. Book G page 135.  [SLC #834837].   The People of the State tractive of New York; to Robert Porter and Charles Porter, sons and heirs at law of Al
2 CONC exander Porter late of Naples, Ontario County, deceased.
2 CONT   Whereas Alexander Porter late of the town of Naples in the county of Ontario is alleged, died instate, on or about the twenty fourth day of March A.D. 1841 being the time of his death, goods chattels and credit within this state
2 CONC  by means whereof the granting administration thereon doth appertain to us and you having is applies to our Surrogate of our county of Ontario, for Setters of Administration upon the said goods chattels and credits, and having tak
2 CONC en and subscribed the oath and with your sureties excites the bond requires by the statute in such case make and provided which oath and bond and filed in the Surrogate Office of our sad county of Ontario.  Now therefore, we as by
2 CONC  these proceed depute, constitute and appoint you the said Robert Porter and Charles Porter administrators of all as so  singular the goods, chattels and credits, which were of the said Alexander Porter, decd, hereby granting unto
2 CONC  you full power to administrate and dispose of the said goods, chattels and credits and to do all such other acts and things may of sight be done, in faithfully discharging according to law the auti-opportaining to the trust commi
2 CONC ttee to you by the Setters of Administration. In testimony is thereof, we have cause the seal of office of our said Surrogate to be keep unto office. Witness: Osorn Benjamin, Esquire, Surrogate of Ontario County deigned the sixth 
2 CONC day of July AD 1841 [not our Alexander]
2 CONT    7.  1800 Census-Ontario Co. New York.  Alexander Porter - Middletown. A-2 [Free white males under 10 years of age];  B-2 [Free white males of 10 years and under 16 years];  C-0 [Free White males of 16 years and under 26 years o
2 CONC f age]; D-1[Free white males of 26 years and under 45 years of age];  E-0 [Free white males of 45 years and older];  F-3 [Free white females under 10 years of age]; G-0 [Free white females 10 years and under 16 years of age];  H-1
2 CONC [Free white females of 16 years and under 26 years of age];  J-1[Free white females 26 years and under 45 years of age];  K-0 [Free white females of 45 years and older];  L-0 [All other free persons except Indians not taxed];  N-0
2 CONC  [Slaves].￼  [not our Alexander]
2 CONT    8.  Family Group Sheet-12 Dec 1996. Oakland LDS Library. ￼
2 CONT    9. DAR #566618-Bernice Meyer Pratte Porestall [mother of Virginia].  DAR #675306 by Virginia Pratt Gotham. 1983.  The said Alexander Porter, is the ancestor who assisted in establishing American Independence while acting in the
2 CONC  capacity of private in the 6th Conn. Company. Revolutionary Army.  Drafted 1775 or 1776, served three tours of three months each as private.  Enlisted May 1777, and served three years in Capt. Champion’s Co.  Allowed pension 14 M
2 CONC ay 1818, while living in Montgomery Co., New York.  age 61.  References:  p 446 Memorial Record of the County of Cuyahoga and city of Cleveland, Ohio also in Pioneer Women of Mayfield Township, Cuyahoga Co., Ohio.  Connecticut Men
2 CONC  in the Revolution--W file #15261 pension statement.￼ ￼
2 CONT    10.  States he died in Freedom, Cattaraugus, New York. [not our Alexander]￼
2 CONT    11.  Mary “Polly” was 18 years old when she was married Hoshea Moffet in 1806. She died in 1830 before his will was written, and had moved to Trumbull, Ohio.  That is why she was not mentioned in his will.
2 CONT    12.  Ralph Hutchinson Family [ediecv@yahoo.com]￼
2 CONT    13.  DESCENDANTS OF BENJAMIN BURR. Gives children as - Lucinda, Polly, Hannah, Alexander, Arathusa born 1796, Sophronia, Emilie and Amasa born 1804.  Henry not mentioned.￼
2 CONT    14. Volume 18 page 225.  Hebron Vital Records  PORTER, Alexander son John & Sarah born December 16, 1756 [volume 2 page 224].￼
2 CONT    15.  Genealogical Abstracts of Revolutionary War Pension Files Volume III: N-Z abstracted by Virgil D. White. The National Historical Publishing Company, Waynesboro, Tennessee 1992. page 2731.  PORTER, Alexander, Zerviah, CT Li
2 CONC ne, W15261, sol lived at Hebron CT at enlisted, soldier applied 14 May 1818 Montgomery Co NY ager 61, sol m Zerviah Phelps 28 Apr 1783 at Hebron CT , sol d 24 Apr 1833, widow d 12 Dec 1841 at Holland in Erie Co, NY aged about 79 l
2 CONC eaving children:  Amasa who was aged 16 in 1820 & later m Eliza [--] & in 1851 was living at Farmington in Trumbull County, Ohio, Arathusa Battles of Mayfield in Cuyahoga Co OH in 1851 & Sophronia Cowin of Machias in Cattaraugus C
2 CONC o NY in 1851. 16.    JOHN PORTER AND HIS DESCENDANTS by Arthur Amasa Porter, Portage, Wisconsin1933.  Page 8.  ALEXANDER PORTER [No.6].  Alexander Porter, son of John Porter and Sarah Mack, married Zerviah Phelps, April 28, 1783. 
2 CONC  She was the daughter of Silvanus Phelps and Zerviah Sweetland Phelps.  Their children were being of the 7th generation.
2 CONT   Page 22. Alexander Porter, a great-grandfather of Arthur Amasa, grandfather of John Lorenzo, the father of Amasa, was born December 16, [or 14], 1757, at Hebron, Connecticut and died at Freedom, New York, April 24, 1;833, the so
2 CONC n of John and Sarah Porter.  He enlisted June 11, 1775, in the Sixth Company, “Champions,” 8th regiment, commanded by Capt. Abijah Rowell.  April 28, 1783, he married Zurviah [Sweetland] Phelps; they were married July 16, 1755.  A
2 CONC lexander had eight children:  Lucinda, Polly, Hannah, Alexander, Arathusia, Saphrona, Emilie and Amasa.  Polly married Hoshea Moffett, who had nine children, Edwin, Lucinda who married Calvin Moffett, Adaline, Louisa sager, Orland
2 CONC o, Eratus, Chauncey, Calvin W. and Amanda Sturtevant, of Cleveland, Ohio.  Hannah, born May 2, 1790, died April 9, 1878. She was married to Eli Hyde and had nine children: Abijah, born April 15, 1810, married Eunice Green and had 
2 CONC two children, John H. and Clark, Ruben, Eliza, who was married October 28, 1836, to James Calker; Almyna; Nelson, Sylveter, born May 1, 1;820; Julius, born March 27, 1822, Laura; Harriet, born December 21, 1827, married Francis C.
2 CONC  Heath, June 20, 1857; Alexander, Jr., settled in northern Pennsylvania had a number of children, one being Dr. Abel Porter; Arathusa, born October 4, 1796, died March 18, 1864; was married in October 1817, to Luther Battles and h
2 CONC ad ten children: Zurviah, born December 18, 1818, married James E. Keyt; Edwin D., born July 22, 1820; Mary; Sarah, born December 25, 1823; married John Presby; Luther, born September 29,1826; Newon, born May 10, 1828; Henry D., b
2 CONC orn October 1, 1836; John T. born November 14, 1839; Sophronia married Nathan Corwin, and had five children: Harvey, Mary J. Lingenfelter, Angeline Haws, Charles Corwin and Juliette: Emilie Applebee [Keokuk, Iowa] and Amasa.
2 CONT    18.  JOHN PORTER AND HIS DESCENDANTS by Arthur Amasa Porter, Portage, Wisconsin1933. Page 41.  [LETTER] written by Mrs. Martha Porter Drake.  Rocky Run, Wisconsin  August 25, 1897.  Dear Arthur:
2 CONT   Your letter received last evening.  I remember as a little girl of seeing Grandfather Porter’s old family Bible looking very dilapidated.  About that time father bought a new Bible, and I suppose copied the records from the old 
2 CONC one.  I do not know what became of the old Bible.
2 CONT   The date as I have it, Alexander Porter was born December 14, 1757.  He married Zerviah Phelps on April 28, 1783.  He died April 24, 1833.  MRS. MARTHA PORTER DRAKE.
2 CONT   Page 49-50.  [LETTER] Office of Probate Court, Hebron, Connecticut, October 25, 1897.  Mr. Arthur A. Porter, Portage, Wisconsin.
2 CONT   Sir:  I have looked over the Porter genealogy record at the Town Clerk’s office.  I find that Alexander Porter was born December 16, 1858; was a son of John and Sarah Porter; was married to Zerviah Phelps.  My mother’s mother wa
2 CONC s Olive Phelps, a sister of Zerviah Phelps; she married a Mann; my mother’s name being Fannie Mann.
2 CONT   My great grandfather, Daniel Porter, was born in 1726.  He had a brother John: born April 20, 1723.
2 CONT   Hebron used to be full of Porters and Phelps seventy years back, and before.  We have in the Probate office here, as all in the state have, a history as complete as patient research could make it, made by state authority; edited
2 CONC  and completed in 1889.
2 CONT   This record shows that No. 1 John Porter came from England: No 2, his son, John Porter; No. 3 John’s son, John Porter; No 4, David Porter, son of John Porter; No 5, John Porter, son of David Porter; No 6 Alexander Porter.
2 CONT   Alexander Porter, was a son of John and Sarah Porter, and was born December 14 [0r 16], 1757.  Yours, MARSHALL PORTER Page 56-57.  [LETTER]   Wilsons Mills, May 17, 1897.   I will state to you what I know about my grandfather, A
2 CONC lexander Porter, and his family.  he was a soldier in the Revolutionary war.  He and grandmother lived at my father’s one winter when I was quite young, and I do not remember much about them.
2 CONT   They had seven children that I have heard my mother speak of.  I have see all but two of them--Polly and Sophrona.  I am giving their names in the order of their ages:  Hannah married Eli Hyde, formerly lived in Trumbull County,
2 CONC  Ohio: two of their children live there now.  Polly married Hosea Moffett, formerly lived in the same county as the Hydes did; one of their children lives there now.  Alexander married Clarisa Buckley, formerly lived in the same c
2 CONC ounty in New York where I was born.  The last I knew of them they were in Pennsylvania.  Arathusa, you have her record; Sophrona married Nathan Corwin, and the last I heard of them they were living in Catatangus County, New York. 
2 CONC  Milla married John Applebee, and the last I heard of them they were with the Mormons.  Amasa Porter, your grandfather, I have seen several times.  i was at his place of residence in 1834.  I remember of his have two small boys, S
2 CONC amuel and Lorenzo, but do not remember any other other children..  Yours truly, E. D. BATTLES.
2 CONT   Page 84-85.  DEPARTMENT OF THE INTERIOR BUREAU OF PENSIONS.  Washington, March 22, 1919.  Mr. A. A. Porter., Portage, Wisconsin.  Sir:--In response to your letter dated the 13th inst. you are advised that the papers in the claim
2 CONC  W. File No. 15261 Rev. War show that Alexander Porter was allowed pension on his application executed May 14, 1818, while a resident of Oppenheim, Montgomery County, New York and sixty-one years of age.
2 CONT   It is stated that he was a resident of Hebron, Connecticut where he was drafted and served three months in 1775; and six months in 1776, no officers stated; and that he afterwards enlisted at New London 1 May, 1777, as a private
2 CONC , in Captain Henry Champion’s company, Colonel Samuel Wyllys’ Connecticut regiment, served three years and was discharged May 7, 1780, at Westfield, New Jersey.
2 CONT   Alexander Porter married at Hebron, Connecticut, April 28, 1783, Zerviah Phelps.
2 CONT   In 1820 he resided at Murray, Genesse County, New York, and referred to his son Amasa Porter, aged sixteen years.
2 CONT   Soldier died April 24, 1839, while a resident of Freedom, Cattaraugus County, New York.
2 CONT   Zerviah Porter died December 12, 1841 at Holland, Erie County, New York, and her pension was allowed for the benefit of her only surviving children, viz.
2 CONT   Amasa Porter [and Eliza his wife] of Sardinia, Erie county, New York; Hannah born May 2, 1790. Married February 13, 1806 to Eli Hyde, residents of Farmington, Trumbull County, Ohio; Arathusa Battles of Mayfield, Cuyahoga County,
2 CONC  Ohio; and Sophronia Corwin of Machias, Cattaraugus County, New York.
2 CONT   Page 85.  STATE OF CONNECTICUT.  MILITARY DEPARTMENT, ADJUTANT GENERAL’S OFFICE.  To Whom it May Concern, Hartford, March 24, 1919.  This is to certify that: ALEXANDER PORTER served as a private in 6th Company Captain Abijah Row
2 CONC lee, 8th Regiment, Col. Jedidiah Huntington; enlisted July 11, 1775, discharged December 17, 1775.  Company from Hebron and Killingly.
2 CONT   The 8th Regiment was stationed on the Sound until September 14th when it was ordered to the Boston Camps and took post at Roxbury in General Spencer’s Brigade.
2 CONT   ALEXANDER PORTER served as a private in Capt. Henry Champion’s Company in 3rd Regiment. Col Samuel Wyllys; enlisted Mary 25, 1777, discharged May 26, 1780, residence Hebron.
2 CONT   Connecticut Pensioner in 1918 residing in New York.
2 CONT   In testimony whereof the seal of this office is hereto affixed.  SEAL  signed: EDWARD SCHULZE, Asst. to Adjutant General. WAR DEPARTMENT THE ADJUTANT GENERAL’S OFFICE Washington, March ;8th, 1919.  The records of this office sho
2 CONC w that one Alexander Porter served in the Revolutionary War as a member of Capt. Abijan Rowlee’s company in the 8th Connecticut Regiment [1775].  His name appears only on a single undated roll of that company, which shows that he 
2 CONC enlisted July 11th and marched August 8th and that there was charged for him 14 shillings for billeting.
2 CONT   This records also show that one Alexander Porter served as a private in Capt. Henry Champion’s company [also known as the 3rd company and as Lieut. Col. Thomas Grosvenor’s company] 3rd Connecticut, commanded by Col. Samuel Wylli
2 CONC s Revolutionary Wa.
2 CONT   The collection of Revolutionary War records in this office is far from complete and it is possible that additional information can be obtained from the Adjutant General of Connecticut, Hartford, or from the Commissioner of Pensi
2 CONC ons, Washington, D. C.  signed P. C. HARRIS, The Adjutant General.
2 CONT   Page 89. [Letter] Rochelle, Illinois, 4-28-1897.  To: A. A. Porter, Portage, Wisconsin.  I will cheerfully tell you all I know in regard to our ancestors what little I know my mother told me but I remember perfectly every thing 
2 CONC just as she told me.  Your great grandfather lived when he was married to Zerviah Phelps your great grandmother, near Hartford, Connecticut.  Her parents also lived near there.  I never heard her speak of only one sister that he m
2 CONC other had she was considerable younger than her mother.  Her name was Olive and was quite a noted singer.  Your great grandfather was a revolutionary soldier during the whole war.  I never heard her say anything about his parents 
2 CONC or brothers or sisters.  After they were married some three or four years they moved to Coos county, New Hampshire.  His family consisted of eight children, Lucinda, who lived until she was eighteen years old.  On the second day o
2 CONC f February she was married to Calvin Moffett just two months after she died with Scarlet fiver.  The next child Polly married Hoshea Moffitt and lived unto she was forty-one.  She left nine children all of which are dead except tw
2 CONC o sons.  They live in the west somewhere.  One of them wrote to your aunt, Mrs. Drake, and told where the other lived just lately but I do not remember where it was.  The next was Hannah, my mother, she was born on the 2nd of May,
2 CONC  1790.  She lived until April 9th, 1878, being nearly 88 years old.  She had eleven children.  All are dead except two brothers, one aged 79 years, the other 75 and myself.  The next child was Alexander Jr.  Your father or Mrs. Dr
2 CONC ake told me later of them than I knew before Arthusa married a man by the name of Luther Battles and moved in 1834 from York State to Ohio.  She had ten children.  I think all are dead but two, Sophronia married, lived and died in
2 CONC  York State.  That is about all that I know about her.  Emille married a man by the name of Applebee.  They went to Missouri in 1836.  She died in a year or two after she went there.  Your grandfather was the youngest of the eight
2 CONC  children.
2 CONT   When my mother was ten years old her father, your great grandfather, moved from New Hampshire to Opingham, Herkimer County, New York State on the 13th day of February 1806.  Mrs. Battles lived in Mayfield, Cuyahoga County, Ohio 
2 CONC  [Signed] HARRIET HEATH
2 CONT   Page 92.  CONNECTICUT STATE LIBRARY.  Hartford, March 10, 1919.  To Who It May Concern:
2 CONT   This is to certify that the following is the revolutionary service found under the name of Alexander Porter in the material in the Connecticut State Library.
2 CONT   a. Connecticut Archives Rev. Series 1 xxx; 3e.  List of men in the Connecticut line who received depreciation of wages in 1780 contains name of Alexander Porter in the 3rd regiment.
2 CONT   b.  Connecticut Archives IIC, 49c [Also published in Connecticut Men in the Rev page 88]  
2 CONT   c. Connecticut Archives.  Roll of 6th Company 8th reg’t July 1775 commanded by Capt. Alijah Rowley of Hebron under Col. Jedediah Huntington contains names of Alexander Porter, enlisted July 11, discharged December 17, 1775, in s
2 CONC ervice 5 months, 6 days.  
2 CONT   d.  Connecticut Archives, XI: 443a [Also Connecticut men in the Rev. page 177]  Roll of Capt. Henry Champion’s Company in Col. Samuel Wyllys’ regiment [3rd] contains name of Alexander Porter private, enlisted May 25, 1777 for 3 
2 CONC years. [Connecticut Men adds discharged May 26, 1780].  
2 CONT   e.  Connecticut Archives VI; 172.  Receipt by Capt. Samuel Wyllys’ for arms for select men of Hebron supplied Alexander Porter and others July 8th, 1776.  
2 CONT   f.  Connecticut Archives.  Jonathan Trumbell collection doc. [Also in Connecticut history Col. VIII page 59]  Return of Capt. Henry Champion’s Company in Col. Samuel Wyllys’ regiment contains the name of Alexander Porter, privat
2 CONC e, enlisted for 3 years, hired by Col. Jeremiah Mason. 
2 CONT   g.  Connecticut Archives Rev Series 2, VI: 65.  Account of clothing and supplies furnished Connecticut line in 1780 contains name of Alexander Porter, private, Connecticut men in the Revolution page 643.  
2 CONT   h.  Connecticut pensioners act 1818 residing in New York contains name of Alexander Porter.  Draper’s Index to revolutionary pensioners who served from Connecticut and whose applications are on file in Washington, D. C. gives th
2 CONC e name of Alexander Porter, widow Zerviah. SEAL [Signed] GEO S. GODARD, State Librarian
2 CONT    Page 109-110.  BRING REMAINS HERE FOR BURIAL.  Ashes of Four Members of Porter Family Recovered From Old Graves.  A. A. Porter has just returned from a 2500 mile auto trip into southern New York.  He left Portage on Tuesday, Ju
2 CONC ly 22 and eight days were consumed in the journey.
2 CONT   The purpose of the long drive was to recover the remains of his great grandfather and great grandmother and other relatives buried in abandoned cemeteries in the Empire state whose far away grave had long been neglected.
2 CONT   The ashes of four bodies along with the inscribed headstones marking their last resting place, were brought back to Wisconsin and the remains will be give burial in the Pacific cemetery where repose many members of the Porter fa
2 CONC mily.
2 CONT   The great grandfather, Alexander Porter, was a soldier of the revolution having fought all though the long war of revolt against English domination.  He died and was buried more than ninety-two years ago.  Besides the remains of
2 CONC  Alexander Porter and his wife, those of the mother of the late D. D. Porter, Mrs. Artemesia Porter, first wife of John L. Porter, father of A. A. Porter and those of a sister of John L. Porter, Miss Lovina Porter, were also broug
2 CONC ht back.  Mrs. Artemesia Porter died in early life about a year after her marriage and Miss Lovina Porter was a girl of eighteen when she passed away.
2 CONT   Alexander Porter was buried in Maple Grove cemetery, Town of Freedom, New York.  [Published in the Wisconsin State Register, Portage, Wisconsin, Friday, August 8, 1924.]   [From Oakland, [California] Tribune, August 16, 1925].
2 CONT    19.  1810 U S Federal Census, Number 134. For Oppenheim, Montgomery, New York.  Lists:  Hosea and John Moffett, Alexander Porter, and Israel Bates Sr & Jr.
