1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Hannah Lyman''' (daughter of Richard Lyman II and Hepzibah Ford) was born on 8 July 1660 at Northampton, and died on 11 Oct 1736 at Colchester.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family
2 CONT
2 CONT https://sites.rootsweb.com/~lyman/pomeroy.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Pomeroy and Hannah LYMAN were married on 20 Jun 1677 in Westfield, Massachusetts.  Joseph POMEROY25 146 (Eltweed-1) was born on 20 Jun 1652. He died on 22 Sep 1734. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph POMEROY and Hannah LYMAN had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT *28 i. Joseph POMEROY1147 was born on 1 Sep 1678 in Westfield, Massachusetts. He died on 26 Nov 1678 in Westfield, Massachusetts.
2 CONT *29 ii. Hannah POMEROY147 was born on 13 Dec 1679 in Westfield, Massachusetts. She died on 1 Jul 1680 in Westfield, Massachusetts.
2 CONT *30 iii. Elizabeth POMEROY 147 was born on 7 Feb 1681/82 in Westfield, Massachusetts. She died on 20 Aug 1683 in Westfield, Massachusetts.
2 CONT *+31 iv. Abigail POMEROY (born on 2 May 1683).
2 CONT *32 v. Medad POMEROY147 was born on 4 Nov 1686.
2 CONT *33	vi.	John POMEROY147 died on 8 Feb 1688 in Westfield, Massachusetts. He was born on 11 Jul 1688 in Westfield, Massachusetts. died in a few days
2 CONT *34 vii. Hannah POMEROY147 was born on 22 Apr 1694 in Westfield, Massachusetts.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Silk hood
2 CONT From “ The Hidden Rules of the Puritan Fashion Police:” Slashed sleeves, frizzed hair, or a gold hatband could land you in court. BY SARAH LASKOW, JULY 10, 2017. [https://www.atlasobscura.com/articles/sumptuary-laws-puritan-fashio
2 CONC n-colonies-modesty AtlasObscura.com]
2 CONT
2 CONT > IN 1676, HANNAH LYMAN WAS in trouble. She was among three dozen or so young women who had been summoned to court: They had flouted the laws of the colony of Connecticut by wearing silken hoods. Among these “overdressed” women, L
2 CONC yman was, apparently, the most rebellious and strong-willed. She appeared in court wearing the very silk hood that she had been indicted for donning.
2 CONT
2 CONT >The judge was, predictably, not very happy. He accused her of “wearing silk in a flaunting manner, in an offensive way, not only before but when she stood presented” at court. She and the other young women were fined for their of
2 CONC fensive sartorial choices.
2 CONT
2 CONT >New England’s Puritan colonies had many laws restricting how citizens—particularly women—could dress. “Sumptuary laws” like these weren’t unique to that time or place; they had governed personal behavior as far back as ancient Ro
2 CONC me. Usually sumptuary laws were enacted to control behavior in order to distinguish the high classes from the lower ones, though sometimes they were intended to keep wealthy-enough people from squandering all these resources on fa
2 CONC shionable indulgences. Pirates’ dramatic and colorful style of dressing was, for instance, a deliberate and brazen violation of these laws.  ...
2 CONT
2 CONT > Eventually, even Puritan New England relaxed, kind of, about the way its people were allowed to dress. Although the laws remained on the books, they weren’t always enforced with vigor. This is in part because the punishments did
2 CONC n’t provide much deterrent. Six years after Hannah Lyman flouted the court with her silk hood, another magistrate went after a different group of women for the same offense. But that second time, no one else in the community was i
2 CONC nterested in indicting them. Law or no law, Puritan women were going to indulge in sinful extravagance, at least every once in awhile.
2 CONT
