2 CONT From Donald Porter Email: Donport@coastalnet.com
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Name: NATHANIEL TILDEN
2 CONT Given Name: NATHANIEL
2 CONT Surname: TILDEN
2 CONT Sex: M
2 CONT Birth: in Tenterden, co. Kent, England 1
2 CONT Death: 1641 in Scituate, Plymouth Colony @ age abt. 58
2 CONT LDS Baptism: 28 JUL 1583 Tenterden
2 CONT
2 CONT Note:
2 CONT He was one of the Jurists of Tenterden and served as Mayor in 1622. He
2 CONT emigrated to New England on the ship "Hercules" in the spring of 1635 from
2 CONT Sandwich, England. He was accompanied by his wife, seven children and seven
2 CONT servants. He settled in Scituate, Plymouth Colony.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Note: It is thought by some that Elder Nathaniel may have been in this country prior to 1634, as the earliest record
2 CONT of land to him was in 1628. Still this may have been done by his brother,
2 CONT Thomas, or his friend, Hatherly, who came in the "Ann" in 1623.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Nathaniel Tilden, during the years following his arrival in Scituate, was
2 CONT chosen to be a Ruling Elder of the First Church, which in those days was a
2 CONT great dignity. He was a wealthy man and owned land in Scituate and Marshfield.
2 CONT
2 CONT The earliest record of a conveyance of land in Scituate is dated 1628 and was from
2 CONT Henry Merritt to Nathaniel Tilden and one of his boundaries was on land
2 CONT previously owned by Nathaniel Tilden. In May 1637, with his friend Hatherly,
2 CONT he was appointed by Plymouth Colony to settle the bounds between it and Mass. Bay Colony.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in 1641, the inventory of his estate being of 31 Jul and his will of 25
2 CONT May preceeding in that year. It names his wife, Lydia, that they married in
2 CONT England; gives Tenterden as his home; names his seven children.
2 CONT The historian, Bayly says; "And a division of lands on the Plymouth
2 CONT settlement, made in 1623, Thomas Tilden was allotted shares for three persons.
2 CONT The will of Nathaniel Tilden shows, at the time of his death, in 1641, he
2 CONT still owned his stonehouse, with lands in Tenterden. He brought with him seven servants.
2 CONT
2 CONT Deane's "History of Scituate" says; "The inventory of his estate in the Colony
2 CONT records show he belonged to the wealthiest class of settlers."
2 CONT The Thomas Tilden, who came to Scituate in the "Ann" in 1623, was probably the
2 CONT oldest brother of Nathaniel Tilden but whether he returned to England, or what
2 CONT became of him is unknown. Nathaniel's brother, Joseph, two years younger than
2 CONT himself, was one of the merchant adventurers of London, who associated
2 CONT themselves, and fitted out the "Mayflower" and furnished capital to maintain
2 CONT the infant settlement.
2 CONT
2 CONT Timothy Hatherly, who first came to new England with Nathaniel Tilden's
2 CONT brother, Thomas, in 1623, was another merchant adventurer. He must be
2 CONT considered as the founder of Scituate. He was an assistant from 1634 to 1655,
2 CONT with the exception of the year 1638, when he declined. He was again assistant from 1633 to 1658
2 CONT when he left out for his leniency towards the Quakers. He was also Treasurer
2 CONT of the Colony in 1640; also a Commissioner of the United Colonies in 1645,
2 CONT 1646 & 1650.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Nathaniel Tilden's death, Hatherly married the widow. Having no children
2 CONT of his own, deane's "History of Scituate" says; "He made a bountiful provision
2 CONT for the children of Nathaniel Tilden before his decease, by deeds of gifts. He
2 CONT left the residue of his estate to Deacon Joseph Tilden, the eldest son of
2 CONT Nathaniel Tilden in 1666, and died that same year.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: THOMAS TILDEN b: in Tenterden, co. Kent, England
2 CONT Mother: ALICE BIGGE
2 CONT
