1 NAME John /Washburn/ III
2 GIVN John
2 SURN Washburn
2 NSFX III
2 _MARNM Washburn
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Lot Section A, Grave 58, First Cemetery,   Bridgewater, Plymouth, MA, USA
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1646
2 ADDR
3 CITY Duxbury
3 STAE Massachusetts
1 DEAT
2 DATE ABT 1719
2 ADDR
3 CITY Bridgewater
3 STAE Massachusetts
1 OCCU Sergeant, Farmer
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Descendant of Mayflower passenger Francis Cooke
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT [http://images.google.com/imgres?imgurl=http://homepages.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~legends/washburnjohn3.jpg&imgrefurl=http://homepages.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~legends/washburn.html&usg=__EQOHbCO1zKelQRjh1CsjA0c1XYE=&h=373&w=378&sz=20
2 CONC &hl=en&start=14&um=1&tbnid=01ycPj628jiC7M:&tbnh=120&tbnw=122&prev=/images%3Fq%3Dlapham%2Bcoat%2Bof%2Barms%26hl%3Den%26sa%3DN%26um%3D1   WASHBURN, c.1500-1980]
2 CONT
2 CONT (6) John Washburn, son of John Washburn and Elizabeth Mitchell, was born probably in Duxbury or Bridgewater about 1646 and died in Bridgewater in 1719, aged 73 years. John married in Bridgewater 16 April 1679 Rebecca Lapham, bapti
2 CONC zed in Scituate 15 March 1645, died in Bridgewater about 1717. In a deed dated 1 July 1697, John Washburn of Bridgewater sold land in Bridgewater to Thomas Snell, Sr., mentioning his (John’s) brothers-in-law Joseph and Thomas Laph
2 CONC am.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 28 November 1716, John Washburn gave to his son, John Washburn of Bridgewater, land in Bridgewater, it being part of his "house farm."  On 17 February 1718/19, John Washburn, yoeman, sold to his son John Washburn of Bridgewater
2 CONC , yoeman, his homestead house where he was living and other lands in Bridgewater.  On 6 April 1719, John deeded two acres of land to the " . . . Inhabitants of the Southerly part of Bridgewater . . . to build them a meeting house 
2 CONC on . . . ." He died the same year. No Plymouth County probate record record has been found for John Washburn.  In a deed dated 11 April 1724, acknowledged in June 1731, John and Rebecca’s children, Josiah and William Washburn and 
2 CONC Abigail Leonard quitclaimed to brother John Washburn.  In a deed, dated and acknowledged 5 September 1720, John Washburn is named as "Jr.". Despite the fact that the deed of 5 September 1720 reads John Washburn, Jr., it must refer
2 CONC  to John Washburn, son of John and Rebecca, even though he should at that time have been referring to himself as John Washburn, rather than John Washburn, Jr., since his father had died in 1719.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT # [http://books.google.com/books?id=KIc-AAAAYAAJ&pg=PA339#v=onepage&q&f=false History of the Early Settlement of Bridgewater in Plymouth County] By Nahum Mitchell.  Page 339
2 CONT
2 CONT Links
2 CONT * http://plymouthcolony.net/families/washburn3.html#359
