1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 1.  SOURCES and HISTORY, Genealogies Mayflower Families. Page 141.  Came to Plymouth in 1621 immigrant in ship  Fortune from London, left in August and arrived at Cape Cod November 9. Settled in Plymouth. From 1633
2 CONC . Res at Duxbury.  He was an ironworker and Gunsmith; deputy. Res at Sandwich in 1650 (Plymouth Colony Record). Rem to Bridgewater.  William Bassett (c. 1590–d. 1667) He came to Plymouth on the ship '''Fortune''' arrived on 9 Nove
2 CONC mber 1621, came with wife Elizabeth - listed on the ships Passenger List as William and Elizabeth Bassett.
2 CONT    MAYFLOWER DESCENDANTS: a.  Volume I pg. 151. Division of cattle. b.  Volume 2 pg 3. Vital Records of Marshfield, MA c. Volume 3 pg. 878. He was published with marriage to Mary Butler. d.  Volume 16, The Planters of the Commonwe
2 CONC alth page 50.
2 CONT    According to one author, in records of the time, his last name was apparently also spelled as “Basset”, and he uses Basset for him in his book. Other historical records show his name spelled "Bassett" 2.    Life in New England 
2 CONC - William Bassett arrived in Plymouth in November 1621 on the ship Fortune most probably with his wife Elizabeth, although Banks states he was unmarried upon arrival.
2 CONT    In the Land Division of 1623 he was allotted two acres indicating he had taken a wife before that date. Wife Elizabeth and his children William and Elizabeth were in the 1627 division, but his wife died later.
2 CONT    In Governor William Bradford's Letter Book,  Bassett is one of 27 names of those men who were creditors hoping to realize profit from the colony and who signed an agreement as “Purchasers” to allow privileges to the eight Under
2 CONC takers (colony leaders Bradford, Standish, Isaac Allerton, Edward Winslow, William Brewster, John Howland, John Alden and Thomas Prence) in return for their assumption of the colony debt. Bassett was a signatory on this agreement 
2 CONC as “William Basset”. The Undertakers being colony leaders who assumed the colony debt from the London Adventurers in return for a monopoly of the fur trade.
2 CONT    He moved to Duxbury by 1637, and to Bridgewater by 1656.
2 CONT    He served on a number of juries and committees, and was a deputy for Duxbury to the Plymouth court.
2 CONT    He was a blacksmith, and a number of smith’s tools are listed in his inventory, which also contained a number of theological books.
2 CONT    In 1658, in records of the time, William Bassett is listed as the Constable of Sandwich.
2 CONT    On November 8, 1666 William Bassett, who described himself as a blacksmith of Bridgewater, sold four lots to John Sprague of Duxbury, and Bassett’s wife Mary gave her consent. John Sprague was the husband of Bassett’s daughter 
2 CONC Ruth.
2 CONT     In 1611 he was a widower of Cecily Light, Leiden. [Note: He was born in 1595 and a widower by 1611?] He is shown as a hod-man from Sandwich, Kent in Leiden betrothal to Mary Butler who died before the third banns. Johanna W. T
2 CONC ammel The Pilgrims and other People from the British Isles in Leiden 1576-1640 (Isle of Man: The Mansk-Svenska Publishing Co. Ltd., 1989), 44. He was a Pilgrim from St. Peter’s, Sandwich.  3.  EARLY SETTLEMENT OF BRIDGEWATER page 
2 CONC 10. Original Proprietors of Bridgewater.  Page 26-Permanent settler - Wm Bassett.  Page 27-First freemen in Bridgewater. Page 30-his lot fell out on the north were to begin at the west at the line betwixt Taunton [now Easton] and 
2 CONC Bridgewater, and to run 7 miles east in length and 1 mile in breadth from the north line southward - his name was one of the Men of the North. Page 34 - 1 share. Page 65 - Yr 1686- North side of the Town River lived William & Jose
2 CONC ph Bassett. Page 73-1702 Wm Bassett's house. [Came to Plymouth in 1621. From 1633. Res at Duxbury.  Gunsmith; deputy. Res at Sandwich in 1650 (Plymouth Colony Record). Rem to Bridgewater. Will dated 3 March and probated 5 July1667
2 CONC  4.  Burke's American Families - page 2990.  5.  SEVENTEENTH CENTURY COLONIAL ANCESTORS, National Society Colonial Dames XVII Century 1915-1975. Page 17.  William Bassett [c1600-1667] Massachusetts; married Elizabeth Tilden; Bridg
2 CONC et Cary.  Military service. 6.  Genealogical Notes of Barnstable Families - pages 45-51  Came over on the Fortune in 1621; settled first in Plymouth, then in Duxbury, and finally in Bridgewater--of which town he was an original pr
2 CONC oprietor.  He was comparatively wealthy, being a large land-holder, only 4 in Plymouth paying a higher tax in the yr 1633. He had a large library, was an educated man.  He was a mechanic and planter.  A freeman in 1633.  7.  New E
2 CONC ngland Marriages page 50. (2 marriages listed 1-Elizabeth & 2-Mary) 8.  The Bassett Family Association of America, page 34,  45  Emigrated to New England in the ship "Fortune" in 1621.  [W 444, PCR. L64.  TRB, 1667  M 111 BP].  9.
2 CONC   WILL - Will dated 3 March and probated 5 July 1667. Plymouth Colony Wills and Inventories pages 162-163.  B A S S E T 
