2 CONT Marriage Information:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Peregrine married Sarah BASSETT, daughter of William BASSETT and Elizabeth TILDEN, on 24 Dec 1646 in Marshfield, Plymouth Co., MA. (Sarah BASSETT was born about 1630 in Bridgewater, Plymouth Co., MA and died on 20 Jan 1710/11 
2 CONC in Marshfield, Plymouth Co., MA 2 6.)
2 CONT
2 CONT    Marriage Notes:
2 CONT
2 CONT     On 6 March 1648/49 Peregrine White and his wife Sarah, both of Marshfield, were fined for fornication before marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT First born of the English on board the 'MAYFLOWER' while anchored in Cape Cod Harbor.  His third son, Jonathan, born June 4, 1658 is the ancestor of the White families of yarmouth.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Born on the Mayflower in Provincetown Harbor
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Peregrine White was born 20 November 1620 aboard the Mayflower, docked at Provincetown Harbor, Provincetown, Massachusetts and was the first English child born to the Pilgrims in the New World.
2 CONT
2 CONT His parents, William and Susanna, named him "Peregrine", which means: "one who journeys to foreign lands" or "pilgrim." Soon after the landing, William died, and Susanna married Edward Winslow. Winslow adopted Peregrine and his ol
2 CONC der brother, Resolved, and made them his heirs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Records state that White held some minor civil and military posts. The records also mention that he and his now-wife Sarah were fined "for fornication before marriage or contract." The couple married on 14 December 1646, and had 7
2 CONC  children.
2 CONT
2 CONT White traveled to England with Winslow, but returned to Massachusetts before his death at the age of eighty-three.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peregrine_White
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Peregrine White (November 20, 1620-July 20, 1704) was the first English child born to the Pilgrims in the New World. He was born in Provincetown Harbor to William and Susanna White, before the passengers of the Mayflower had decid
2 CONC ed where they would settle. Aboard the Mayflower with his parents was his older brother Resolved, who was about five at the time. "Peregrine" means "one who journeys to foreign lands," or, more simply, it is a French and Middle En
2 CONC glish word for "pilgrim." Soon after the landing, his father died, and his mother remarried Edward Winslow. It was the first wedding in the New World. Winslow later adopted Peregrine and made him an heir.
2 CONT
2 CONT White's name appears frequently in the records of the colonists. He was an esteemed member of the community as the first child born in New England. He later became a citizen of the settlement of Marshfield, Massachusetts, and held
2 CONC  some minor civil and military offices.
2 CONT
2 CONT White died in 1704 at the age of eighty-three.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Born aboard the Mayflower
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Peregrine White (November 20, 1620-July 20, 1704) was the first English child born to the Pilgrims in the New World. He was born in Provincetown Harbor to William and Susanna White, before the passengers of the Mayflower had decid
2 CONC ed where they would settle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aboard the Mayflower with his parents was his older brother Resolved, who was about five at the time. "Peregrine" means "one who journeys to foreign lands," or, more simply, it is a French and Middle English word for "pilgrim." So
2 CONC on after the landing, his father died, and his mother remarried Edward Winslow. It was the first wedding in the New World. Winslow later adopted Peregrine and made him an heir.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT White's name appears frequently in the records of the colonists. He was an esteemed member of the community as the first child born in New England. He later became a citizen of the settlement of Marshfield, Massachusetts, and held
2 CONC  some minor civil and military offices.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT White returned to England with his stepfather (probably in 1646) and stayed there. His descendents in the UK have a family tree down to the present day that shows the generations in between and a good deal else. Among the families
2 CONC  with whom White's descendents were to form connections are that of John Harrison, winner of the Longitude Prize, and that of Elias Durnford, who laid out Pensacola, and Anthony Durnford, whose death in the Zulu War remains a subj
2 CONC ect of controversy.
2 CONT
2 CONT White died in 1704 at the age of eighty-three.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Mayflower
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT 1620, December 7-10: Born on the Mayflower, Cape Cod Harbor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Residence: Marshfield, MA.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1637- Military Service: Pequot War.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sibling: Resolved born circa 1615.
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 1704, July 20 or 22. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT William White brought his 7-months pregnant wife Susanna, and son Resolved on the Mayflower.  Susanna gave birth to Peregrine onboard the Mayflower in Provincetown Harbor in early December, 1621.  The name Peregrine means wanderer
2 CONC , traveller, or foreigner
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Born on the Mayflower.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT This may be the same Peregrine White who was the first English child born to Pilgrims in the New World.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Peregrine (meaning "wanderer") White was born on the Mayflower at harbor in America on November 20, before the settlement at Plymouth
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Peregrine White (November 20, 1620-July 20, 1704) was the first English child born to the Pilgrims in the New World. He was born in Provincetown Harbor to William and Susanna White, before the passengers of the Mayflower had decid
2 CONC ed where they would settle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aboard the Mayflower with his parents was his older brother Resolved, who was about five at the time. "Peregrine" means "one who journeys to foreign lands," or, more simply, it is a French and Middle English word for "pilgrim." So
2 CONC on after the landing, his father died, and his mother remarried Edward Winslow. It was the first wedding in the New World. Winslow later adopted Peregrine and made him an heir.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT White's name appears frequently in the records of the colonists. He was an esteemed member of the community as the first child born in New England. He later became a citizen of the settlement of Marshfield, Massachusetts, and held
2 CONC  some minor civil and military offices.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT White returned to England with his stepfather (probably in 1646) and stayed there. His descendents in the UK have a family tree down to the present day that shows the generations in between and a good deal else. Among the families
2 CONC  with whom White's descendents were to form connections are that of John Harrison, winner of the Longitude Prize, and that of Elias Durnford, who laid out Pensacola, and Anthony Durnford, whose death in the Zulu War remains a subj
2 CONC ect of controversy.
2 CONT
2 CONT White died in 1704 at the age of eighty-three.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Added by Elwin Nickerson II about My Great Uncle-Peregrine White : His Death
2 CONT
2 CONT [From the Vital Records of Marshfield, Mass.] : "Capt Perigrine White deceas July ye 20: 1704." Mayflower Descendant, Vol. 8, p. 177.
2 CONT
2 CONT [The death of Peregrine White was noted in the Boston News-Letter for Monday 31 July 1704 :] "Marshfield, July 22, Capt. Peregrine White of this Town, Aged Eighty three years, and Eight Months; died the 20th Instant. He was vigoro
2 CONC us and of a comly Aspect to the last; Was the Son of Mr William White and susanna his Wife; born on board the Mayflower, Capt. Jones Commander, in Cape Cod Harbour, November 1620. was the First Englishman born in New-England. Alth
2 CONC o’ he was in the former part of his Life extravagant ; yet was much Reform’d in his last years ; and died hopefully."
2 CONT
2 CONT [The death of Peregrine White’s oldest daughter Sarah White Young was noted in the Boston Weekly News-Letter for Friday 29 August 1755 with reference to Peregrine :] "Saturday August 9th died at Scituate, in the 92d Year of her Ag
2 CONC e, Mrs. Sarah Young, the virtuous Widow of Mr. Thomas Young, and eldest Daughter of That Mr. Peregrine White of Marshfield, who was the First Born English Child in New-England : Being Son of William and Susannah White, born on boa
2 CONC rd the Ship in Cape-Cod Harbour, in the latter Part of Nov. 1620, in which Governor Carver and the Rest of our Plimouth Planters came to New-England, before the Ship left said Harbour and set sail for said Plimouth. Said Peregrine
2 CONC  White liv’d in great Health and Vigour to the 84th Year of his Age, when a Fever carried Him off on July 22, 1704, as our News-Letter soon after inform’d the Publick : And this his Eldest Daughter was Born at Marshfield in Oct. 1
2 CONC 663, enjoy’d her Senses and Health in good measure, till towards her End, and left four Sons surviving. Two observable Instances of the Long Lives of the very first and second Race of Children born in this happy Country." Mayflowe
2 CONC r Descendant, Vol. 24, p. 128-129.
2 CONT
2 CONT another source: 
2 CONT
2 CONT New England Families, Genealogical and Memorial: A Record of the ..., Volume 3
2 CONT  edited by William Richard Cutter 	  	
