2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Brief Life History of Sarah
2 CONT When Sarah Bassett was born about 1682, in Yarmouth, Barnstable, Plymouth Colony, British Colonial America, her father, Nathaniel Bassett I, was 54 and her mother, Dorcas Joyce, was 43. She married John Nickerson on 11 July 1706, 
2 CONC in Harwich, Barnstable, Massachusetts Bay Colony, British Colonial America. They were the parents of at least 3 sons and 1 daughter. She died after 1747, in Ridgefield, Fairfield, Connecticut, United States.
2 CONT MORE
2 CONT Sources (1)
2 CONT Records
2 CONT VIEW ALL
2 CONT World Events (1)
2 CONT 1708
2 CONT Age 27
2 CONT Ridgefield was first settled by English colonists. It was founded in 1708 when 24 English families from Long Island Sound, taken from Norwalk and Milford . Settlements bought these uplands from the Ramapoo Indians.
2 CONT Name Meaning
2 CONT
2 CONT Bassett
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah
2 CONT English (of Norman origin): from Old French basset ‘of low stature’, a diminutive of basse ‘low, short’, either a nickname for a short person or a status name for someone of humble origins.
2 CONT Altered form of French Bessette 1 or Besset (see Bessette 2).
2 CONT History: William Bassett (c. 1598–1667) came to Plymouth, MA, from Kent, England, in the 1620s; c. 1650 he moved to Duxbury and subesequently to Bridgewater. He had many prominent descendants, among them one of the earliest famili
2 CONC es on Martha's Vineyard. — The surname Bassett of French origin (see 2 above) is listed in the register of Huguenot ancestors recognized by the Huguenot Society of America.
