1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT JOHN, only son of Pilgrim Francis Sprague, was born. say 1630 to his 2nd wife. He married by about 1655 Ruth Bassett, daughter of WILLIAM BASSETT (on 8 June 1655 "we present John Sprague and Ruth Bassett, of Duxburrow, for fornica
2 CONC tion before they were married [PCR 3:82]). (John Sprague is treated in detail by Maclean W. McLean, who estimates his birth as about 1635, whereas we make him slightly older [TAG 41:178-81].)
2 CONT
2 CONT John and Ruth's children:
2 CONT * 1. Lt. John Sprague (Jr.), born abt 1656 in Duxbury, Plymouth Co., MA and died March 1728 in Lebanon, New London Co., CT at age 72 years. He married 1st Lydia and 2nd Lois Standish 
2 CONT * 2. Elizabeth Sprague abt 1657 in Marshfield, Plymouth Co., MA and died May 27, 1727 in Plympton, Plymouth Co., MA. She married George Sampson abt 1678 in Plympton, MA.
2 CONT * 3. Ruth Sprague born February 12, 1660 in Marshfield, Plymouth Co, MA and died 1743 in Duxbury, Plymouth Co., MA at age 82. She married August 12, 1680 in Dartmouth, Bristol Co., MA. She married Eleazer (Elizer) Smith.
2 CONT * 4. William Sprague born 1658 in Duxbury, MA and died November 25, 1712 in Duxbury, MA at age 48. He married Grace Wadsworth January 1700 in Duxbury, Plymouth Co., MA. William died by drowning - his whaling ship went down.
2 CONT * 5. Dorcas Sprague was born abt 1666 in Duxbury, MA and died January 1710 at age 44. She married January 10, 1710 in Duxbury, Plymouth Co., MA Joseph Hatch born May 7, 1654 in Falmouth, Barnstable Co., MA (son of Jonathan Hatch a
2 CONC nd Sara Rowley) and died February 16, 1734..
2 CONT * 6. Desire Sprague was born abt 1668 in Marshfield, Plymouth Co., MA and married November 24, 1696 in Falmouth/Sandwich, Barnstable Co., MA John Gifford.
2 CONT * 7. Lt. Samuel Sprague born abt 1669 in Duxbury, Plymouth Co., MA and died July 25, 1740 in Rochester, Plymouth Co., MA at age 71. He married November 29, 1695 in Duxbury, Plymouth Co., MA Ruth Alden.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/52429539/john-sprague
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://familysearch.org/tree/person/LZVN-R56/details
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes for JOHN SPRAGUE: 
2 CONT
2 CONT The book, "Saints and Strangers", by George Willison says of John:  "(He) was a spark of the old flint, it appears, and spent hours in the stocks for highly misdemeaning himself in the house of James Cole of Plymouth, near unto or
2 CONC  on the evening before the Sabbath Day, in drinking, gameing, and uncivil revelling, to the dishonor of God and the offense of this government, by his gameing and bringing of his mare uncivilly into the parlour of James Cole afore
2 CONC said." 
2 CONT
2 CONT An article in the "American Genealogist", vo. 41-3, by Maclean W. McLean of Pittsburgh, PA, continues to quote the record of John Sprague's offense:  "for which midedmeanors hee was sentence to sitt in the stockes, two houres whic
2 CONC h accordingly was performed; and for his being there the greatest part of the afternoon unto the evening, hee (was fined) 5s." 
2 CONT
2 CONT The book, "One Bassett Family in America", by Buell Burdett Bassette says that John & Ruth lived in Marshfield, MA.  On June 8, 1655, they were both presented to Court (The reason is not given.). 
2 CONT
2 CONT McLean's article asserts that Winston Churchill can be counted among the descendants of John Sprague.  McLean gives further information about John Sprague:  A Plymouth Colony deed, dated 27 April 1661, from Francis Sprague to his 
2 CONC son John says:  "my dwelling house at Duxbury", but with the provision that John was "not to enter into possession thereof until after the decease" of the father.  In another deed, dated 3 May 1664, John Sprague was granted also h
2 CONC is father's land at Namskakett (Middlebourgh).  John succeeded his father in the business of keeping the tavern in 1669.  John served on a coroner's jury 8 June 1673. 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Sprague genealogy, John was a Counsellor of Sir Edmiund Andros.  John lost his life in Pierce's fierce fight at Pawtucket in King Philip's War.  His estate was appraised in 1675, and was sworn to by his widow Ruth
2 CONC . 
2 CONT
