2 CONT Extensive article on John Sprague.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Keith Kingman.
2 CONT
2 CONT John and Ruth (Bassett) Sprague first lived in Duxbury. Then before 1649, they moved to Marshfield, MA (north of Plymouth) where their third child, Ruth, was born. They then moved back to Duxbury where in 1670, John succeeded to h
2 CONC is father's business of running an "Ordinary" (tavern). 
2 CONT
2 CONT From Sue Bates, correspondent.
2 CONT
2 CONT The son of Francis and Anne Sprague, John Sprague was born about 1637, probably in Duxbury, Massachusetts. In 1655 he married Ruth Basset whose father, William Basset (born about 1590 in England) had arrived in America in 1621 abo
2 CONC ard the ship FORTUNE (Thomas Barton, Master). 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Sprague and his wife Ruth Basset lived in Marshfield, Massachusetts for a number of years before settling at Duxbury around 1668. Shortly after their arrival in Duxbury, he became co-proprietor of his father's tavern in Duxbu
2 CONC ry and remained such until his death. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Sprague apparently inherited his father's ardent temperament. Described as a "...spark off the old flint", he is known to have spent several hours in the stocks on at least one occasion for "...highly misdemeaning himself in 
2 CONC the house of James Cole of Plymouth near unto or on the evening before the Sabbath Day, in drinking, gaming and uncivil reveling, to the dishonor of God and the offense of the government, by his gaming and the bringing of his mare
2 CONC  uncivily into the parlor of James Cole, aforesaid." 
2 CONT
2 CONT It is believed that he was the John Sprague who was a counselor to Sir Edmund Andros, rather than the John Sprague who was the son of William Sprague. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Sprague was killed in the massacre of Captain Michael Pierce's Company of English Militia during the King Philip's War when, on 26 March 1676, that company of 65 men (supplemented by about 20 friendly Indians) engaged a super
2 CONC ior force of hostiles near the Pawtucket River in Rhode Island, about 5 miles north of Providence. 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Douglas Edward Leach in his history of that war entitled FLINTLOCK AND TOMAHAWK - NEW ENGLAND IN THE KING PHILIP'S WAR, Captain Pierce, having determined that there was a band of hostile Indians located near the Pawtu
2 CONC cket River, had prepared his men for battle and had sent a messenger into the nearby town of Providence requesting reinforcements before attacking. 
2 CONT
2 CONT For some reason this messenger, arriving at the time of public worship, chose to wait until after the service had concluded before delivering Captain Pierce's request. When the situation was made known, Captain Andrew Edmunds of t
2 CONC he Providence Militia immediately set out with a group of armed men in order to join forces with Pierce's company. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile, the Plymouth Militia group had unwittingly engaged and become surrounded by an extremely large force of hostile Narrangansett Indians and were overwhelmed. By the time Edmunds and his men arrived, it was too late. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The fact that some 42 of the 55 colonists killed that day were buried at the site of the battle, including that of John Sprague of Duxbury, indicates that there were some survivors, or it may indicate that there were bodies which 
2 CONC may not have been recovered. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From Joe, a correspondent, note of June 9th, 2006
2 CONT
2 CONT In your profile of John Sprague, Sr., you state that Sprague died on March 26, 1676 in the "Swamp Fight" of King Philip's War. Then you go on to describe action associated with the battle known as "Pierce's Fight." 
2 CONT
2 CONT The "Swamp Fight" and "Pierce's Fight" were two related however separate incidents. The former occurred on December 19, 1675 in a swamp of Kingston, Rhode Island, while the latter was on March 26, 1676, on the banks of the Blackst
2 CONC one River in what is now Central Falls, Rhode Island. 
2 CONT
2 CONT I am presently writing an longish article about Pierce's Fight.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Mary Longstreth, correspondent, note of July 1st, 2008 quoting from prior correspondence.
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE: The following is not proven to be in anyway connected with this Sprague family member:
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Longstreth on 6/27/2008: I am looking for confirmation of which Captain Sprague was responsible for the transport of Native Americans prisoners from King Phillip's War as slaves. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Response from Dick Weber dated June 30, 2008: As to your question on "Capt. Sprague" I'm afraid I do not know. There were seven Spragues for whom I have a record of participation in King Philip's War. Only one of them in my record
2 CONC s had a title of Capt. I've attached a report on Capt. John Sprague and as you will see it shows evidence of his service but no indication of any role with transporting prisoners. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary's response dated July 1, 2008: You may want to read the book, "Mayflower" by Nathaniel Philbrick. This is where I found the reference. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dick's response dated July 1, 2008: As the participation in the Sprague Project increases each year I'm more and more dependent on others to share the results of their research for inclusion in the Sprague family history. If there
2 CONC  is any short quote you can provide from "Mayflower" please do so. 
2 CONT
2 CONT I find it interesting that the Spragues would show up in a book on Mayflower descendants since there were not Spragues on the Mayflower. They did of course within a couple generation start marrying descendants 
2 CONT
2 CONT of the Mayflower immigrants.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary's response dated July 1st, 2008: From the Epilogue, Page 345:
2 CONT
2 CONT "As early as the fall of 1675, they had begun to sail from the coast of New England: the slave ships. It began in September when a Captain Sprague departed from Plymouth with 78 Indians. By July of 1676, Plymouth had formalized th
2 CONC e process of removing potentially dangerous Native men and boys.......The English were not so sure what to do with Phillip's 9 year old son.....In the end, Phillip's son, like his mother before him was shipped off as a slave."The 
2 CONC premise is that the action of shipping the Native Americans as slaves initiated slavery in America. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The book does not maintain that Spragues were on the Mayflower. It's a very interesting discourse on King Phillip's War and the relationship between Atherton and Church
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Died in Pawtucket, Rhode Island while fighting in King Phillip's war.
