1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Not the same as https://www.geni.com/people/John-Eames-Sr/6000000003147030812?through=6000000002385340221
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===John Ames  (1647 - abt. 1725)
2 CONT * Born 24 May 1647 in Braintree, Suffolk, Massachusetts Bay Colony
2 CONT * Died about 1 Mar 1725 at about age 77 in Bridgewater, Plymouth, Massachusetts Bay Colony
2 CONT * Son of William Ames and Hannah (Unknown) Niles
2 CONT * Brother of Hannah (Ames) Hayden, Rebecca Ames, Liddia Ames, Sarah (Ames) Hayward and Deliverance (Ames) Leonard
2 CONT * Husband of Sarah (Willis) Ames — married 1 Mar 1672 in Bridgewater, Massachusetts
2 CONT * Father of John Ames, William Ames III, Nathaniel Ames, Elizabeth (Ames) Field, Thomas Ames II, Sarah (Ames) Field, David Ames and Hannah (Ames) Packard
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT JOHN, Bridgewater, only s. of William of Braintree, m. Sarah, d. of John Willis, had John, b. 14 Apr. 1672; William, 6 Nov. 1673; Nathaniel, 9 Oct. 1677; Eliz. 6 Sept. 1680; Thomas, 21 Feb. 1682 ; Sarah, 12 Oct. 1685; David, 30 Au
2 CONC g. 1688; and Hannah
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Ames-126
2 CONT
2 CONT In Bridgewater, John, the son of William Ames was known as John Jr., likely to distinguish him from his uncle John Ames who may also have become his guardian after William's death. John Jr. was his Uncle John's heir.
2 CONT
2 CONT His uncle, John Ames, had no children, but he had a large estate which he gave by deeds to his nephew John, "son of his brother William of Braintree, and to the sons of his said nephew."
2 CONT
2 CONT John came to Plymouth, MA in 1626. John removed to Bridgewater, MA and died without issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT Two brothers, William and John, sons of John and Christian (Browne) Ames, were the originators of about half of all persons in the United States with the surname of Ames. Their arrival to the New World has not actually been ascert
2 CONC ained. Many historians have given their immigration to New England in 1634/5, aboard the ship "Hercules". The passenger list for the Hercules, John Kiddy as the master, porting in Massachusetts the summer of 1635, does not include
2 CONC  the brothers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth
2 CONT
2 CONT 24 MAR 1647 West Bridgewater, Massachusetts (Plymouth)
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife: Sarah Willis Marriage 1 Mar 1672 Bridgewater, Plymouth, Massachusetts
2 CONT
2 CONT # Child: John Ames
2 CONT # Child: William Ames
2 CONT # Child: Nathaniel Ames
2 CONT # Child: Elizabeth Ames
2 CONT # Child: Thomas William Ames
2 CONT # Child: Sarah Ames
2 CONT # Child: David Ames
2 CONT # Child: Hannah Ames
2 CONT
2 CONT Probate
2 CONT
2 CONT He died intestate and on 8 July 1726, his son John was granted the letter of administration for his estate.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT His inventory was taken on 13 July 1726 in Bridgewater.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1726, his estate settlement mentions his children, John, William, Nathaniel, Thomas, David, Elizabeth Field, wife of Capt John Field, and Hannah Packard, wife of David Packard.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT John Ames was the only son of William, who had 7 (?) children.
2 CONT
2 CONT John was distinguished in King Phillip's War.
2 CONT
2 CONT From  Dale H. Cook, Member, NEHGS and MA Society of Mayflower Descendants; Plymouth Co. MA Coordinator for the USGenWeb Project http://members.cox.net/plymouthcolony/index.shtml 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Savage (pg.49) states: “John was the only son of William Ames.” 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Ames families in Bridgewater and Easton are one family, descended from John and Cyprian (Browne) Ames of Bruton, Somerset, England. John and Cyprian's son William and and his wife Hannah (___) settled in Braintree about 1638. 
2 CONC Their son John was born in Braintree and removed to Bridgewater by 1672. The line to the renowned Ames family of Easton is William, John, Thomas, Thomas, John, Oliver. Oliver removed from Bridgewater to Easton by 1803, when he fou
2 CONC nded the shovel manufacturing business that grew and prospered under his sons Oliver Jr. and Oakes.
2 CONT
2 CONT John and Cyprian's son John came to Duxbury by 1643 and married Elizabeth Hayward about 1645. He was an original proprietor of Bridgewater and removed there in 1656. He had no children and left his large estate to his brother Will
2 CONC iam's son John and to the sons of John.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Marshfield Eames family is a completely different family. The immigrant founders were Anthony Eames and his wife Margery (___), who probably came from Dorsetshire, England. In later years (by about 1800) some members of this f
2 CONC amily spelled their surname "Ames".
2 CONT
2 CONT South Easton is a village in Easton, which is in Bristol County. Norton was the first town formed in the Taunton North Purchase, and from it and the unincorporated land in the Purchase were formed  Easton and Mansfield. Most of th
2 CONC e early Easton families were originally either Taunton families or Bridgewater families. Cape Cod is Barnstable County.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
