2 CONT
2 CONT 8. Jonathan (s. of John 2) m. Mary, D. of George Vaughn of Mid'o. about 1683, and had Elizabeth 1684, Josiah 1686, Benjamin 1688, Ebenezer 1690, Martha 1692, Joanna 1693, Nathan 1699, Jonathan 1700, Cornelius 1702.—Elizabeth m. Jo
2 CONC hn Benson 1710.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes from Francis Cooke of the Mayflower First Five Generations by Ralph V. Woods, Jr., Piction Press 1996
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://archiver.rootsweb.ancestry.com/th/read/WASHBURN/2000-06/0961812921
2 CONT
2 CONT The parentage of Jonathan Washburn is established by: (a) his father's will, dated 1686, which names son Jonathan, (b) his brother Benjamin's will, dated 1690, naming brother Jonathan, and (c) a deed, dated 1694, from Jonathan Was
2 CONC hburn et al to Samuel Washburn, selling land "left unwilled by our Father & father in law John Washburne deceased..." No marriage record has been found for Jonathan Washburn and Mary Vaughan. However, the will of George Vaughan, d
2 CONC ated 30 June 1697, names his daughter Mary Washburn. In a deed, dated 25 October 1697, Jonathan Washburn of Bridgewater, with the consent of his wife Mary, sold a house and land in Middleborough which had been conveyed to Mary by 
2 CONC the will of her father, George Vaughan. On 10 January 1725, Benjamin Washbourn of Bridgewater was named administrator on the estate of his father Jonathan Washbourn late of Bridgewater. On the same day, Benjamin Washbourn, with Th
2 CONC omas Snell of Marshfield and Amos Snell of Bridgewater as sureties, posted bond. On 20 June 1727, Mrs. Mary Washburn, widow, was appointed administratrix of the estate of her husband Jonathan Washburn of Taunton, deceased. Apparen
2 CONC tly this Washburn family lived in the Bridgewater/Taunton area. Possibly Jonathan owned land in both towns, which are in separate counties.
2 CONT
2 CONT Generation 5 = Josiah WASHBURN was married first to Elizabeth DAVENPORT, & second to Sarah Richmond.
2 CONT
