1 NOTE
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert OLIVER and his wife, Mary WALKER were cousins. Robert's father, Lancelot OLIVER m. Mary, sister to William WALKER.
2 CONT *****Washington County Public Library; the book "Washington County, Ohio to 1980", page 265; by Bernice (Oliver) Wagner.*****
2 CONT
2 CONT @Jack Ross, Internet contact and descendent.
2 CONT
2 CONT *****The book "Wolf Creek and the Muskingum", page232.*****
2 CONT
2 CONT *****The book "History of Washington County, Ohio", pages 55, 362, 419, 461, 546.*****
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 1738- Born in the north of Ireland. Parents moved to America while Robert was young, and settled on a farm in Worcester County; Rutland, Massachusetts. ***Birth - 1739 - validated by Latter Day Saints records; film #1985335. ***
2 CONT
2 CONT His education was as good as the schools of that period afforded.
2 CONT
2 CONT %Early Massachusetts Marriages Prior to 1800 - Worchester County, Worchester; page 9: Robert Oliver of Rutland, married Mary Walker, October 13, 1763.
2 CONT
2 CONT He entered the Revolution as a lieutenant, but at the close of the war had advanced to the position of colonel.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the close of the war in the campaign against Burgoyne, he again settled on a farm in Belpre, Ohio where he lived quietly until 1786, when he volunteered to assist in putting down Shay's rebellion.
2 CONT
2 CONT Summer of 1788 came to Marietta, Ohio (Washington County) and settled with his family in the Campus Martius stockade.
2 CONT
2 CONT March 17th, 1789 was elected as a Police Commissioner for Marietta, Ohio.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1789 - Built in partnership with 2 others,  Major Haffield White and Captain John Dodge > the first water operated grist mill in Ohio. Located on Wolf Creek at the new settlement of Waterford. (At that time referred to as MILLSBUR
2 CONC GH)
2 CONT
2 CONT May 10, 1792 was issued 3 patents of Land in relation to the OHIO LAND SURVEY COMPANY.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1792 - became high priest of AMERICAN UNION LODGE #1 of Marietta, Ohio; a military lodge of the MASONIC ORDER.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1799 - 1803 Senator of the TERRITORIAL LEGISLATURE. One of five men commissioned by President of the United States, John Adams.
2 CONT % Biographical and Historical Memoirs of the early Pioneer Settlers of Ohio - by S.P. Hildreth, M.D. / Cincinnati: H.W. Derby & Company, Publishers (1852)
2 CONT
2 CONT November 23, 1801 chosen as President of the Legislative Council held at the town of Chillicothe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Justice of the Peace in Waterford settlement until his death in May 1810. (Age 72)
2 CONT
2 CONT % Biographical and Historical Memoirs of the early Pioneer Settlers of Ohio - by S.P. Hildreth, M.D. / Cincinnati: H.W. Derby & Company, Publishers (1852)
2 CONT ....."Col. Oliver possessed a clear, discriminating mind, and was truly dignified in his manners; had a perfect command of his passions, and was very amiable.. with his associates. He had a good fund of anecdote, which he related 
2 CONC in a very interesting manner." - Page 394.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5ft. 10, stoutly built, and commanding appearance; face full, mild, and bland, with a pleasant expression when in conversation with friends, but severe and terrible to the vicious and undeserving. His head was finely formed, but e
2 CONC arly became bald. - Page 395.
2 CONT
2 CONT "In early life he became a professor of religion, .....and at the formation of the first Congregational Church in Marietta, Ohio, in 1796, he was a member." - Page 395.
2 CONT
2 CONT -Claims to be buried at Cedar Hill Cemetery - Waterford Township; Ohio (Washington County)
2 CONT
2 CONT Washington County, Ohio to 1980; page 265.
2 CONT
2 CONT @Jack Ross, Internet contact and descendent.
