1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A Patriot of the American Revolution for MASSACHUSETTS with the rank of LIEUTENANT.   [https://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A086124  DAR Ancestor #: A086124]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Alexander Oliver was born in Sherborn, Massachusetts, to Launcelot Oliver and Mary Walker. Before the war he married Mary Warner, third cousin of Seth Warner[citation needed] and seventh cousin of George Washington[citation needed
2 CONC ]. His decision to fight against the British was very much disliked by part of his family, mainly because his second cousin was Andrew Oliver, a Massachusetts politician who was commissioned to enforce the Stamp Act in Massachuset
2 CONC ts, and another cousin more distant this time Thomas Oliver would become Lieutenant Governor of Massachusetts. He was made a corporal of 13th Massachusetts Regiment raised by Colonel Samuel Brewer, while in the 13th he fought at t
2 CONC he Battle of Bunker Hill, Battle of Valcour Island and Battle of Saratoga where he led a group of men to capture general John Burgoyne. Then he was Regimental Quartermaster of Jeduthan Baldwin's Artillery Artificer Regiment,[1] th
2 CONC en an Ensign or Second lieutenant in the 5th Massachusetts Regiment, then a Lieutenant, and then a Colonel for Massachusetts. Alexander Oliver was in charge of recruiting men to fight all around Massachusetts. Oliver was a partici
2 CONC pant in the Boston Tea Party. Alexander was also in George Washington's staff at Dorchester Heights and was near him as they marched into Boston. Oliver and major Nathan Goodale were sent with an exploring committee down the Ohio 
2 CONC River into the Kanawha River for about six weeks to find out as much information as possible on the area, traveling through present day West Virginia, Virginia, and North Carolina on the way  After arriving in Ohio Oliver helped f
2 CONC ound Belpre, Ohio along with thirteen other Revolutionary War veterans. After leaving Belpre he moved to Point Pleasant, Ohio where he later died.                                             
2 CONT
2 CONT He was also the third great grandson of Thomas Oliver one of the first Olivers in the Colonies r arriving in Ohio Oliver helped found Belpre, Ohio along with thirteen other Revolutionary War veterans. After leaving Belpre he moved
2 CONC  to Point Pleasant, Ohio where he later died.  itations[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations: Daughters of the American Revolution's "Lineage Book Volume 41" (1928) Pg. 244 Gerald Faulkner Shepard's "The Shepard Families of New England" (1973) Pg. 125 Egbert Cleave's "Cleave's Biographical Cyclopaedia of Homeopa
2 CONC thic Physicians and Surgeons" (1873) Pg. 345 William Thomas Roberts Saffell's "Records of the Revolutionary War: Containing the Military and Financial Correspondence of Distinguished Officers" (1894) Pg. 429 Samuel Crocker Cobb's 
2 CONC "An Historical Address Delivered on the Occasion of the Centennial Celebration at Boston, Massachusetts" (1883) Pg. 45 Cornelius Evarts Dickinson and Samuel Prescott Hildreth's "A History of Belpre, Washington County, Ohio" (1920)
2 CONC  Pg. 191     
2 CONT
2 CONT Col. Alexander wes  also the third great grandson of Thomas Oliver one of the first Olivers in the Colonies and a Ruling Elder of the Church of Boston.  (Source Wikipedea)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/18218503/alexander-oliver
2 CONT
2 CONT Col Alexander Oliver, Sr BIRTH	30 Nov 1744 Sherborn, Middlesex County, Massachusetts, USA DEATH	2 Sep 1828 (aged 83) Point Pleasant, Clermont County, Ohio, USA BURIAL	 Spring Grove Cemetery Cincinnati, Hamilton County, Ohio, USA  
2 CONC Show Map PLOT	15075=interment id springgrove.org Garden LN, Section 22, Lot 34, Space 7 MEMORIAL ID	18218503 · View Source
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander was the son of Lanslet/Lanslot/Lancelot Oliver (1704-6/24/1781) and his wife Mary Walker (1706-8/1788). They married in 1730. Alexander's father (and perhaps his mother, too) emigrated from the Ulster province, Ireland, 
2 CONC to Boston, MA, and then to Barre, MA.
2 CONT
2 CONT 8 OLIVER siblings.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander Oliver's Biography - LDS: Corporal - Brewer's Massachusetts Regiment (1775) Regiment Quarter Master - Baldwin's Artillery (1777) Sergeant - In Col. David Field's Fifth Reg., Hampshire County,Massachusetts (1779) Ensign -
2 CONC  (1781-1783) Lieutenant - Revolutionary War.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander married Miriam Mary "Mary" Warner (7/21/1743/50-9/1/1808) in Conway, Franklin, MA, on 2/4/1771. Miriam Mary (Warner) Oliver: 131351952. Linked
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander and Miriam were among the second wave of pioneers to settle in Ohio in 1788.
2 CONT
2 CONT "There were eleven [twelve] children, all of whom settled in Ohio. Two of Alexander [and Miriam's] sons [Alexander Jr. and Andrew] became ministers; David became a physician in western Ohio; Lancelot and John were the other sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT "The daughters were: Lucreta (sic: Lucretia), wife of Levi Munsel (sic: Munsell), who lived several years in Marietta; Betsy (sic: Elizabeth), married Hon. Daniel Symmes, of Cincinnati; Sally, married Major (_) Austin, United Stat
2 CONC es army, and settled in Cincinnati; Lucreata (sic: Lucinda), wife of George Putnam, son of Colonel Israel Putnam; Mehala (sic: Mahala), married Calvin Shepherd, of Marietta: Electa, wife of O.M. Spencer, of Cincinnati; and Mary, m
2 CONC arried Oliver Wing, who settled in Washington Co. During the Indian war the family took shelter in Farmer's castle."
2 CONT
2 CONT 1800 US Census, Adams Twp., Washington Co., OH: Alexander Oliver son Lancelot Oliver are listed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Linking in progress.
