1 OCCU Shipmaster, Rear Adm, Thomas was a mariner. For some years he commanded a vessel running between Massachusetts and ports in England. He captured a Dutch ship in the British Channel and was appointed "Captain" in 1632.
1 TITL Rear Adm.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Rear Adm. Thomas Graves
2 CONT * BIRTH 6 Jun 1605 Ratcliff, London Borough of Tower Hamlets, Greater London, England
2 CONT * DEATH 31 Jul 1653 (aged 48) Boston, Suffolk County, Massachusetts, USA
2 CONT * BURIAL Buried or Lost at Sea. Specifically: Charlestown Suffolk County Massachusetts (At Bay)
2 CONT * MEMORIAL ID 73089718 ·Photos by Craig & Ro§e❈
2 CONT * Son of John Graves Sr. (Mayor of Hull) and Sarah (Malter) Graves 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Biography==
2 CONT •~••~════~••~════~••~════~••~•
2 CONT Captain Thomas Graves was born on 6 June 1605 at Ratcliff, England, near London (or at Stepney, England. He was baptized 16 June 1605 at Stepney, part of the city of London, and died 31 July 1653 (old style calendar, 31st day, 5th
2 CONC  month, 1653) in a sea battle.. In 1630 in England he married Katherine Gray (1605-1683). They came to live in Charlestown, Massachusetts circa 1636. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas was a mariner, working as a Shipmaster, Captain and Rear Admiral. For some years he commanded a vessel running between Massachusetts and ports in England. He captured a Dutch ship in the British Channel and was appointed "C
2 CONC aptain" in 1632. "Captain" Thomas Graves died in 1653 in Charlestown, Massachusetts. CHILD: Rebecca (Graves) Adams (1631-1664) Married: Samuel Adams (1616-1688) in Charlestown, Massachusetts in 1653.
2 CONT
2 CONT Captained frequent voyages across the Atlantic on ships "Reformation", "Desire", "Adventure", and "Talbot" bringing passengers from England. He settled in Charlestown in 1639 but returned to England to participate in the Civil War
2 CONC * in the British Navy, where he attained the rank of Rear Admiral and was the first Admiral at Charlestown, MA. He was killed in action in a battle with the Dutch.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Graves was born 6 June 1605 at Ratcliff, England, near London (or at Stepney, England), was baptized 16 June 1605 at Stepney, part of the city of London, and died 31 July 1653 (old style calendar, 31st day, 5th month, 1653)
2 CONC  in a sea battle with the Dutch in the English Channel. A number of sources say he died in Charlestown, Massachusetts.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Katherine (or Katharine or Catharine) Gray before 1635 (probably by 1630) in England. She was born about 1606 in Harwich, England, died on 21 Feb. 1682 old style calendar, 21, 12, 1681, and was buried 23 Feb. 1681/2. Sh
2 CONC e was a daughter of Thomas Gray and Katherine Myles (daughter and coheir of Robert Myles, of Sutton, in County Suffolk. Thomas Gray died in 1607, and his widow married on 23 Dec. 1610 at Harwick, England, Rowland Coytemore, of Wap
2 CONC ping next to London, in Middlesex, a widower, who died in 1626; she came to New England in 1636 or 1637 and settled in Charlestown, Mass.; was admitted to the church in 1638, and died 28 November 1659. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Her will, dated 28 or 30 April 1658 named 14 grandchildren, including the 5 children of her daughter Katherine. An article in the NEHGS Register gives much more information on the Myles family, and also says that Katherine (Myles)
2 CONC  Gray had 6 children.
2 CONT Katherine Coytemore, mother of Thomas Graves' wife, with her son Thomas and wife and her daughter Elizabeth, also came to America, probably at the same time as the family of Thomas Graves.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Chronology== 
2 CONT 1629  Arrival on the "George Bonaventure" Salem, Massachusetts, USA
2 CONT 1630 Mate of the "Arabella", Emigration 
2 CONT 1630 Master of the "Plantation"
2 CONT 1631-Master of the "Plough"
2 CONT 1632-Master of the "Whale"
2 CONT 1632 appointed "Captain" after capturing a Dutch ship in the British Channel 
2 CONT 1633 -1634 Master of the "Reformation" and "Elizabeth Bonaventura"
2 CONT 1635 Master of the "James"
2 CONT 1636 - 1637 Charlestown, MA Emigrated
2 CONT 13 May, 1639 became a Freeman, Massachusetts Colony, MA
2 CONT 7 OCT 1639 Admitted to First Church, Charlestown with his wife
2 CONT 13 MAY 1640 Made freeman at general court in Boston
2 CONT 1643 Master of the "Tryall", [1st American ship built] on her second voyage
2 CONT 30 MAY 1652 Appointed by Parliament Captain of the frigate "President" in the Royal Navy on 30 May 1652
2 CONT 13 JUN 1652 Will was deposited with his brother Abraham in England.
2 CONT 1653 Appointed Rear Admiral of the "White"
2 CONT 31 JUL 1653 While onboard the "St. Andrew" (360 men and 56 guns) he was killed participating in a naval battle
2 CONT 1 DEC 1653 Will was probated Charlestown, Massachusetts Bay, British Colonial America
2 CONT ••~════~••~════~••~════~••
