2 CONT ============================
2 CONT
2 CONT Susan Hardman Moore, Abandoning America: Life-stories from early New England (Woodbridge, Suffolk, UK: The Boydell Press, 2013) p 64, 121-2
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.amazon.com/Abandoning-America-Life-stories-early-England/dp/1843838176
2 CONT
2 CONT GOOKIN, Daniel (1612-1687)
2 CONT
2 CONT Daniel Gookin, born near Canterbury in Kent, was the third son of Daniel 
2 CONT Gookin. In 1616 the family moved to Ireland, to Carragaline, near Cork.  Gookin was a nephew of Sir Vincent Gookin (ODNB) of Munster, and a  cousin of Vincent Gookin (ODNB). He went to Virginia with his father in  1621, and in 163
2 CONC 1 acted as his agent. The elder Gookin had not been successful  in the tobacco trade, and when he died Daniel inherited land claims and debts,  rather than cashable assets. Little is known of Gookin's activities in the 1630s.  He 
2 CONC married Mary Dolling in London on 11 November 1639.  By 1641 Gookin had emerged as a major landowner in Norfolk County,  Virginia. He was the second of seventy-two signatories on a letter to  Massachusetts 'earnestly entreating a 
2 CONC supply of faithful ministers', 24 May  1642. A letter also went to New Haven. As a result, William Thompson, John  Knowles* and Thomas James* were sent to Virginia from New England.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1644 Gookin fled to New England after the authorities in Virginia  enforced an order against nonconformity. Cotton Mather described him as one  of William Thompson's 'constellation of Great Converts ... By Thompson's  pains Chr
2 CONC ist and New-England, a dear Gookins gains'. He arrived in Boston  on 20 May 1644, became a church member six days later, and a freeman of  dismissed to the church at Cambridge. (Gookin had been tempted to Cambridge  by the offer o
2 CONC f a five hundred-acre farm.) In the decades that followed, he held  significant roles for the town of Cambridge and at the Massachusetts General  Court. He also took a keen interest in John Eliot's work with local American  Indian
2 CONC s, and became superintendent of 'praying Indians' in 1656.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gookin made several journeys to England. He may have gone back on a  trading visit in 1646: on 7 October he promised Thomas Bell* £5 in England,  followed by £1 18s 6d to be paid in New England. He was there on 24 July  1650, when
2 CONC  England's council of state gave permission for him to transport  ammunition to New England. In 1655, Cromwell chose Gookin - in England  once again - to further his plans in the Caribbean. By this time Cromwell's  'Western Design
2 CONC ' had floundered and failed, except for the capture of Jamaica,  but Cromwell remained earnest about transplanting New Englanders south.  He asked Gookin to advertise Jamaica to New England's settlers. Gookin took  this commission
2 CONC  to Boston in 1656 and printed a promotional flyer, but met  with little success.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gookin next travelled to England in the autumn of 1657. He went on family  business and this time stayed for several years. On 10March1658/9, the council  of state appointed Gookin a collector of customs at Dunkirk; he became  dep
2 CONC uty treasurer in September 1659.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the Restoration Gookin decided his future was not in England. He  reached Boston in July 1660 with the regicides William Goffe ( ODNB) and  Edward Whalley ( ODNB). Gookin brought letters to John Winthrop Jr* from  his son Fitz 
2 CONC John*. In Massachusetts, Gookin became licenser of the press and  an overseer of Harvard. His appointment as superintendent of the 'praying  Indians' was renewed, and he championed their cause during the AngloIndian  conflict of K
2 CONC ing Philip's War, 1675-6. In the 1680s he vigorously  resisted pressure from England to force Massachusetts into renegotiating  its charter, and backed up his politics with meticulous attention to military  duties. Gookin wrote bo
2 CONC oks on Native American culture, one of which was  published posthumously.
2 CONT
2 CONT Publication 'Historical collections of the Indians in New England', MHSC 1 (1792),  141-227. NEHGR 1: 345-52; F.W. Gookin, Daniel Gookin, 1612-1687 (Chicago, IL: privately  printed, 1912); WJ, 508n.; MHS, Henry Dunster's Notebook,
2 CONC  fols 107-14 (Dunster's  copies of the letters from Virginia); To all persons whom these may concern in the  several townes, and plantations of the United Colonies in New-England (Cambridge,  MA, 1656), ESTCW6490; Thurloe IV, 440,
2 CONC  449, 601, 635, V, 6-7, 147-8, 374, 509- 10,  VI, 362; Magnalia, I, 440; Davenport Letters, 181; 5 MHSC 8: 62-3; Pilgrims, 116. ANB,ODNB
2 CONT
2 CONT =================================================
2 CONT Daniel Gookin (1612-1686/87) emigrated to America from England in 1641, and settled in Cambridge, Mass., in 1648, where he was probably engaged in the intercolonial coasting trade. He was greatly interested in the welfare of the I
2 CONC ndians and in 1656 and again in 1661, he was appointed superintendent of the “praying Indians,” (i.e., Christian Indians). His defense of them during the King Philip’s War made him extremely unpopular in Massachusetts. In 1681, Go
2 CONC okin was appointed major general of all the militia of the colony. He wrote three books, none of which were published until after his death: “An Historical Account of the Christian Indians of New England,” Transactions and Collect
2 CONC ions of AAS, vol. II, 1836; “Historical Collections of the Indians in New England,” Massachusetts Historical Society Collections, I. ser., I, 1792; and a history of New England which has been lost. This volume is a copy of Gookin’
2 CONC s 1677 manuscript, “An Historical Account ... of the Christian Indians in New England,” made by Jared Sparks (1789-1866) in 1830. (The original manuscript essay cannot be located.) Gookin writes extensively of the movements and su
2 CONC fferings of the Christian Indians during the King Philip’s War, 1675 to 1676. He describes, in great detail, Indian tribes and individuals, the captivity of both Indians and colonists, the savage attacks, verbal and physical, agai
2 CONC nst the Christian Indians, and the efforts made by John Eliot (1604-1690) and Gookin to defend them. He also includes copies of orders of various councils in regard to the fate of the Christian Indians, who were finally exiled to 
2 CONC Deer Island. Gookin also includes a copy of a 1677 letter from John Eliot, praising this account, and copies of three 1677 certificates, signed by an army officer and two government officials, praising the loyal efforts of the Chr
2 CONC istian Indians during the war
2 CONT
2 CONT == Sources ==
2 CONT
