2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_Dudley
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Dudley (23 September 1647 – 2 April 1720) was an English colonial administrator. A native of Roxbury, Massachusetts and the son of one of its founders, Dudley had a leading role in the administration of the Dominion of New 
2 CONC England (1686–1689), overthrown in the 1689 Boston revolt, and served briefly on the council of the Province of New York. In New York, he oversaw the trial that convicted Jacob Leisler, the ringleader of Leisler's Rebellion. He sp
2 CONC ent eight years in the 1690s as lieutenant governor of the Isle of Wight, including one year as a Member of Parliament. In 1702 he was appointed governor of the provinces of Massachusetts Bay and New Hampshire, posts he held until
2 CONC  1715.
2 CONT
2 CONT His rule of Massachusetts was characterized by hostility and tension, with political enemies opposing his attempts to gain a regular salary, and regularly making complaints about his official and private actions. Most of his tenur
2 CONC e was dominated by Queen Anne's War, in which the two provinces were on the front lines with New France and suffered from a series of major and minor French and Indian raids. He orchestrated an unsuccessful attempt to capture the 
2 CONC Acadian capital of Port Royal in 1707, raised provincial militia forces for its successful capture in 1710, and directed an unsuccessful expedition against Quebec in 1711.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dudley's governorship institutionalized a pattern of hostility toward royal governance in Massachusetts, most frequently over the issue of the salaries of crown officials. The colonial legislature routinely challenged or disputed 
2 CONC the prerogatives of the governor. While this hostility affected most of the governors of Massachusetts up to the American Revolutionary War and the end of British rule, his rule of New Hampshire was comparatively uncontroversial.
2 CONT
2 CONT _______________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Dudley (1647-1720)
2 CONT
2 CONT President, Dominion of New England 1686, 1702-1715
2 CONT
2 CONT In May of 1686, the Massachusetts Bay Colony came to an end, as Joseph Dudley became President of New England under a commission of King James II. He established his authority later in New Hampshire and the King's Province (part o
2 CONC f today's Rhode Island), maintaining this position until Sir Edmund Andros arrived to become the Royal Governor of the New England Dominion. Dudley continued on as a member of Governor Andros' council.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Dominion was short-lived and both Andros and Dudley were sent back to England during its "Glorious Revolution" to answer for their actions. Dudley served the crown in New York and returned to England to become the Governor of 
2 CONC the Isle of Wight. Years later in 1702, Dudley was again commissioned to govern Massachusetts and New Hampshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Though it was sixteen years later, Massachusetts colonists remembered Dudley's support of the popularly hated Governor Andros. Governor Dudley began his administration in an air of mistrust, which he did little to overcome in the 
2 CONC following years. He set about purging old political foes and by the end of his service in 1715, he had fostered a legacy of resentment and suspicion, which permanently attached itself to the Royal Governorship.
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT Joseph Dudley studied theology and graduated from Harvard College in 1665. However, he did not pursue the ministry, instead followed a political career instead. Marston writes that "his subsequent official career was the most bril
2 CONC lant that had been witnessed in the new world." Most of his children predeceased him as his will of 27 Oct 1719 mentions only two sons and bequeaths his body to be buried with his father. 1 Birth: 23 SEP 1647 in Roxbury, Suffolk, 
2 CONC MA Death: 1720
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Thomas DUDLEY b: 12 OCT 1576 in Yardley Hastings, Northamptonshire, England Mother: Katherine DEIGHTON b: ABT JAN 1614 in Gloucester, Gloucestershire, England
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT Answers.com Dudley, Joseph, 1647– 1720, colonial governor of Massachusetts, b. Roxbury, Mass.; son of Thomas Dudley. In 1682 he was one of the agents sent to England to protest against the threatened loss of the Massachusetts char
2 CONC ter. Having found favor in England, Dudley was appointed head of the temporary government in the colony until Sir Edmund Andros arrived (1686) a few months later as governor of all of New England. Under Andros he held several prom
2 CONC inent positions, but with Andros's fall (1689) Dudley was sent to England to answer charges brought against him by the colonists. Acquitted of the charges, he was appointed chief of the council of New York (1690– 92) and acted as 
2 CONC chief justice during the trial of Jacob Leisler. Back in England again, he was elected to Parliament (1701), but soon returned as governor of Massachusetts (1702– 15). Dudley raised and directed military expeditions against Canada
2 CONC , but his administration was marked by dissension because of his earlier unpopularity in the colony and his uncompromising attitude.
2 CONT
2 CONT See biography by E. Kimball (1911).
2 CONT
2 CONT His son, Paul Dudley, 1675– 1751, b. Roxbury, Mass., rose to considerable prominence as a jurist in spite of his father's unpopularity and the hostility of the Mather faction. He was chief justice of Massachusetts (1745– 51) and w
2 CONC as well known as a naturalist.
2 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT http://freepages.history.rootsweb.com/~dav4is/people/DUDL426.htm Dudley, Joseph, governor of Massachusetts, was born at Roxbury, Mass., Sept. 23, 1647; son of Governor Thomas Dudley. He was graduated from Harvard in 1665, taking h
2 CONC is second degree in 1668. He was made freeman in 1672; was deputy, 1673-75; was engaged in the battle with the Narragansetts in 1675, and was a commissioner with Edward Hutchinson and others who made the treaty with the Indians, J
2 CONC uly 15, 1675. He was an assistant, 1676-85, and in 1682 was sent as an agent to England to obtain a renewal of the old charter, but was unsuccessful in his quest. In 1685 he was commissioned president of the part of New England in
2 CONC cluded in Massachusetts Bay, New Hampshire, Maine and King's Province. In December, 1686, he was placed at the head of the council of Sir Edmund Andros, the newly appointed governor of New England. When the superior court was esta
2 CONC blished in March, 1687, he was made chief justice and held the office for a year, when he was superseded by John Palmer and forced to accept a subordinate place on the bench. In 1689 he went a second time to England, having been a
2 CONC rrested with Andros and sent thither with him. In 1690 he returned, having been appointed chief justice of New York. In 1693 he went a third time to England and received a commission from King William as lieutenant-governor of the
2 CONC  Isle of Wight, where he continued eight years. He was a member of the House of commons for the borough of Newton up to the time of the death of King William. He received from King William his commission as governor of the provinc
2 CONC e of Massachusetts which was renewed by Queen Anne, and he arrived in Boston, June 11, 1702, and continued in the government till November, 1715. He was married in 1669 to Rebecca, daughter of Edward Tyng, Esq., an early magistrat
2 CONC e of Massachusetts, and of their thirteen children, seven lived to maturity. He died in Roxbury, Mass., April 2, 1720. [BDNA]
2 CONT
2 CONT http://capecodhistory.us/genealogy/getperson.php?personID=I11340&tree=Nauset
