3 CONT "Boston, On Saturday the 2d Currant; dyed the very Honourable JOSEPH DUDLEY, Esq; at his Seat in Roxbury, in the 73d Year of his Age, being born, September 23d 1647; and on Friday the 8th Currant he was Iterred in the Sepulchre of
3 CONC  his Father with all the Honour and Respect his Country was capable of doing him; there being Two Regiments of Foot with Two Troops of Horse in Arms; and while the Funeral was passing, the Guns at His Majesty's Castle Willima were
3 CONC  fired; and on the Occasion all the Bells of the Town of Boston were Tolled.
3 CONT
3 CONT There attended at the Funeral the Members of His Majesty's Council, in Boston, and the Neighbouring Towns; a great Number of Justices of the Peace, Ministers, Gentlemen, Merchants and others.
3 CONT
3 CONT The late Governour Dudly was the Son of the Honourable Thomas Dudley, Esq; (for many Years Governour of New England) and the Son of his Old Age, being born after his Father was 70 Years Old. --During his Childhood, he was under th
3 CONC e care of his Excellent Mother, and the Reverend Mr. Allen the Minister of Dedham, who Marryed her. In his Youth he was Educated at the Free-School in Cambridge, under the Famous Master Corlet; from thence he went to College in Ca
3 CONC mbridge, and there took his Degrees in the Presidentship of Mr. Charles Chauncey --The first of his Publick appearance for his Countrey's Service, was in the Narraganset Indian War, Anno 1675. --The Year after he was Chosen a Magi
3 CONC strate of the Massachusetts Colony --In 1682 he went for England with John Richards, Esq; in an Agency for his Country, --In 1686 the Government of the Massachusetts Colony being changed to a President and Council, he has a Commis
3 CONC sion to Command in chief; and after the Arrival of Sir Edmund Androes in the Government of New England, New York, &c. he continued President of the Council and Chief Justice. In the Winter 1689 he went a second time for England; a
3 CONC nd in 1690 returned with a Commission of Chief Justice for New York. In 1693 he went a third time for England, and in the Winter of that Year, he received a Commission from King William, appointing him Lieutenant Governour of the 
3 CONC Isle of Wight, where he continued Eight Years. While in England, he had the Honour to serve as a Member of the House of Commons for the Borough of Newton on the Isle of Wight, in the last Parliament of King William, from whom he f
3 CONC irst received his Commission for his Government; but staying in England till His Majesty's Death, he was obliged to get his Commission renewed from Queen Anne, with which he Arrived at Boston the 11th of June 1702; and was receive
3 CONC d with great Respect and Affection; and continued in the Government until November 1715, saving an Intermission of about 7 Weeks, that the Government dev[?]lv'd upon His Majesty's Council.
3 CONT
3 CONT He was a Man of rare Endowments and shining Accomplishments, a singular Honour to his County, and in many Respects the Glory of it; He was early its Darling, always its Ornament, and his Age its Crown: The Scholar, the Divine, the
3 CONC  Philosopher and the Lawyer, all me in him: --He was visibly form'd for Government; and under his Administration (by God Almighty's Blessing) We enjoyed great quietness, and were sfely sterr'd thro' a long and difficult, Indian an
3 CONC d French War. 
3 CONT
3 CONT His Country have once and again thankfully acknowledge his Abilities and Fidelity in their Addresses to the Throne. --He truly Honour'd and Lov'd the Religion, Learning and Verture of New England; and was himself a worthy Patron a
3 CONC nd Example of the all, --Nor did so bright a Soul, dwell in a less amiable Body, being a very Comely Person, of a Noble Aspect, and a graceful Mien, having a Gravity of a Judge, and the Goodness of a Father, --In a word, he was a 
3 CONC [?] Gentleman, of a most polite Address; and had uncommon Elegancies and Charms in his Conversation.
3 CONT
3 CONT 'Tis said a Funeral Sermon will be Preach'd for him at the Publick Lecture in Boston the next Thursday."
3 CONT
3 CONT Boston News-Letter, Boston MA, 4 Apr - 11 Apr 1720, issue 834, page 2.
3 CONT
3 CONT
