2 CONT Birth: 1640 1 
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: Graduated from Harvard College in 1659, and was preacher in the town of Groton until the town was attacked by Indians in 1676, during King Philip's War. He was then called to the Old South Church in Boston, and became the se
2 CONC cond most important spokesman of the Puritan Church, after Increase Mather. It was said of him (by John Dunton) "He's a Man of Profound Notions: Can say what he will, and prove what he says." He was less dictatorial than Mather, b
2 CONC ut his scholarship was broader and his mind more philosophical. A series of sermons he delivered over the years were published after his death, providing a systematic outline of orthodox Puritan theology. He was Vice-President of 
2 CONC Harvard College from 1701 to 1707, during a time when there was no President, so he was essentially in charge. (Paraphrased from "The Puritans; A Sourcebook of Their Writings" edited by Perry Miller and Thomas H. Johnson.) 1 
2 CONT
