1 OCCU Served as member of the Senate; Judge of Probate, Suffolk County; President of Union Bank; Fellow of the Corp. of Harvard College; Trustee, Phillips Academy, Andover
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT Oliver Wendell performed Patriotic Service in Massachusetts in the American Revolution.
2 CONT
2 CONT Oliver Wendell is a DAR Patriot Ancestor, A214213.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography===
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Oliver was born March 5, 1733 in Boston, Massachusetts. Oliver Wendell was the son of Jacob Wendell and Sara Oliver.[1] [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Mary Jackson (1740–1804) [3] [4]
2 CONT
2 CONT He passed away January 15, 1818, in Cambridge, Massachusetts and was buried in Kings Chapel Burying Ground, Boston, Massachusetts[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT At various times in his life, he served as member of the Senate; Judge of Probate, Suffolk County; President of Union Bank; Fellow of the Corp. of Harvard College; Trustee, Phillips Academy, Andover. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Selectman Boston, in 1770 Oliver Wendell's black slave Andrew described the soldier's trial after the Boston Massacre in which Crispus Attucks Attucks-1 was killed. [5]
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Wendell-224
2 CONT
2 CONT Oliver Wendell (1733 - 1818)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Oliver Wendell===
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 5 Mar 1733 in Boston, Suffolk, Massachusetts
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Jacobus Wendell and Sarah (Oliver) Wendell
2 CONT
2 CONT Brother of Jacob Wendell, Mary Wendell, Elizabeth (Wendell) Wibird, Sarah (Wendell) Hunt, Mercy (Wendell) Oliver, Katherine (Wendell) Cooper, John Micro Wendell, Ann Wendell, Abraham Wendell, Susannah Wendell and Margaret Wendell
2 CONT
2 CONT Husband of Mary (Jackson) Wendell — married 9 Sep 1762 in Boston, Suffolk, Massachusetts
2 CONT
2 CONT Father of Sarah Oliver (Wendell) Holmes
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 15 Jan 1818 at age 84 in Cambridge, Middlesex, Massachusetts, United States
2 CONT
2 CONT Profile last modified 26 Apr 2022 | Created 16 Jun 2015
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT ↑ 1.0 1.1 1.2 Find-a-Grave "http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=70401336"
2 CONT ↑ Journals of the Rev. Thomas Smith, and the Rev. Samuel Deane: By Thomas Smith, Samuel Deane, William Willis Pastors of the First Church of Portland Page 29
2 CONT ↑ Quincy Political Family Wikitree
2 CONT ↑ Generations and Change: Genealogical Perspectives in Social History By Robert M. Taylor, Ralph J. Crandall Page 175
2 CONT ↑ pages 59-60 "Trumpet of a Prophecy, Revolutionary America 1763-1783 by John Anthony Scott, Knoph Inc, 1969 New York. Call E208 S3
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT The Town of Wendell MA is named for a Judge Oliver Wendell (1733-1818) of Boston  and was incorporated in May 1781.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wendell has an area of 32.22 square miles, which is 32 square miles of land and .22 square miles of water. The 2000 population was 874 adults.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Millers River runs along the entire northern boundary, separating Wendell from Erving.  Other abutting towns are Montague, New Salem, Orange, Shutesbury, and Warwick.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wendell is at a longitude of 72° 23" 50' W and a latitude of 42° 32" 54' N.  Our elevation is 1,164 feet above sea level.
2 CONT
2 CONT Our average temperature in January is 18.8° Fahrenheit and in July is 69.3° Fahrenheit.
