2 DATE 6 NOV 1864
2 ADDR
3 STAE Maine
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Hammond was the youngest child of Elijah Estabrooks and Sarah Hammond. 
2 CONT
2 CONT About 1842, Hammond  Estabrook moved his family from Hartland, Carleton Co., New Brunwick, Canada to the Estabrook Settllement  near Amity, Aroostook Co., Maine, where they first built a log cabin. He later built a house which is 
2 CONC still stanidng. He was a farmer. At the time of his death, Hammond  and his wife, Catherine, were living with his son Jedidiah and his  family.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Hammond Estabrook and Rebeckah Glazier:
2 CONT * (Es-100) HAMMOND D. b. ca.1806-08 Lincoln, N.B. m. Jemima Belyea
2 CONT * (Es-102) WINSLOW b. ca.1808 Lincoln m. --- Acorn
2 CONT * (Es-104) '''WENTWORTH W. b. ca.1810 Lincoln m. Mary A. Day'''
2 CONT * (Es-105) CAVALIER b. ca.1811 Lincoln m. Sarah Ann Wood
2 CONT * (Es-101) GLAZIER b. ca. Mar. 20, 1813 Lincoln m. #1 Elzie Estabrook aka Ann? (father unknown) m. #2 Catherine Mahoney
2 CONT * (Es-106) JUDITH b. ca. 1814 Lincoln m. William Martin
2 CONT * (Es-108) EMMA b. Jan. 1815 Lincoln m. Cornelius Brown
2 CONT * (Es-107) FRANCIS b. Oct. 3, 1816 Lincoln m. Caleb Hall
2 CONT * 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Hammond Estabrook and Catherine Glazier:
2 CONT *(Es-103) GEORGE FREDERICK b.ca.1820/23 Lincoln m. Francis Ann Estabrook
2 CONT * (Es-109) JAMES b. ca. 1823 Lincoln
2 CONT * (Es-110) CHARLES b. Lincoln
2 CONT * (Es-111) JEDIDIAH b. Aug. 17, 1825 Lincoln m. SUSAN JANE SWIMM
2 CONT * (Es-113) LOUISE/LOIZA b. Dec. 18, 1826 Lincoln m. James Thomas Dakin
2 CONT * (Es-114) CAROLYN b. Apr. 2, 1829 Lincoln m. Freeman Rue Dakin
2 CONT * (Es-112) JOHN (AMES ?) b. Lincoln probably died young
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT --ESTABROOK FAMILY OF AROOSTOOK COUNTY, MAINE, Descendants of Hammond Estabrook (1783-1864 by Barbara Ann Estabrook 2006
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes 
2 CONT Hammond "Married Rebeckah Glasier, aunt of Stephen, John and DuncanGlasier. They lived for a time in Lincoln. In 1809 he applied for land atLittle River which apparently he got and farmed. Rebeckah was probablysomewhat older than 
2 CONC her husband. The Glasiers owned a large section of land in Lincoln and evidently gaveRebeckah a small property there. Her children were born and grew up inLincoln. Hammond was probably employed in the Glasier lumberingoperations, 
2 CONC and her sons also as they grew up. Possibly their tenureended at her death. Rebeckah died about 1816. Hammond and four of hissons decided to exchange lumbering for farming and migrated to Amity,south of Houlton in Maine. Apparentl
2 CONC y Hammond married a second time, a widow, Catherine [Glasier]Estabrooks. She had two daughters by her first husband Elizabeth andElzie? Hammond moved from New Brunswick to Amity, Maine in 1840.  It is reportedthat all the children
2 CONC  went except Winslow who moved to Grand Falls, NewBrunswick, Cavalier who lived at Sheffield, Sunbury Co, New Brunswick andLilliam who remained in New Brunswick. Hammond left his son Jedediah his father's farm in Amity, Maine. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
