0 @N6000000003490628855@ NOTE He arrived at Plymouth Colony in 1621 aboard the Fortune and took up residence in Plymouth.  Later he moved to Duxbury (by 1637) and Bridgewater (by 1656).  Basset was a blacksmith.  When he died, his estate included blacksmith's 
1 CONC tools including a pair of bellows, an anvil, a vice, tongs and hammers and coal shovels and "all the rest of the smithy shop" items.  His estate also included more than twenty books, mostly theological.  The value of his estate wa
1 CONC s in excess of one hundred pounds, quite a sum for that time.   Court records of Plymouth Colony indicate that Basset bought and sold real estate extensively, perhaps that is how he supplemented his smithy shop income to become on
1 CONC e of the relatively well to do middle class residents of the colony.   
