0 @N6000000003525444281@ NOTE
1 CONT Birth reported as 1593 at E Farleigh, Kent in the LDS IGI. Marriage date reported as 29 Mar 1624 at Kent Eng. by LDS IGI files. Came to Cambridge, MA before Aug.4, 1634 when town records record a land grant of 25 acres to "Dollard
1 CONC  Davis". Probably came with his brother-in-law Simon Willard and family. His family came on the ship "Elizabeth", master Wm Stagg, from London to Boston. Then granted a house lot of 1/2 rood on Winthrop St. Cambridge MA, located b
1 CONC etween what is now Harvard College and Charles River. Moved from Cambridge along with many others, possibly because of strict religious rules and persecution. Only church members could be Freeman. Settled in Duxbury in Plymouth Co
1 CONC lony 1638 via Scituate & Concord where Simon Willard went. Granted lands on NW side of Northill, Duxbury. 31 Aug. granted land on Namasacussett River. Duxbury church member until 1648 but lived in Barnstable beginning 1640. Became
1 CONC  Freeman 2 Jun 1646. Barnstable surveyor of highways 1652, constable 1654. Sold 10-acres plus and moved to  Concord in MA colony 1655 where 3 younger children married and settled in  Concord. Bought 150 acres recorded 18 Feb 1666 
1 CONC East Cambridge Register of Deeds. The farm was next owned by Simon and then James Davis. One of first appointed selectman of the new, nearby settlement in Groton, but he apparently did not live there long. Remained in Concord 10 p
1 CONC lus years, returning to Barnstable in 1666. A map appears on the inside cover of the book "Concord Climate for Freedom" by Ruth Wheeler showing the location of the Dolar Davis farm. Will written 13Sep 1672 and proved at Plymouth 2
1 CONC  Jul 1673 and included in Probate Index  of Middlesex County, MA.  He left 5 children and 34 grandchildren.
