0 @N6000000006295798686@ NOTE 1.  Note from Grant Misbach:
1 CONT Source: http://www.misbach.org/genealogy/g333.html#I333
1 CONT "James Barton of 81 Cocasset St., Fozboro, MA lists Reuben as being born in Coventry, Tolland, CT instead of Bolton. Do not know which is correct. Mr. Barton has also given me 12 additional children which I did not have. He does n
1 CONC ot list his sources except for the book "Genealogy of the Descendants of John Hester of Fleming Co, KY by H.G.L. Searl."
1 CONT ---------------
1 CONT
1 CONT 2.  Newspaper Clipping from Dayton Watchman newspaper
1 CONT Location http://ftp.rootsweb.com/pub/usgenweb/oh/oh-footsteps/1999/may/V99-343.txt
1 CONT Accessed 2/17/2003
1 CONT "1823, Aug. 5 - DIED, at Fairfield, Greene County, on Monday [Monday is Aug 4th], Mr Reuben Searl, age 75 years. He took an active part in the Revolutionary  War, and was a member of the Masonic Lodge."
1 CONT -------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT 3.  Addison, Steuben County, New York
1 CONT HISTORICAL GAZETTEER, Steuben County, New York, FIRST PART, Compiled by: Millard F. Roberts, John Single Paper Company, Ltd., Syracuse, N.Y., 1891
1 CONT http://www.rootsweb.com/~nysteube/ad/ad.html
1 CONT
1 CONT "Addison, originally a part of the old town of Painted Post, was organized as Middletown at the time of the organization of Steuben county in March, 1796. The name was changed to Addison, in honor of Joseph Addison, the English au
1 CONC thor, on April 6, 1808. The early settlers called it also "Tuscarora". It is an interior town, situated southeast of the centre of the county, and lies upon both sides of the Canisteo river. It is bounded north by Thurston and a p
1 CONC art of Campbell, east by Erwin, south by Tuscarora and west by Rathbone.
1 CONT The surface is hilly upland, broken by the valley of the Canisteo and its branches. The principal valley is about one mile wide and is bordered by steep hillsides rising from three to four hundred feet. The chief streams are the C
1 CONC anisteo river, Tuscarora and Goodhue creeks. Goodhue lake, in the northwest corner of the town, covers an area of about fifty acres. The alluvial soil of the valleys is rich and productive; on the hills it is clay mixed with the '
1 CONC debris' of broken shale, and compares favorably with the average farming lands of the county. The population of the town in 1890 was 2,884."
1 CONT
1 CONT Addison - First Settlement - pages 92,93,94,95,96,97 (added - 5/31/1999)
1 CONT "The first settlement was made by SAMUEL RICE in 1791. Mr. Rice was born about fifteen miles from the city of New Haven, Conn., and when but fifteen years of age entered the war of the revolution and served about six months. From 
1 CONC Connecticut he moved to the state of New Hampshire, thence to Chenango county in this state. His settlement in Addison was made a short distance up Tuscarora creek, near the Wombough mills, where he built the first house erected b
1 CONC y a white man in this town. He married LUCRETIA MARTIN. Their son, STEPHEN RICE, born April 5, 1791, was the first white child born in the town. Mr. Rice moved with his family to Troupsburgh in 1805, of which town also they were t
1 CONC he pioneers.
1 CONT
1 CONT "Of those who soon followed Mr. Rice in the settlement of Addison were REUBEN AND LEMUEL SEARLES, OLIVER MILLER, GEORGE GOODHUE, who had previously settled near Painted Post; JOHN MARTIN, JONATHAN TRACY, ABLE White..."
1 CONT ---------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT 4.  Church Membership, Vernon, Connecticut
1 CONT
1 CONT Records transcribed in the New England Historical and Genealogical Record, Volume 58 (pp. 193-198) and also Volume 6 (pp. 77, 202), offer evidence that Reuben Searl and his wife Mercy Allis were active in the life of the church an
1 CONC d resided in the Vernon (formerly North Bolton), Connecticut community at least between 1764 when they were married and 1774, when their son Timothy's baptism was recorded.  In 1764, his brother Gideon Searl and wife Cynthia "owne
1 CONC d the covenant" (I assume that was the equivalent of being accepted into membership) of the church.  Reuben and Mary followed on October 20, 1765.
1 CONT ----------------------
