0 @N6000000006348235597@ NOTE Captain Joseph Morse lived in Sherborn, where he built the first mill in company with Captain Ware; was representative. The first public worship was held at his house.
1 CONT
1 CONT During Philip's war, Capt. Morse was sorely tried, and, according to tradition, his combativeness, largely developed by nature, was largely called into action. On that memorable morning of Feb. 21, 1675-6, when Medfield was assaul
1 CONC ted by Philip at the head of "500 warriors," laid in ashes, and 18 persons massacred, he was at his remote and feeble settlement, with a wife near confinement, and her sister-in-law still nearer, whose husband, being on the other 
1 CONC side of the river, fell the first victim to the fierce tomahawk. The warriors were now da??cing in fiendish exultation on the west side of the river: the only bridge by which they could flee to a stronger garrison in Medfield, or 
1 CONC draw relief from thence, was on fire; and the two or three garrisons of Bogestow consisted of only about ten or twelve men each; so that immediate destruction seemed before them. The effect of all was, the premature confinement an
1 CONC d death of the sister-in-law; and Mrs. Morse subsequently lost her child. The Indians deferred their attack until the 6th of May, when "they met with a notorious repulse at a stone house on the margin of Bogestow Pond. On the 2d o
1 CONC f July there was near this a new conflict in the woods, and more execution done upon the enemy." "So vigorous was the attack upon them that they never dared to show their faces there afterwards." Capt. M. is understood to have bee
1 CONC n personally engaged in these conflicts, and by his courage and conduct to have won the confidence of the public in him as a soldier.
1 CONT
1 CONT Source:  Bibliographic Information: Morse, Abner. Memorial of the Morses. William Veazie. Boston. 1850.
1 CONT
1 CONT Genealogical and Family History of the State of Connecticut: A Record of the Achievements of Her People in the Making of a Commonwealth and the Founding of a Nation. Volume IV 
1 CONT  Phillips 
1 CONT   (The Morse Line). 
1 CONT (III) Captain Joseph (2) Morse , son of Joseph (1) Morse , was born September 26, 1649 , died in Sherborn , February 19, 1717 . He lived in Sherborn , and built there, in company with Captain Ware , the first mill. The first publi
1 CONC c worship there was held at his house. He represented the town in the general court. He married (first) October 17, 1671 , Mehitable , daughter of Nicholas and Mary (Wilkes) Wood , born July 22, 1655 , died November 12, 1681 . He 
1 CONC married (second), April 11, 1683 , Hannah , daughter of Robert and Joanna Babcock , born in Milton, Massachusetts , February 8, 1664 , died in Sherborn , November 9, 1711 . He married (third), May 17, 1713 , Mrs. Hannah Baxter Dye
1 CONC r , widow of Captain Joseph Dyer , of Braintree, Massachusetts , born in 1661 , died September 4, 1727 . Children of first wife: Mehitable , born April 25, 1673 , died young; Joseph , April 3, 1676 , died July 12, 1676 ; Elisha , 
1 CONC December 11, 1677 ; Joseph , March 25, 1679 ; Mehitable November 2, 1681 ; children of second wife: James , July 1, 1686 ; Hannah , April 5, 1689 ; Sarah , April 12, 1692 ; Captain David , December 31, 1694 ; Isaac , September 14,
1 CONC  1697 , mentioned below; Keziah , June 30, 1700 ; Asa , August 24, 1703 . 
0 @N6000000006348235922@ NOTE David was the first settler in Natick.  He was town clerk, treasurer, and captain of the militia.
0 @N6000000006348235578@ NOTE (IV) Isaac , son of Captain Joseph (2) Morse , was born September 14, 1697 . He lived in Holliston, Shrewsbury and Worcester, Massachusetts . His estate was administered in 1749 . He married Elizabeth , daughter of Thomas and Rach
1 CONC el Drury , born Framingham, Massachusetts , June 22, 1701 . Children: Isaac , born September 2, 1722 ; Elizabeth (twin), born in Holliston , in 1725 ; Keziah (twin), 1725 ; Joseph , August 21, 1729 ; Uriah, January 31, 1730 , ment
1 CONC ioned below; Caleb, March 19, 1732 , died in Worcester , November 17, 1743 ; John , February 25, 1736 ; Hannah , 1738 ; Rachel , married, in Shrewsbury , April 26, 1764 , James Saddler ; Sarah , born 1741 .
1 CONT
1 CONT Genealogical and Family History of the State of Connecticut, Vol. I-IV 
0 @N6000000006345214754@ NOTE He was captured about 1780 by the British and Indians
1 CONT     and imprisoned at Montreal, Canada, until Oct., 1781, when he
1 CONT     with others was removed to Prison Island, 45 miles above
1 CONT     Montreal. From this place he, with about 20 others, escaped
1 CONT     on the night of Sept. 10, 1782, and with William Clark of Vir-
1 CONT     ginia, Simeon Belknap and Zadock Steele, both of Randolph,
1 CONT     Vt., reached Ft. Pittsford, Vt., in Oct., 1782, after 22 days of
1 CONT     suffering in the wilderness. This episode is fully narrated in
1 CONT     Steele's Indian Narratives, pub. Claremont, N. H., 1854.
1 CONT       About 1810 he sailed for Jamaica, W. I., as supercargo
1 CONT     and his family heard no more of him. They dwelt at Milton
1 CONT     till 1796-8 and then settled on Lot No. 17 in Pompey, where
1 CONT     Polly died.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Source:  SPRAGUE FAMILIES
1 CONT IN
1 CONT AMERICA
1 CONT Compiled and Published by
1 CONT WARREN VINCENT, SPRAGUE, M, D.
