0 @N6000000000400973770@ NOTE [sweet.ftw]
1 CONT
1 CONT [Brøderbund WFT Vol. 1, Ed. 1, Tree #5059, Date of Import: Dec 19, 2001]
1 CONT
1 CONT      The son of Francis and Anne Sprague, John Sprague was born about 1637, probably in Duxbury, Massachusetts.  In 1655 he married Ruth Basset whose father, William Basset (born about 1590 in England) had arrived in America in 16
1 CONC 21 aboard the ship FORTUNE (Thomas Barton, Master).
1 CONT      John Sprague and his wife Ruth Basset lived in Marshfield, Massachusetts for a number of years before settling at Duxbury around 1668.  Shortly after their arrival in Duxbury, he became co-proprietor of his father's tavern in
1 CONC  Duxbury and remained such until his death.
1 CONT      John Sprague apparently inherited his father’s ardent temperament.  Described as a "...spark off the old flint", he is known to have spent several hours in the stocks on at least one occasion for "...highly misdemeaning himse
1 CONC lf in the house of James Cole of Plymouth near unto or on the evening before the Sabbath Day, in drinking, gaming and uncivil reveling, to the dishonor of God and the offense of the government, by his gaming and the bringing of hi
1 CONC s mare uncivily into the parlor of James Cole, aforesaid."
1 CONT      It is believed that he was the John Sprague who was a counselor to Sir Edmund Andros, rather than the John Sprague who was the son of William Sprague.
1 CONT      John Sprague was killed in the massacre of Captain Michael Pierce’s Company of English Militia during the King Philip’s War when, on 26 March 1676, that company of 65 men (supplemented by about 20 friendly Indians) engaged a 
1 CONC superior force of hostiles near the Pawtucket River in Rhode Island, about 5 miles north of Providence.
1 CONT      According to Douglas Edward Leach in his history of that war entitled FLINTLOCK AND TOMAHAWK - NEW ENGLAND IN THE KING PHILIP’S WAR, Captain Pierce, having determined that there was a band of hostile Indians located near the 
1 CONC Pawtucket River, had prepared his men for battle and had sent a messenger into the nearby town of Providence requesting reinforcements before attacking.
1 CONT      For some reason this messenger, arriving at the time of public worship, chose to wait until after the service had concluded before delivering Captain Pierce’s request.  When the situation was made known, Captain Andrew Edmund
1 CONC s of the Providence Militia immediately set out with a group of armed men in order to join forces with Pierce’s company.
1 CONT      Meanwhile the Plymouth Militia group had unwittingly engaged and become surrounded by an extremely large force of hostile Narrangansett Indians and were overwhelmed.  By the time Edmunds and his men arrived, it was too late.
1 CONT      The fact that some 42 of the 55 colonists killed that day were buried at the site of the battle, including that of John Sprague of Duxbury, indicates that there were some survivors, or it may indicate that there were bodies w
1 CONC hich may not have been recovered.
1 CONT      After the death of her husband, Ruth Basset later married a man named _____(?) Thomas, date unknown.
0 @N6000000006345214894@ NOTE Counsellor of Sir Edmund Andros, mentioned in Hutch. i, 354.     5 1
1 CONT     He was slain in Pierce's fierce fight at Pawtucket in Philip's
1 CONT     war, Mch. 26, 1676. His estate was appraised in 1676 and was
1 CONT     sworn to by the widow Ruth Sprague.
1 CONT
1 CONT Source:  SPRAGUE FAMILIES
1 CONT IN
1 CONT AMERICA
1 CONT Compiled and Published by
1 CONT WARREN VINCENT, SPRAGUE, M, D.
1 CONT
1 CONT THE TUTTLE COMPANY, PRINTERS
1 CONT RUTLAND, VERMONT 1913
1 CONT
1 CONT John Sprague, like his father, proved to be a rebel.  He spent several hours in the stocks on at least one occasion for "highly misdemeaning himself in the house of James Cole of Plymouth near unto or on the evening before the Sab
1 CONC bath Day, in drinking, gaming and uncivil reveling, to the dishonor of God and the offense of the government, by his gaming and the bringing of his mare uncivily into the parlor of James Cole, aforesaid."
1 CONT
1 CONT June 6, 1655 (GC Presentments by Grand Enquest, PCR 3:82):
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Item, wee present John Sprague and Ruth Bassett, of Duxburrow, for
1 CONT fornication before they were married. [Cleared by paying the fine.]
1 CONT
1 CONT Capt MichaelPeirse & Co. of Plymouth Colony
1 CONT
1 CONT --Capt Peirse[sic]was of Hingham,Mass 1646-1663 and later of Scituate,Mass.
1 CONT
1 CONT   The following information was " chance list found here and there"....the
1 CONT names also show up in the Treasurer's list as "Narraganset Grantees". This
1 CONT company was nearly wiped out [doesnt list survivors].
1 CONT ------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT                    March 1675/6
1 CONT
1 CONT                 Pawtucket Falls fight
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT -------------------------------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT from Duxbury.....slain
1 CONT     John Sprague             Benjamin Soal
1 CONT     Thomas Hunt              Joshua Fobes -------------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT John Sprague [Constable 1663/1664] Biographical
1 CONT
1 CONT John Sprague is the most colorful of all the constables of Duxbury. Aside
1 CONT from fornication before marriage and signing fraudulent divorce papers, he
1 CONT apparently liked to get drunk and out of control at other peoples' houses. He
1 CONT was born between 1638 and 1643 since he appears in no freemen listings before
1 CONT 1658 but was constable by 1663. He died (in extreme debt) before 1677. His
1 CONT life was short, it seems, but unlike the other constables he had fun. His
1 CONT service record to the community is extremely brief, but he served on a few
1 CONT committees and two juries. Apparently nobody saw him as a good representative
1 CONT to other towns.
