0 @N6000000000284169471@ NOTE On 11 August 1722 Samuel Sprague of Hingham bought by exchange 20 acres in Plymouth from John Fearing.
1 CONT
1 CONT From Sprague database:
1 CONT
1 CONT "Sprague Families in America", by Dr. Warren Vincent Sprague, page 137.
1 CONT Samuel Sprague was from Hingham and Plymouth, MA.
1 CONT 1723. Elizabeth Sprague appointed administrator of the estate of Samuel Sprague of Plymouth.
1 CONT 1726. Guardian appointed for Ruth and Samuel Sprague, children of Samuel.
1 CONT 1734. Another guardian appointed. They were then under the age of twenty-one.
1 CONT "Richard Warren of the Mayflower" by Robert S. Wakefield, page 342. 1722, Aug. 11. Samuel Sprague of Hingham bought by exchange 20 acres in Plymouth from John Fearing. 1723, June 17. Elizabeth Sprague widow was appointed administr
1 CONC atrix of the estate of her husbnad Samuel Sprague of Plymouth. 1726, December 22. Samuel Bates was appointed guardian to Samuel Sprague and Ruth Sprague, both under 14, children of Samuel Sprague, late of Plymouth. On 15 Aug. 1734
1 CONC  Noah Sprague of Rochester was appointed guardian of Samuel and Ruth Sprague.
1 CONT
1 CONT "The Genealogy of the Sprague's in Hingham", by Hosea Sprague, page 33.
1 CONT Samuel Sprague, in 1717, had 10 pounds paid him by the town for keeping the widow Hewet.
1 CONT
1 CONT "History of Hingham", Volume II: Genealogies, by George Lincoln, page 165. Samuel Sprague resided on the paternal homestead several years with his brother, Matthew. His name, however, disappears from our tax-lists soon after 1720.
